{"parentUuid":"bd5e2630-8e9d-4287-9f2e-22810f4a2fbb","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af4ae0306e46b6e08","attachment":{"type":"skill_listing","content":"- session-start-hook (startup-hook-skill): Creating and developing startup hooks for Claude Code cloud sessions. Use only when the user asks for a SessionStart hook committed to their repository. Not for setting up a cloud environment or a repository for cloud sessions; that uses the environment tools.\n- data:analyze: Answer data questions -- from quick lookups to full analyses. Use when looking up a single metric, investigating what's driving a trend or drop, comparing segments over time, or preparing a formal data report for stakeholders.\n- data:build-dashboard: Build an interactive HTML dashboard with charts, filters, and tables. Use when creating an executive overview with KPI cards, turning query results into a shareable self-contained report, building a team monitoring snapshot, or needing multiple charts with filters in one browser-openable file.\n- data:create-viz: Create publication-quality visualizations with Python. Use when turning query results or a DataFrame into a chart, selecting the right chart type for a trend or comparison, generating a plot for a report or presentation, or needing an interactive chart with hover and zoom.\n- data:data-context-extractor: Generate or improve a company-specific data analysis skill by extracting tribal knowledge from analysts. BOOTSTRAP MODE - Triggers: \"Create a data context skill\", \"Set up data analysis for our warehouse\", \"Help me create a skill for our database\", \"Generate a data skill for [company]\" → Discovers schemas, asks key questions, generates initial skill with reference files ITERATION MODE - Triggers: \"Add context about [domain]\", \"The skill needs more info about [topic]\", \"Update the data skill with [metrics/tables/terminology]\", \"Improve the [domain] reference\" → Loads existing skill, asks targeted questions, appends/updates reference files Use when data analysts want Claude to understand their company's specific data warehouse, terminology, metrics definitions, and common query patterns.\n- data:data-visualization: Create effective data visualizations with Python (matplotlib, seaborn, plotly). Use when building charts, choosing the right chart type for a dataset, creating publication-quality figures, or applying design principles like accessibility and color theory.\n- data:explore-data: Profile and explore a dataset to understand its shape, quality, and patterns. Use when encountering a new table or file, checking null rates and column distributions, spotting data quality issues like duplicates or suspicious values, or deciding which dimensions and metrics to analyze.\n- data:sql-queries: Write correct, performant SQL across all major data warehouse dialects (Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks, PostgreSQL, etc.). Use when writing queries, optimizing slow SQL, translating between dialects, or building complex analytical queries with CTEs, window functions, or aggregations.\n- data:statistical-analysis: Apply statistical methods including descriptive stats, trend analysis, outlier detection, and hypothesis testing. Use when analyzing distributions, testing for significance, detecting anomalies, computing correlations, or interpreting statistical results.\n- data:validate-data: QA an analysis before sharing -- methodology, accuracy, and bias checks. Use when reviewing an analysis before a stakeholder presentation, spot-checking calculations and aggregation logic, verifying a SQL query's results look right, or assessing whether conclusions are actually supported by the data.\n- data:write-query: Write optimized SQL for your dialect with best practices. Use when translating a natural-language data need into SQL, building a multi-CTE query with joins and aggregations, optimizing a query against a large partitioned table, or getting dialect-specific syntax for Snowflake, BigQuery, Postgres, etc.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, handing the viewer a file to save, or using the viewer's camera, microphone, location, screen or device motion. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's recipe covers them) at the effort level given first, before any target (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings to many, some uncertain); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply them to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default. - Use the max level only when the user, or instructions such as CLAUDE.md or a skill, explicitly ask for it, never on your own judgment: it is the costliest level and starts many reviewer subagents.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- plugin-authoring: Make a mod: a change to Claude's own interface or behaviour, such as a panel or pane that stays beside the chat and updates, a band, status line, toast, slash command, or hook on tool calls. Use to make, change or debug a mod or its hooks module.\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\n- anthropic-skills:built-in-browser: Read this skill before the first step that uses the built-in browser, the browser pane inside the Claude desktop app (also called the in-app browser, the browser pane, Claude's browser, or \"your own browser\"), whose tools are named mcp__Claude_Browser__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser tool instead. It covers the pane's persistent sign-ins, tabs and preview_start, reading pages as text, site approvals, what the pane cannot open, and what to do when it cannot be reached. It is not for Claude in Chrome (mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\n- anthropic-skills:chrome-browser: Read this skill before the first step that uses Claude in Chrome, the browser extension whose tools are named mcp__claude-in-chrome__* (also called Chrome, the browser extension, or the external browser) and which acts in the person's real Chrome with their own sign-ins; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__claude-in-chrome tool instead. It covers loading the tools in one ToolSearch call, checking the person's open tabs and working in a new tab, site permissions, GIF recordings, console logs, dialogs to avoid, and when to stop and ask. It is not for the built-in browser (mcp__Claude_Browser__* or mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\n- anthropic-skills:computer-use: Read this skill before the first step of any request to do something in an app on the person's own computer (Notes, Finder, System Settings, any desktop app), to look at their screen, or for \"computer use\". Computer use (desktop control) lets Claude take screenshots of the person's desktop and control it with clicks, typing and scrolling through the Claude desktop app; its tools are named mcp__computer-use__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__computer_* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before computer use is turned on for a conversation there may be no such tools, only an enable__mcp__remote-devices__computer tool, which turns it on. It covers turning it on, picking the right tool, the access flow, and the safety rules for tiered apps, links and financial actions. It is not for websites, which go through Claude in Chrome or the built-in browser and their own skills.\n- anthropic-skills:deep-research: Use this skill when the user's prompt requires (1) researching a topic across multiple sources, comparing options or alternatives, analyzing trends or history, understanding markets or industries, or reviewing literature or studies and (2) synthesizing that research into a comprehensive, narrative report. If you're planning to search the web or internal knowledge bases, consider using this skill. This skill coordinates research subagents, so use it only when you have a tool for spawning subagents (the Agent or Task tool); otherwise, research the question directly.\n- anthropic-skills:docs: docs (editable docs people share and comment on; the default for any document, named as a doc or not: a document, report, proposal, resume, cover letter, letter, contract, policy, form, template, worksheet, essay, handbook, guide, how-to, cheat sheet, SOP or other writing to keep, share, collaborate on, send, submit, print or sign; a doc exports to Word, PDF, Markdown or Google Docs, so needing a file to send, attach, upload, submit or print is no reason to pick Word, and a file nobody asked for is a doc, not Word; a plan, comparison, summary or notes asked in chat stays in chat; a pasted claude.ai artifact link may be a doc: check with docs tools first; Word or another file format named, tracked changes wanted, or a .docx to change or use as a template → that format's skill): making one → if no docs-connector instructions are in context, call its `guide` (topic.instructions) first; then create the doc (headings only, no body) before any search, file read or plan, even with files attached.\n- anthropic-skills:docx: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation.\n- anthropic-skills:google-workspace: Read this before the first Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides connector call whenever the task creates or changes a Google file. Use this skill whenever the user wants to create or change a Google Doc, Sheet or Slides file in their Google Drive. Triggers include: a request that names Google Docs, Sheets, Slides or Drive and asks to make, edit, format, copy or rename a file; a docs.google.com link with a request to change that file, even a one-line fix or suggested edits; and any follow-up change to a Google file from earlier in the chat, even \"change it\" or \"add a tab\". Includes helper scripts for document positions, cell ranges and slide layout. However, if the user asks for a doc, deck or spreadsheet without naming Google, or gives a Google file only as source material for something new, use Claude's own output type instead. Do NOT use for read-only questions about a Google file, or for Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF files.\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory: Import a memory export from another AI assistant into Claude's memory — conversationally, additively, and with the content treated as data.\n- anthropic-skills:morning: Render the user's morning brief as a styled HTML artifact, or set it up as a recurring weekday task. Use only when the user explicitly asks to run, see, or set up their morning brief, or if they invoke /morning by name. A question about their day, schedule, or calendar is not by itself a request for the brief; answer it directly instead.\n- anthropic-skills:pdf: Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:pptx: Use this skill any time a .pptx or .potx file is involved in any way — as input, output, or both. This includes: creating slide decks, pitch decks, or presentations as PowerPoint (.pptx) files; reading, parsing, or extracting text from any .pptx or .potx file (even if the extracted content will be used elsewhere, like in an email, summary, or creating a different type of slide deck); editing, modifying, or updating existing presentations; combining or splitting slide files; working with templates (.potx), layouts, speaker notes, or comments. Trigger whenever the user asks for a PowerPoint or .pptx file, or references a .pptx or .potx filename, regardless of what they plan to do with the content afterward. However, when the user asks for a deck, slides, a slide deck, or a presentation without naming a file format, default to using a dedicated slide-deck artifact type or a separate slides skill if this session offers one; otherwise, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:setup-writing-style: Learns how the user writes from their own sent messages and docs, and builds a voice profile so future drafts sound like them instead of generic AI. The profile is saved as the my-writing-style skill. Use when the user asks to set up, learn, or capture their writing voice, or complains that drafts sound generic or unlike them and no my-writing-style profile exists. Only for drafting text the user will send as themselves, not for Claude's own replies.\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator: Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy.\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx: Use this skill any time a spreadsheet file is the primary input or output. This means any task where the user wants to: open, read, edit, or fix an existing .xlsx, .xlsm, .xltx, .csv, or .tsv file (e.g., adding columns, computing formulas, formatting, charting, cleaning messy data); create a new spreadsheet from scratch or from other data sources; or convert between tabular file formats. Trigger especially when the user references a spreadsheet file by name or path — even casually (like \"the xlsx in my downloads\") — and wants something done to it or produced from it. Also trigger for cleaning or restructuring messy tabular data files (malformed rows, misplaced headers, junk data) into proper spreadsheets. The deliverable must be a spreadsheet file. Do NOT trigger when the primary deliverable is a Word document, HTML report, standalone Python script, database pipeline, or Google Sheets API integration, even if tabular data is involved.","skillCount":42,"isInitial":true,"names":["session-start-hook","data:analyze","data:build-dashboard","data:create-viz","data:data-context-extractor","data:data-visualization","data:explore-data","data:sql-queries","data:statistical-analysis","data:validate-data","data:write-query","dataviz","artifact-design","artifact-diagramming","artifact-capabilities","update-config","keybindings-help","code-review","simplify","fewer-permission-prompts","loop","claude-api","workflow-authoring","claude-in-chrome","run","plugin-authoring","init","security-review","anthropic-skills:built-in-browser","anthropic-skills:chrome-browser","anthropic-skills:computer-use","anthropic-skills:deep-research","anthropic-skills:docs","anthropic-skills:docx","anthropic-skills:google-workspace","anthropic-skills:import-memory","anthropic-skills:morning","anthropic-skills:pdf","anthropic-skills:pptx","anthropic-skills:setup-writing-style","anthropic-skills:skill-creator","anthropic-skills:xlsx"],"digests":["182a65d0c7821a07","66526fb79b9238a6","c846f7ab9ca0e144","c2a7010ac656f9b9","18f8304381d6aa40","70ce57642a13fee0","deeebc45ae9440a8","6e63eb89da591218","a5533276bc6099d4","fcd1cc69bea1b653","4fe794bd42e5f952","140faf7359fd9e4c","6184489843ae568d","630d2958b5800934","45b8a38b72b1d2e3","909bf5fe1d1fb81f","8bcbb8b001b829d1","8986cb20b0ed6ddf","e6e9b542dec5def2","6b9c0ebd3ca594f8","0d4f2716799e058f","163e03d4834f66b2","49e061d3cd347fd2","800c972acbf6f30c","c3733391c3cbaef1","ce022e5236030920","ff38bf755f0da166","08f19dfd18875bba","afd1a9aef4e788a9","06b86a7ddf7e5d20","3623cd6a9941bed4","45ca34721ed7ed45","d077440c2f409160","86f9648d364018ff","5423beadec23fb35","66f7e9140cf13d4a","099e58dbfd5042b8","e3b049022caec160","417c92e161a66330","4b75b662818ad479","f5c93b5982444cdb","c08fa2c3cd200503"]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"3071a1d6-dc0b-407a-ac12-cb9eb665d6f1","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:10:32.746Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- session-start-hook (startup-hook-skill): Creating and developing startup hooks for Claude Code cloud sessions. Use only when the user asks for a SessionStart hook committed to their repository. Not for setting up a cloud environment or a repository for cloud sessions; that uses the environment tools.\n- data:analyze: Answer data questions -- from quick lookups to full analyses. Use when looking up a single metric, investigating what's driving a trend or drop, comparing segments over time, or preparing a formal data report for stakeholders.\n- data:build-dashboard: Build an interactive HTML dashboard with charts, filters, and tables. Use when creating an executive overview with KPI cards, turning query results into a shareable self-contained report, building a team monitoring snapshot, or needing multiple charts with filters in one browser-openable file.\n- data:create-viz: Create publication-quality visualizations with Python. Use when turning query results or a DataFrame into a chart, selecting the right chart type for a trend or comparison, generating a plot for a report or presentation, or needing an interactive chart with hover and zoom.\n- data:data-context-extractor: Generate or improve a company-specific data analysis skill by extracting tribal knowledge from analysts. BOOTSTRAP MODE - Triggers: \"Create a data context skill\", \"Set up data analysis for our warehouse\", \"Help me create a skill for our database\", \"Generate a data skill for [company]\" → Discovers schemas, asks key questions, generates initial skill with reference files ITERATION MODE - Triggers: \"Add context about [domain]\", \"The skill needs more info about [topic]\", \"Update the data skill with [metrics/tables/terminology]\", \"Improve the [domain] reference\" → Loads existing skill, asks targeted questions, appends/updates reference files Use when data analysts want Claude to understand their company's specific data warehouse, terminology, metrics definitions, and common query patterns.\n- data:data-visualization: Create effective data visualizations with Python (matplotlib, seaborn, plotly). Use when building charts, choosing the right chart type for a dataset, creating publication-quality figures, or applying design principles like accessibility and color theory.\n- data:explore-data: Profile and explore a dataset to understand its shape, quality, and patterns. Use when encountering a new table or file, checking null rates and column distributions, spotting data quality issues like duplicates or suspicious values, or deciding which dimensions and metrics to analyze.\n- data:sql-queries: Write correct, performant SQL across all major data warehouse dialects (Snowflake, BigQuery, Databricks, PostgreSQL, etc.). Use when writing queries, optimizing slow SQL, translating between dialects, or building complex analytical queries with CTEs, window functions, or aggregations.\n- data:statistical-analysis: Apply statistical methods including descriptive stats, trend analysis, outlier detection, and hypothesis testing. Use when analyzing distributions, testing for significance, detecting anomalies, computing correlations, or interpreting statistical results.\n- data:validate-data: QA an analysis before sharing -- methodology, accuracy, and bias checks. Use when reviewing an analysis before a stakeholder presentation, spot-checking calculations and aggregation logic, verifying a SQL query's results look right, or assessing whether conclusions are actually supported by the data.\n- data:write-query: Write optimized SQL for your dialect with best practices. Use when translating a natural-language data need into SQL, building a multi-CTE query with joins and aggregations, optimizing a query against a large partitioned table, or getting dialect-specific syntax for Snowflake, BigQuery, Postgres, etc.\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. When the destination is a first-party document connector (host-designated, never self-described) that renders live charts, hand it the rows (inline, or as an uploaded data file the chart cites) rather than a rendered PNG/SVG — a picture of a chart loses hover, data inspection and per-value comments. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- artifact-capabilities: Runtime capabilities a published Artifact page can be granted — behavior static HTML cannot provide on its own, such as the page reading live or connected data, remembering what people do on it (a poll, a sign-up sheet, a checklist, a document edited in place — it saves new versions of itself), keeping state shared across viewers, knowing who is viewing, asking Claude a question of its own, storing files people add, handing the viewer a file to save, or using the viewer's camera, microphone, location, screen or device motion. Serves this user's live capability roster and the typed call definitions. Load it whenever any such runtime behavior would make an artifact more useful, before writing the page.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs (plus reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups where the model's recipe covers them) at the effort level given first, before any target (low|medium|high|xhigh|max: from few high-confidence findings to many, some uncertain); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply them to the working tree after the review. Pass --max-findings <n> to report up to n findings, or --max-findings all for every finding. The choice stays until you pass --max-findings default. - Use the max level only when the user, or instructions such as CLAUDE.md or a skill, explicitly ask for it, never on your own judgment: it is the costliest level and starts many reviewer subagents.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- claude-in-chrome: Automates your Chrome browser to interact with web pages - clicking elements, filling forms, capturing screenshots, reading console logs, and navigating sites. Opens pages in new tabs within your existing Chrome session. Requires site-level permissions before executing (configured in the extension). - When the user wants to interact with web pages, automate browser tasks, capture screenshots, read console logs, or perform any browser-based actions. Always invoke BEFORE attempting to use any mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- plugin-authoring: Make a mod: a change to Claude's own interface or behaviour, such as a panel or pane that stays beside the chat and updates, a band, status line, toast, slash command, or hook on tool calls. Use to make, change or debug a mod or its hooks module.\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\n- anthropic-skills:built-in-browser: Read this skill before the first step that uses the built-in browser, the browser pane inside the Claude desktop app (also called the in-app browser, the browser pane, Claude's browser, or \"your own browser\"), whose tools are named mcp__Claude_Browser__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser tool instead. It covers the pane's persistent sign-ins, tabs and preview_start, reading pages as text, site approvals, what the pane cannot open, and what to do when it cannot be reached. It is not for Claude in Chrome (mcp__claude-in-chrome__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\n- anthropic-skills:chrome-browser: Read this skill before the first step that uses Claude in Chrome, the browser extension whose tools are named mcp__claude-in-chrome__* (also called Chrome, the browser extension, or the external browser) and which acts in the person's real Chrome with their own sign-ins; before those tools are turned on there may be a single enable__mcp__claude-in-chrome tool instead. It covers loading the tools in one ToolSearch call, checking the person's open tabs and working in a new tab, site permissions, GIF recordings, console logs, dialogs to avoid, and when to stop and ask. It is not for the built-in browser (mcp__Claude_Browser__* or mcp__remote-devices__Claude_Browser__* tools), which has its own skill, and it does not decide which browser to use.\n- anthropic-skills:computer-use: Read this skill before the first step of any request to do something in an app on the person's own computer (Notes, Finder, System Settings, any desktop app), to look at their screen, or for \"computer use\". Computer use (desktop control) lets Claude take screenshots of the person's desktop and control it with clicks, typing and scrolling through the Claude desktop app; its tools are named mcp__computer-use__* when the session runs in the desktop app and mcp__remote-devices__computer_* when a cloud session is linked to the person's computer; before computer use is turned on for a conversation there may be no such tools, only an enable__mcp__remote-devices__computer tool, which turns it on. It covers turning it on, picking the right tool, the access flow, and the safety rules for tiered apps, links and financial actions. It is not for websites, which go through Claude in Chrome or the built-in browser and their own skills.\n- anthropic-skills:deep-research: Use this skill when the user's prompt requires (1) researching a topic across multiple sources, comparing options or alternatives, analyzing trends or history, understanding markets or industries, or reviewing literature or studies and (2) synthesizing that research into a comprehensive, narrative report. If you're planning to search the web or internal knowledge bases, consider using this skill. This skill coordinates research subagents, so use it only when you have a tool for spawning subagents (the Agent or Task tool); otherwise, research the question directly.\n- anthropic-skills:docs: docs (editable docs people share and comment on; the default for any document, named as a doc or not: a document, report, proposal, resume, cover letter, letter, contract, policy, form, template, worksheet, essay, handbook, guide, how-to, cheat sheet, SOP or other writing to keep, share, collaborate on, send, submit, print or sign; a doc exports to Word, PDF, Markdown or Google Docs, so needing a file to send, attach, upload, submit or print is no reason to pick Word, and a file nobody asked for is a doc, not Word; a plan, comparison, summary or notes asked in chat stays in chat; a pasted claude.ai artifact link may be a doc: check with docs tools first; Word or another file format named, tracked changes wanted, or a .docx to change or use as a template → that format's skill): making one → if no docs-connector instructions are in context, call its `guide` (topic.instructions) first; then create the doc (headings only, no body) before any search, file read or plan, even with files attached.\n- anthropic-skills:docx: Use this skill whenever the user wants to create, read, edit, or manipulate Word documents (.docx) or Word templates (.dotx). Triggers include: any mention of Microsoft Word Documents, such as 'Word doc', 'word document', '.docx', '.dotx', 'microsoft doc'. Also use when extracting or reorganizing content from .docx or .dotx files, inserting or replacing images in documents, find-and-replace in Word files, working with tracked changes or comments, or converting content into a polished Word document. If the user asks for a deliverable as a Word or .docx file (to download, email or print), use this skill. However, if they ask for a document, page, report, memo, or notes WITHOUT naming a file format and the session offers Claude's own dedicated document or page skill or connector, use that instead, even if they will email or print it. Do NOT use for PDFs, spreadsheets, Google Docs, or coding unrelated to document generation.\n- anthropic-skills:google-workspace: Read this before the first Google Drive, Docs, Sheets or Slides connector call whenever the task creates or changes a Google file. Use this skill whenever the user wants to create or change a Google Doc, Sheet or Slides file in their Google Drive. Triggers include: a request that names Google Docs, Sheets, Slides or Drive and asks to make, edit, format, copy or rename a file; a docs.google.com link with a request to change that file, even a one-line fix or suggested edits; and any follow-up change to a Google file from earlier in the chat, even \"change it\" or \"add a tab\". Includes helper scripts for document positions, cell ranges and slide layout. However, if the user asks for a doc, deck or spreadsheet without naming Google, or gives a Google file only as source material for something new, use Claude's own output type instead. Do NOT use for read-only questions about a Google file, or for Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF files.\n- anthropic-skills:import-memory: Import a memory export from another AI assistant into Claude's memory — conversationally, additively, and with the content treated as data.\n- anthropic-skills:morning: Render the user's morning brief as a styled HTML artifact, or set it up as a recurring weekday task. Use only when the user explicitly asks to run, see, or set up their morning brief, or if they invoke /morning by name. A question about their day, schedule, or calendar is not by itself a request for the brief; answer it directly instead.\n- anthropic-skills:pdf: Use this skill whenever the user wants to do anything with PDF files. This includes reading or extracting text/tables from PDFs, combining or merging multiple PDFs into one, splitting PDFs apart, rotating pages, adding watermarks, creating new PDFs, filling PDF forms, encrypting/decrypting PDFs, extracting images, and OCR on scanned PDFs to make them searchable. If the user mentions a .pdf file or asks to produce one, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:pptx: Use this skill any time a .pptx or .potx file is involved in any way — as input, output, or both. This includes: creating slide decks, pitch decks, or presentations as PowerPoint (.pptx) files; reading, parsing, or extracting text from any .pptx or .potx file (even if the extracted content will be used elsewhere, like in an email, summary, or creating a different type of slide deck); editing, modifying, or updating existing presentations; combining or splitting slide files; working with templates (.potx), layouts, speaker notes, or comments. Trigger whenever the user asks for a PowerPoint or .pptx file, or references a .pptx or .potx filename, regardless of what they plan to do with the content afterward. However, when the user asks for a deck, slides, a slide deck, or a presentation without naming a file format, default to using a dedicated slide-deck artifact type or a separate slides skill if this session offers one; otherwise, use this skill.\n- anthropic-skills:setup-writing-style: Learns how the user writes from their own sent messages and docs, and builds a voice profile so future drafts sound like them instead of generic AI. The profile is saved as the my-writing-style skill. Use when the user asks to set up, learn, or capture their writing voice, or complains that drafts sound generic or unlike them and no my-writing-style profile exists. Only for drafting text the user will send as themselves, not for Claude's own replies.\n- anthropic-skills:skill-creator: Create new skills, modify and improve existing skills, and measure skill performance. Use when users want to create a skill from scratch, edit, or optimize an existing skill, run evals to test a skill, benchmark skill performance with variance analysis, or optimize a skill's description for better triggering accuracy.\n- anthropic-skills:xlsx: Use this skill any time a spreadsheet file is the primary input or output. This means any task where the user wants to: open, read, edit, or fix an existing .xlsx, .xlsm, .xltx, .csv, or .tsv file (e.g., adding columns, computing formulas, formatting, charting, cleaning messy data); create a new spreadsheet from scratch or from other data sources; or convert between tabular file formats. Trigger especially when the user references a spreadsheet file by name or path — even casually (like \"the xlsx in my downloads\") — and wants something done to it or produced from it. Also trigger for cleaning or restructuring messy tabular data files (malformed rows, misplaced headers, junk data) into proper spreadsheets. The deliverable must be a spreadsheet file. Do NOT trigger when the primary deliverable is a Word document, HTML report, standalone Python script, database pipeline, or Google Sheets API integration, even if tabular data is involved.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","sessionId":"cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"zany-kindling-liskov"}
{"parentUuid":"3071a1d6-dc0b-407a-ac12-cb9eb665d6f1","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"af4ae0306e46b6e08","attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# SchwingNet\n\nThis repository is a Claude project kept as files: its instructions, its goal, its shared memory and its board live here, and Claude Code reads them in every session, local or cloud. The repository is the source of truth for every Claude session and every account; a claude.ai Project only links it, its own fields stay blank, and Claude Code loads this file and what it imports at the start of every session, a Project's thread and a cloud session opened on the repository alike.\n\n## Read first\n\n- The goal, the scope and who owns what, loaded into every session by this line: @docs/project.md\n- How work is claimed, done in the open and finished, loaded the same way: @docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n- Who works here: every session, routine and worker in GitHub Actions is a worker under a name of its own, labelled worker:<name> (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). A session's name is the GitHub login of the person it works for, unless its environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; a routine's or a worker in Actions' is the one its prompt gives.\n- The board: the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow from the open issues, the claim branches and the pull requests. It lives on another branch, which no import reaches: read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on main only describes it.\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board, check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n\n## Shared memory\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nA fact worth keeping is one file under docs/memory/ with one index line in MEMORY.md, added in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- The board first (.claude/rules/board.md): fetch, read the board (STATE.md on the branch `board`) and PROTOCOL.md, check the claim; never push to another worker's branch; never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. A hook refuses the pushes this rule forbids, and /board (.claude/commands/board.md) reprints the whole board mid-session.\n- Memory (.claude/rules/memory.md): one fact per file, a kebab-case name, a one-line description first, one index line; never a secret.\n- Writing (.claude/rules/writing.md): plain prose that says why, short functions, names that read as English, no emoji, ASCII and LF, no new dependencies; tag a durable fact in conversation as Decision: or Constraint: so a checkpoint keeps it.\n- Research (.claude/rules/research.md): what a session finds out goes under docs/research/, one file or folder per topic that opens by naming its source, with a line in docs/research/README.md, in the same pull request as the work and before its checkpoint; a handoff under docs/checkpoints/ points to it and never carries it.\n- Skills: .claude/skills/claim, .claude/skills/remember and .claude/skills/checkpoint are the three procedures, in the order a piece of work meets them; .claude/skills/drive-organize (a OneDrive sorted into projects, read through Microsoft Graph under the owner's own Microsoft sign-in), .claude/skills/lens-review (several reviewers on one Word document) and .claude/skills/notebook (drive files held together as a notebook in a repository of its own, never moved) are run when the owner asks.\n- The block at the end of this file, between the cswap markers, is rewritten by the checkpoint skill and by `cswap checkpoint write`; do not edit it by hand.\n\n## Checks\n\nRun `bash scripts/check.sh` before opening or updating a pull request. CI runs the same script as the check named \"check\", and the default branch takes only pull requests that pass it. The same check fails a pull request marked ready whose body has no section `## Adversarial review` (tools/review_record.py, CONTRIBUTING.md), so write the review into the body before marking it ready.\n\n## Session checkpoint (kept by cswap)\n\nRead `.claude/session.md` at the start of a session: it holds the completed work, the current state and the remaining tasks. Tag durable facts in conversation as `Decision:` or `Constraint:` so the next `cswap checkpoint write` keeps them.\n\n### Durable decisions\n- The goal (the owner, 2026-10-20): low-latency, high-accuracy vertebral classification on fluoroscopic video or images; the data workflow first, one model per stage as version 1, evaluation on real frames visible from the first checkpoint, no ensembles until one model works, no comparison to the old baseline.\n- Every grid job and every training waits for the owner's word and the number of jobs; usage credits are never used; CT-RATE (21.3 TB) is never submitted without his TB number.\n- Dataset request letters go from the owner's Wayne State Outlook on his word, subject tag [XR/CT Imaging Research], short, the opening line \"I study the application of computer science to improving spinal health, and my current work needs ...\", addresses verified from institutional pages or papers, he signs any DUA; no external text says or implies that a model, a neural network, machine learning, AI or training is built or used.\n- The unheld datasets are obtained with collaborators in a separate private repository worked through Symphony (OpenSpineConsortium/spine-datasets, the owner's ask of 2026-10-21); SchwingNet stays confidential until the provisional is filed, and the talk shows results, not methods.\n- Radiopaedia is never fetched by script.\n### Constraints\n- A cloud session cannot create a repository, approve a deployment or publish a release (403), and its proxy refuses figshare, Zenodo, Mendeley, Hugging Face, openi.nlm.nih.gov, doi.org and most publishers, so no real fetch runs in a container; the grid paste in grid/README.md is the first real run.\n- The pre-push hook runs the whole gate and refuses a push from a tree with anything uncommitted; a cloud session exports PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\" before pushing.\n- Scripts under tools/ drop tools/ from sys.path before importing torch (tools/profile.py shadows the standard library's profile); functions that take a pydantic model are listed under [tool.beethoven-qa.unproven].\n- The Microsoft 365 connector sends only a draft created first (create, then send); a direct send is refused."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/docs/project.md","type":"Project","content":"# SchwingNet: goal and settings\n\nThis file is the project's goal and the settings every account's thin Project shares. It is the one place where the goal is written: CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session, and the claude.ai Projects carry no copy of it. It changes by pull request like everything else.\n\n## Goal\n\nConfidential: this file describes unpublished work with a provisional patent in preparation, and stays in this private repository; nothing of it is copied to a public place. SchwingNet names vertebral levels on intraoperative fluoroscopy; the reported stack and its numbers are spine-program's 01_carm_level_detection (README.md, STATUS.md, docs/MODEL_CARD.md). This project makes the next SchwingNet as a workflow that grows: low-latency, high-accuracy vertebral classification on fluoroscopic video or images, built through Symphony's claim protocol and beethoven-qa's definition of done between 2026-10-20 and the CNS talk of 2 November 2026 (mini-oral 66629), so that every number the talk shows is reproduced by a command from a record in this repository. Done is: a dataset added by one pull request and one incremental run, no epoch run twice; version 1 of SchwingNet, one model per stage trained to work on the registry as it stands, with its card; an evaluation page that draws fixed real witness frames at every checkpoint and tables every version with intervals and its latency, in Weights and Biases and on this repository's docs site; and the recorded C-arm sweep with the overlay and the measured latency, ready to show once the provisional is filed (by 30 October). The plan is docs/research/retraining-plan.md (version 2, the owner's words of 2026-10-20); no training job is submitted before the owner gives the word and the number for it.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope: the areas this project owns, each in one line; docs/agents/OWNERSHIP.md maps them to workers.\n\n- The retraining, once the owner has stated the measure: the pre-registered plan, the grid jobs through Beethoven (docs/setup/grid.md), the clean encoder rebuild, the split check, the eval gate; every number carries its run id, commit and result file.\n- The measurement: held-out C-ArmLSpine, the transitional stratum with two readers, the synthetic anomaly arm with exact truth, never pooled with the real one.\n- The demo: the recorded sweep (C-ArmLSpine case134 and case39), the overlay, the measured latency, the slides' figures.\n- The quality gate: new code to beethoven-qa's definition of done; the legacy tools of the reported pipeline listed under unproven, never refactored before the talk, since a refactor voids the parity gate.\n- The synthesis of the artifacts (artifacts/_synthesis/), the record the Artifact Textbook is written from, kept current by .github/workflows/synthesis.yml: it looks with no Claude at every change of an artifact, a research note or a dataset record, within minutes of a merge a person or an App makes and at the next daily run for one GitHub's auto-merge makes, and runs the Claude reads a change needs, 10 runs a day unless the variable SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY says otherwise, 50 at most, each adding what it read to the one open synthesis pull request, or opening it (#166).\n\nOut of scope, by decision: what this project does not do, each with the reason, so nobody proposes it again.\n\n- Anything public before the provisional is filed: no push to a public repository, no preprint, no slide of the method; the talk shows what SchwingNet does, not how (the owner, 2026-10-20).\n- A live run at the podium: no laptop latency exists and the CPU path took seconds per frame; the demo is a recording of a causal run with its latency on screen.\n- A grid job, a Claude run or a training without the owner's word and the number of jobs; usage credits are never used. The synthesis reads of .github/workflows/synthesis.yml have that word and number: the owner chose on 2026-10-23 that they start on their own, 10 runs a day unless the variable SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY says otherwise, 50 at most, and the variable SYNTHESIS_READS set to off stops them (#166). The repository's private claude.ai front door, tools/front_door.html, runs no workflow and no Claude run of the project's: no GitHub Action's session carries the Artifact tool and a cloud thread's checks refuse to publish a page that declares a connector, so the owner publishes it from the owner's own computer with `python3 tools/textbook_publish.py local`, the owner's choice of 2026-10-25 (#236, #277), once and again only when the page changes, the page being since 2026-10-27 the door's loader, tools/front_door_loader.html, which runs the door main holds at every open, so a change to the door needs no publish (the owner, 2026-10-27); opened, it reads the repository itself, main and the board, through the viewer's own GitHub connector in claude.ai, with the viewer's own access, so nothing is staged or republished when the repository moves, the owner's word of 2026-10-26 (it replaced the reader of #294, which read a copy a thread staged into the Claude project's files, since the Artifact tool publishes only what Claude has read whole). The textbook's research loop, .github/workflows/research.yml, has a standing exception for grid jobs, fetches and training: the owner asked on 2026-10-24 at 16:17 UTC for a Claude integration that continues the research \"AUTONOMOUSLY ... INDEPDENTLY OF HUMAN INTERACTION\", and at 17:36 UTC, told it would ask first, answered \"no i dont want this restriction ... give it full command to direct autonmous research\" (#220). It runs every four hours, at most RESEARCH_RUNS_PER_DAY runs a day (6 unless it says otherwise, 50 at most), the variable RESEARCH_LOOP set to off stops it, and it still keeps the size rule, the size readers for gdown, Kaggle and Redivis (#163), no CT-RATE without the owner's terabytes, no Radiopaedia by script, no usage credits, the claim protocol and nothing public; letters, e-mails and the gate words it cannot reach stay the owner's (.github/symphony/research.md).\n- A settings-as-code app (github/safe-settings, or the Repository Settings App reading .github/settings.yml), decided no on 2026-10-11 (symphony-template#18): it is a dependency with admin rights on every repository it covers, and a server of our own in one case, for settings that Symphony Setup applies for one run from the front door, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables for what an organization makes.\n\n## Repositories\n\n- OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet, this one: the shared plane for every account and every session; the default branch is main.\n- Repositories this project reads, all private, each a record and never changed from here (a change lands as a patch in the claim's pull request, as spine-program's CLAUDE.md says):\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/spine-program, folder 01_carm_level_detection: the reported SchwingNet, its STATUS.md numbers, MODEL_CARD.md, the cine demo build plan and the handoff; 02_transitional_naming_truth for the transitional truth file and the frozen G3 split; 07_plain_film_level_id for the CNS manuscript rows.\n  - Gregory-Schwing-MD-PhD/spinesurg-ct-nnunet, branch L6_Class: the training and evaluation code (tools/level_prototype.py, tools/eval_gate.py, the slurm/ scripts); the demo tools cine_eval.py and cine_latency.py are untracked there and live as copies in spine-program's 01_carm_level_detection/code/.\n  - The grid, Wayne State's, reached through Beethoven's runner chain (docs/setup/grid.md): the corpora under /rs/rs_grp_oschome/go2432/data/, C-ArmLSpine among them; nothing of them is ever copied here.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven-qa, private, to be taken as a pinned copy under vendor/beethoven-qa once its 6.0.0 is merged and tagged (issue #5; the steps are symphony-template's docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md, symphony-template#280), and OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven-research, whose split check the measurement uses.\n- The template's own repositories, four, each owned by the OpenSpineConsortium organization, whose owners merge to its default branch:\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, public: the claim protocol as a Python library with no dependencies (the board, the protocol check, team mode's clerk, the push guard's rules and the merge gate). It is vendored here under tools/sdlc_protocol, with tools/VENDORED recording the version, the commit and the sha256 of every copied file, and scripts/check.sh fails when the copy differs from that record, or, on CI, which clones the library, from the library's files at that commit. A change to the rules is made there, never here: once the owner has approved and published a release there that carries it, its sync workflow opens the pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template with that release's copy, and the template's check decides. A repository made from the template is not on the sync's list; its own .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each new release in instead, weekly, as a pull request from a branch of the same name, or as an issue linking that pull request where GitHub Actions may not open one. By hand it is `python3 <symphony-protocol>/scripts/vendor.py <this repository> --consumer OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template` and a pull request. Its ecosystem.json is the one list of which repository takes which modules, and its ECOSYSTEM.md, generated from that list, maps all five repositories.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/coordbench, public: the playground and benchmark that compares ways of coordinating Claude Code agents on CooperBench's tasks, this protocol among them; its sdlc arm runs on the library's merge gate, vendored under coordbench/vendor. It began as experiment/ on OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template's branch claude/10.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-marketplace, public: the Claude Code plugin marketplace with one plugin, symphony, which brings the skills, /board and the hooks to a repository not made from this template. .claude/settings.json names the marketplace and enables nothing, since this repository wires the same hooks itself.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-app, private: the GitHub App SymphonyBoard (slug symphonyboard). Its installation token lets template-init's first commit start the Checks and the Board, which a push with GITHUB_TOKEN cannot, and the symphony-protocol sync opens its pull requests in every consumer with it; its webhook server, where it is run, posts the protocol check as a check run of its own, sdlc/protocol.\n\n## Model and effort\n\nEvery account's Project uses the same model and the same effort, so the work reads the same whoever did it. Record the choice here and set it in each Project's settings when you make the Project (docs/setup/claude-project.md).\n\n- Model: not yet chosen.\n- Effort: not yet chosen.\n\n## Crowd mode\n\n- Crowd mode: off. To open the project to volunteers who each work on their own Claude account, the owner runs `python3 scripts/crowd.py setup` (docs/setup/crowdsourcing.md), whose pull request turns this line on with the decisions that come with it; from then on the required check holds the repository to them.\n\n## Owners\n\n- Repository owner: OpenSpineConsortium, who merges to main; .github/CODEOWNERS names Gregory-Schwing-MD-PhD, the owner's own login, or in an organization's copy the person who made the copy, because GitHub asks a user or a team for review and never an organization (docs/setup/github.md, section 7).\n- Workers: each person's sessions, under the person's GitHub login, and the routines and workers in GitHub Actions the registry .github/symphony/workers/ names (docs/agents/WORKERS.md); none registered yet. Each worker's label is worker:<name>, made when it first needs it.\n- The Claude token: the secret CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, from one account's `claude setup-token`, on whose plan @claude, the workers in Actions, the coordinator workflow, the synthesis reads and the research loop run (docs/setup/claude-action.md); not set until the setup issue's second box is ticked, or until the owner adds it for the synthesis reads, the one Claude credential the project keeps, by the owner's choice of 2026-10-23 (#166)."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md","type":"Project","content":"# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by .github/workflows/board.yml, which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to main, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or a worker in GitHub Actions, which .github/workflows/workers.yml runs on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike. docs/agents/WORKERS.md says how they share the repository.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session's environment sets `SYMPHONY_WORKER` to another; a person needs no file to work here. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. .github/symphony/workers/README.md lists the settings, and `bash scripts/check.sh` fails on any file that does not hold together (`python3 tools/workers.py check`). A worker is added with the \"Add a worker\" issue form, whose workflow .github/workflows/add-worker.yml opens the pull request that adds its file, or by adding the file by hand in a pull request of its own. A person may register too, with `\"runs\": \"session\"`, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take as Pick says.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done (Blockers).\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. Where the labels of several workers name one issue, it is theirs alone in the same way: given to each of them, and held by the first of them to claim it, whose Announce takes the others' labels off. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below). The board's Queue leaves an epic out, since nobody takes one whole.\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\ntemplate-init makes every label above but worker:<name>, six in all, when the repository is made, and scripts/setup-repo.sh makes them again from a clone. A worker's label is made when the worker first needs it: the claim skill makes it before putting it on an issue, and the coordinator when it gives a worker a task.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first. Else take a blocker, with or without todo, that the Blockers section lists, that nobody has claimed and that no worker's label gives to another, since a blocker comes before the Queue. Else take an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of. Of the blockers and the Queue, take only one in your area when your file under .github/symphony/workers/ gives you one; an issue that lists no files fits every area (Planning). Wherever an issue is listed, never take an epic, which the coordinator plans (Planning), nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link, on the template in docs/agents/claims/README.md. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue. The board reads two parts of the claim file: the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix (a line with nothing after it but markup or a note in parentheses names nobody: the board shows such a claim as unnamed, since the file is there to be corrected, while the clerk and a release, which must name a worker, take the one the issue's label names, as they do once the pull request has deleted the file), and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes its `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off every other worker's label, one an earlier claim left there or one that gave the issue to several workers, so that from the claim on the labels name the holder alone, as the claim file does; and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. GitHub opens one pull request at a time between the same two branches, so where one that someone who may not write opened from the branch stands in the way, which is no claim's (Rules that hold everywhere), close it, as anyone who may write can, and open your own. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, which it does in whichever run finds the branch behind or conflicting, once per commit of the default branch, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. The board reads a branch as behind where GitHub calls its pull request behind, as it does under a rule that requires an up-to-date branch, or, where GitHub's word is blocked or draft, which says only that something else stops the merge, a rule still holding the pull request or the draft itself, where the comparison of the branch with the default branch says so. A pull request GitHub calls clean, unstable or has_hooks, a draft among them, since GitHub gives a draft the word its branch has, is not held for being behind under the repository's rules, so it hears nothing however far the default branch has moved; one GitHub calls dirty, in conflict with the default branch, hears it. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch. Since a worker that has fetched holds a remote-tracking ref that matches origin, git itself would take its lease onto any claim, so before a lease onto `claude/N` the push guard reads the claim file `docs/agents/claims/N.md` on the branch the lease holds against (the commit the lease names, else origin's `claude/N` as the fetch left it) and on the branch pushed, each at its tip or, once the branch has deleted the file, where its history last added it, and refuses a lease that would take another worker's claim: one whose two files name different workers, or whose branch pushed carries none; a claim file git cannot show leaves the lease to GitHub's rules, as the guard leaves everything it cannot read (the template carries it since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy takes it once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, whose Given to workers heading 0.5.0 changes, since the copy's protocol.yml brings only tools/, and with the template's tools/guard_push.py where its own predates symphony-template#69, since an older one hands the guard no claim files).\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes `bash scripts/check.sh` and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `\"lands\": \"auto-merge\"`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. A worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, such as a cloud session whose `gh pr` calls are refused, keeps the claim file instead, pushes the finished work with `bash scripts/check.sh` passing, comments \"finished; ready to mark\" on its draft and stops, leaving the steps above to a person who can take them; until then its claim is not released (Release). Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself (docs/setup/github.md). An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: .github/workflows/tidy.yml deletes `claude/<issue>` once no open pull request uses it, and with it any `released/<issue>-<sha>` a released claim left (Release).\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. The board lists every open issue labelled blocker under Blockers, claimed or not, and a worker takes one there before the Queue, as Pick says. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it held waits for it, helps on the one pull request by review, or takes other work it does not stand in the way of (Pick).\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready says it is done (Finish). When a branch's last commit came, here and below, is when GitHub received the push that set its head, as the repository's activity records that push, so no worker's clock decides it; an entry that set the branch to another head, or deleted it, dates it too when it came after the run began, a push after the run read the branch, while an older one is stale and lists no push that set the head; the head's committer date stands in where the activity, read once more after a pause when the first read lists no push that set the head or GitHub gives it no answer that holds, still lists none, or where GitHub refuses to read it, since the push a run was started by is the one the activity is likeliest not to list yet, but never earlier than the newest entry either read listed for the branch, which the push that set the head came no sooner than. In the run that a push to the branch started, which the workflow's event names, a head no read dates is dated at the run's start instead, and so is one a read dates by an entry that set it more than a few minutes before the run began, an older push of the same head that the activity lists while it does not list that push yet, or that push itself where the run, queued behind others, began that much after it, for that run alone, since that push came just before the run, whatever the two reads list, so no claim is released in the run its own push started; the next run reads the activity again. The coordinator here is a step of the board's own run, in .github/workflows/board.yml, on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). Before it releases, it frees each reopened issue whose labels still speak of a claim that ended with its close (Finish), so the in-review that claim left finishes no claim, and a claim branch still standing for the issue is released in that same run once it has gone a day without a commit. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off, and puts the label todo on it when it carries none of todo, epic and blocker, so the Queue lists it again even when a worker's label alone gave it to the worker, while a blocker is listed under Blockers without it; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails at the labels, taking one off or putting todo on, puts back the labels it took off, even one whose taking off GitHub answered with an error, unless GitHub refused it with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of taking it off, since GitHub may have taken it off and a label put back that is still on changes nothing, and takes off the todo it put on, even one whose putting on GitHub answered with an error, since GitHub may have written it, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. A run whose comment fails reads the issue's comments, and the comment there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, makes the release whole. Where they list none and GitHub refused the comment, with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of writing anything, the run puts back the labels it took off and takes off the todo it put on, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. Where they list none after any other answer to the comment, a rate limit's refusal, a 429 or a 403 that names a rate limit or a wait in retry-after, on the last try or on one the run made the comment again after, which GitHub's documentation never says it gives in place of acting on the call, a server error, a redirect, an answer the run could not read or no answer at all, none of which says GitHub wrote nothing, and GitHub may list a comment it wrote only later, or where the run cannot read them, whatever GitHub answered the comment with, the run cannot tell whether a release comment stands, its own, an earlier run's or one someone else wrote: it leaves the labels as the release left them and counts no release of its own, so its page lists the claim as held and the branch keeps its name, since labels put back beside a comment that stands would hold the claim for good, the comment telling every later coordinator the claim is released and the label claimed telling the board it is held, and the next run, reading the comments, finds the release whole where a release comment that counts stands, or releases the claim again where none does, unless its holder pushed since, which keeps the claim, its labels as the release left them, naming no holder, until it finishes, a person puts them back or it goes a day without a commit and is released again, or a person put the label claimed back beside a comment that stands, which undoes that release (below), though a branch at a head a release already kept is released in that same run by its latest release comment, where one that counts stands, which counts for such a branch whenever it came, with no word on the issue of this run's release where GitHub wrote none. Where GitHub refuses the putting back too the labels are left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; so, running on every board run, it releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, todo put on as the coordinator puts it, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest. A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue as it lists any unclaimed one, in the Queue or, for a blocker, under Blockers, though until the same run has renamed the branch Pick reads the branch as another worker's and the clerk deals the issue to nobody (Planning), and .github/workflows/board.yml renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file (.claude/skills/claim says how). Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept, one a branch `released/<issue>-<short sha>` stands at, brings nothing new: its latest release comment counts for it whenever it came, before that push or after, so the board deletes it instead of renaming it, since its work is already kept, and the coordinator releases it only where the board counts no release of it, no release comment counting or the label claimed put back, once it has gone a day without a commit. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules, and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target (docs/setup/github.md).\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest, the work that began first, when GitHub says the push that made its branch came, or, for a pull request from a fork or from a branch it says nothing of, when the pull request was opened; work whose beginning nothing says, which can only be a claim with no pull request yet, counts as the newest, since a claim opens its draft at once (Work in the open), and the number breaks a tie. In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it; a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. The marker names each piece by its identity: a claim and its own pull requests are one piece, the issue's number, told on that issue, so opening a pull request tells nothing again, and a marker on one of the claim's own open pull requests counts as one on the issue; a pull request that is no claim's is a piece of its own, its number, told on itself. An issue's number names each claim on it in turn, so a marker tells a claim's work, or tells of it, only when it came no earlier than the push that made that claim's branch, as GitHub dates that push for the order above, and every marker counts for a claim whose push GitHub does not date. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude in .github/workflows/coordinator.yml on the repository's Claude token (docs/agents/WORKERS.md), plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it todo and `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue, labelled todo alone. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: tools/team.py. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run of .github/workflows/workers.yml do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes its own claim that needs work, one whose claim file names it, whatever the issue's labels say, or, where no claim file names a worker, one whose issue's label names it, a worker:<name> label before the older spellings and the first by name among labels of one spelling, as a release names whose the claim was, but never a claim that counts as released, which no worker carries and none takes until the board has renamed its branch (Pick, Release), nor a claim labelled blocked, whose holder waits on a peer or the coordinator (Labels) and so has nothing else to do; else the task it was given, one given to it alone first, and a task the labels of several workers give going to the first of them, in the order the registry lists the workers in Actions, with nothing else to do; else the next unclaimed blocker or queued issue, blockers first, whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area). The order is the team's: every worker the deal is for, in .github/workflows/workers.yml every registered worker in Actions, whether or not the run starts it, so a worker outside the run keeps its share, and two runs that look at one state deal no issue to two workers. The workers of one run are dealt from one look at the repository, taken once for the run and handed to each worker (`python3 tools/team.py deal`), since two workers looking for themselves at different moments, one while a peer's ready pull request is still clean and one after the default branch has moved past it, could be dealt one issue; a worker that asks alone (`python3 tools/team.py next`) is dealt by the same rule from its own look.\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard, tools/guard_push.py, wired as a hook in .claude/settings.json, refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it (docs/setup/github.md).\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from (.claude/rules/memory.md).\n- `bash scripts/check.sh` passes before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (tools/check_protocol.py, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in this repository, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it.\n- A claim and its pull request live in this repository, so a pull request from a fork is no claim's, whatever its branch is called: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename, which closes the claim's own pull request, leaves it open.\n- A claim's pull request is opened by the repository's owner, a member of its organization, a collaborator or anyone else who may write to the repository, or by a GitHub App, which opens one only where it is installed, as the workers in Actions open theirs; a pull request anyone else opens from a claim's branch, as GitHub's documentation says anyone who may read the repository can, is no claim's either: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename closes it with the claim's own, since GitHub closes each open pull request whose branch it renames. Where GitHub will not answer whether someone may write, a pull request they opened stays the claim's, and a release comment or an overlap marker they wrote counts for nothing, so a lost answer never frees a finished claim and never keeps work from being told."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/docs/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"Project","content":"# Memory index\n\nOne line per fact under docs/memory/: a dash, the fact's title as a link to its file, a colon and one line saying what it is. CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session; a fact's own file is read when its line says it matters. Add or correct a fact with the remember skill (.claude/skills/remember): one file, one line here, in the same pull request as the change it came from. This index stays under 200 lines and 25 KB, a limit of this project's own: an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing here or a line names a file that is not there.\n\n- [A copy runs the template's tests](a-copy-runs-the-templates-tests.md): every copy's required check runs the template's tests over the filled files, sharing perhaps off; tests/test_filled_copy.py runs a filled copy's check, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, so a test that holds only in the template fails there first.\n- [A new repository starts with the owner's commit](a-new-repository-starts-with-the-owners-commit.md): the push guard refuses a push to main in any repository a session's git touches, so the owner creates a new repository with \"Add a README\" ticked, or adds one from its empty page, and sessions bring everything else by pull request.\n- [A Project with several repositories runs no hooks](a-project-with-several-repositories-runs-no-hooks.md): GitHub's ruleset, the claim lock, the required check, /board and the claim skill still hold; the push guard, the board at session start and the Symphony-Usage trailer do not, so keep one repository per Project.\n- [A template copies no settings](a-template-copies-no-settings.md): what \"Use this template\" leaves to the owner (one run of scripts/setup-repo.sh, the token from claude setup-token, or scripts/crowd.py setup for a crowd repository) and what an organization on Team or above can set once for every repository it makes.\n- [Agents](agents.md): agents are Claude Code subagent files under .claude/agents/ with .github/symphony/agents.json, imported by pull request (tools/agents.py, agents.yml), run in sessions, Actions and as workers on the tokens accounts.json gives their names, and measured as they are by coordbench --agent-file and a flowbench stage's agent; docs/agents/AGENTS.md.\n- [An auto-merge starts main's workflows only when the App armed it](auto-merge-starts-no-workflow.md): from beethoven-qa 7.0.0 the kit's automerge.yml arms with the SymphonyBoard App's token, so its merge starts main's push workflows; a pull request that changes a workflow file, or one armed while the App's token cannot be made, is still armed with the workflow token, whose merge starts none and leaves its issue open: close it by hand and Run workflow what must follow.\n- [Artifacts are kept as files](artifacts-are-kept-as-files.md): the artifacts in the claude.ai gallery of each of the owner's accounts live in artifacts/ of their repository, less what the export's screen holds back for the owner (SchwingNet and spine work here, Symphony and Beethoven in symphony-template), a page as index.html with its files, a Docs document as index.md, one row each in artifacts/README.md; each new artifact and each other account's are added the same way, an account's all at once by symphony-template's /export-artifacts, and its artifacts-grid.yml keeps both repositories' folders in ~/claude-artifacts on the grid for every account (the owner, 2026-10-22).\n- [beethoven-qa in a Symphony repository](beethoven-qa-in-a-symphony-repository.md): a repository made from the template carries beethoven-qa 6.0.0 or later beside Symphony (the claim protocol on one side, the contracts gate and code standards on the other, a draft with a claim file satisfying both); the kit hook is beethoven-qa-session-start.sh since the old name collided with the board hook; check.sh runs the copy's vendor-check only under pixi; the steps are docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md.\n- [Beethoven's worker and Claude projects](beethoven-worker-and-claude-projects.md): a project reaches the worker only through its own Work locally, never through the worker's link, so Beethoven 21.0.0 (beethoven-extension#17) opens the project's page named in Settings; the research is docs/research/beethoven-worker-opening.md, with what is not yet measured (the successor's resume, --spawn worktree); a merge to beethoven-extension's main can be a Chrome Web Store release (CWS_ON_PUSH).\n- [bordercall on the grid](bordercall-on-the-grid.md): the word `bordercall <pilot|full>` runs bordercall over the census's existing SPINEPS masks counted from C2, and `bordercall complete` (SchwingNet #470, one CPU job on the owner's count of 2026-10-29 15:22 UTC) runs its matcher over those masks for the gold tier 3 completion, the report with no data-root path (no C2, no number; never TotalSegmentator, never a re-run; ribs from the Moller table as the independent arm) on the owner's count of two CPU jobs, the copy under grid/bordercall/ held to its VENDORED record by check.sh; `update`, the status word, the pilot, then `full`; docs/research/bordercall-corpus.md.\n- [Carried folders](carried-folders.md): files a cloud session holds reach the grid as a commit on a private branch, a record grid/carry/<id>.txt and the word `fetch <id>`, which git lands on the login node, checked against SHA256SUMS, with no job; the first is the project symphony_4's shared folder (2026-10-28).\n- [CI runs Python 3.12](ci-python-is-3-12.md): the checks run on GitHub's 3.12, so tools and tests use no API newer than that; test with python3.12 where it exists.\n- [Claim branches push by name](claim-branches-push-by-name.md): a claim branch is made with --no-track, a clone made for one branch is told to fetch every branch, every push names its branch and the lease the tip pushed, since git's push.default, a single-branch clone and a lease after a fetch each sent a push somewhere the push guard cannot see.\n- [@claude runs on the main account's subscription](claude-mentions-run-on-the-main-accounts-subscription.md): mentions run .github/workflows/claude.yml on the token from `claude setup-token`, spending plan limits, never an API key; in crowd mode it never runs.\n- [Code owners are users or teams](code-owners-are-users-or-teams.md): GitHub never asks an organization for review, so template-init writes the owner, or in an organization's copy the person who made it, into .github/CODEOWNERS; check with the codeowners/errors API.\n- [Crowd mode](crowd-mode.md): scripts/crowd.py sets a repository up for volunteers on their own Claude accounts; the variable CLAUDE_CROWD, the required check's crowd guard and queue.yml then keep the Claude workflows off, Claude secrets out, checkpoints out of commits and the queue to maintainers.\n- [Dataset mail is read with Junk Email](dataset-mail-is-read-with-junk-email.md): a read of the [XR/CT Imaging Research] mail searches Junk Email for returns too, since the ReXGradient-160K bounce of 2026-10-24 sat there unread; the connector cannot open a bounce's transcript.\n- [DICOM banks and leak checks](dicom-banks-and-leak-checks.md): DICOM decodes only in the train environment; NIH ChestX-ray14 is checked against Academic Torrents piece hashes since NIH gives no checksum; the leak checks run as grid/run-leaks.sh after stage 3; tests needing optional dependencies go under tests/ as unittest, since the gate refuses skips under tests/schwingnet; a CrossHair counterexample on catalog.changed_keys once flaked on CI.\n- [DRR domain-gap study](drr-domain-gap.md): tools/domain_gap.py, calibrated separation tests of FleXray's and other synthetic DRRs against the held fluoroscopy and film corpora with the replication test of FleXray's renderer and the segmenter block; bench flexray_drr_seg makes MOOSE's masks (flow drr_seg), bench flexray_drr_gap runs prepare, an array of 16 tasks on any GPU claiming units and a merge, each its own drr_gap flow run under the frozen PREREG.md with DEVIATIONS.md, the merge's writing the CLAIM report, every step a timestamped run log that status, the bench report and the CLAIM report show; results only in docs/research/drr-domain-gap/README.md.\n- [Every deep learning job runs on a launched flow](every-deep-learning-job-runs-on-a-launched-flow.md): the owner's rule of 2026-10-29: a flow flows/launch.py starts, listed in flows/registry.txt with its frozen PREREG (exit 90 when missing or moved, a Covers: line for the parts it covers), the CLAIM and REFORMS report checked in end (flows/stage.py) and a progress page under <datastore>/progress/, shown by status and drawn off the grid by tools/progress_page.py; the sweep, the FluXray reproduction's edit and stats, and the DRR study with its MOOSE step keep it.\n- [Evaluation and W&B](evaluation-and-wandb.md): witness frames chosen by schwingnet.witness and frozen by `flow.py record --freeze`, drawn at every checkpoint from epoch 0 (validation by the trainer, test over every kept epoch by the evaluator); W&B online where the grid's key is found (WANDB_API_KEY, ~/.wandb/token.env, ~/.netrc), offline pushed by the flow after each step otherwise, loud when not pushed; the page docs/research/evaluation/README.md from the recorded runs, never on Pages.\n- [Flux realism](flux-realism-edit.md): no CSAIL-trained realism model exists, FluXray is zero-shot FLUX.2 klein-9B over MOOSE DRRs; the reproduction on ENHANCE.PET CTs at FluXray's poses is frozen in docs/research/flux-realism/PREREG.md and runs as RealismFlow through flows/launch.py, edit then stats, with its CLAIM and REFORMS report and progress page; the count of 2026-10-29 is three fetches (sent 02:57 UTC, never again) and two GPU jobs, lora and eval refused until a count and PREREG of their own; resumed 2026-10-29 12:27 UTC, the review's 17 findings fixed in #456, the two GPU jobs sent after it merges.\n- [From 3D to X-ray](from-3d-to-x-ray.md): the programme carrying the segmented CTs to X-ray (docs/research/xr-from-3d/, issues #56 to #70, #127 and #135; the benchmarks to beat, FluoroSAM and Zeng 2024 among them, in docs/research/benchmarks-to-beat.md and docs/research/fluoroscopy-list-prior-art.md; the lumbosacral-to-C2 feasibility in docs/research/lumbosacral-to-cervical-extrapolation.md): the corner annotation format, anomalies called on CT as the counting prior's odds, settings chosen on validation only, the covariate census, COBRA non-commercial.\n- [Field metrics](field-metrics.md): .github/workflows/metrics.yml writes aggregates only (no name, title or text) to the branch metrics with GITHUB_TOKEN; a maintainer's own claude -p adds App F counts; a breakdown by work setting (one person's several sessions apart from several people) and in pairs; what the work cost, from sessions' Symphony-Usage trailers and the Claude workflows' usage artifacts, and seven alone-versus-parallel ratios, over 1 where parallel did better; PROFILE.json beside it sums the Symphony-Profile lines and the runs' profiles the same ways, unread by the collector; the switch is .github/symphony-metrics or SDLC_METRICS=off; the release marker and the board's words it counts.\n- [Grid jobs go through hpcworker job submit](grid-jobs-go-through-hpcworker-job-submit.md): every grid job is queued by grid/hpcw.sh through hpcworker's `job submit` with a class (batch for CPU, gpu for GPU), checked with --check first, no partition, QoS or output line in any .sbatch header; logs are in hpcworker's log folder, live jobs still read from squeue (issue #77).\n- [How this repository works](how-this-repository-works.md): the repository is the shared plane for every Claude account and session; what lives where.\n- [Issue dependency graphs](issue-dependency-graphs.md): GitHub holds the epic hierarchy and the \"blocked by\" dependencies natively and draws no graph, Linear draws none at issue level and Jira only on Premium; the recommendation of 2026-10-17, awaiting the owner's choices, is native blocked_by written by the coordinator and a Mermaid section drawn by the board, with Linear as an optional one-way tier (docs/research/control-board-graph.md).\n- [Labels and words that start a Claude run](labels-and-words-that-start-a-claude-run.md): epic starts coordinator.yml's plan unless the body holds `<!-- team:plan -->` first; todo, blocker and worker:<name> start workers.yml; \"@claude\" in an issue, a comment or a review starts claude.yml; goal starts nothing, and experiment.yml and figure.yml answer only their forms' issues; so a session marks an epic before labelling it and puts none of the others on without the owner's word.\n- [Latency and the recorded sweep](latency-and-the-recorded-sweep.md): task #13: no latency number without ONNX parity for the same file, the 97 ms budget, latency/<arm>.json and evaluate/latency.json in the run directory, truth boxes in v1 sweeps, the grid jobs in grid/run-latency-sweep.sh, each network part since 2026-10-29 the launched flow latency (flows/latency.py) under the frozen latency-and-sweep/PREREG.md; docs/research/latency-and-sweep/.\n- [Literature on the grid](literature-on-the-grid.md): the word `literature <list>` fetches a reading list of grid/literature/ onto the grid under the size rule with the Elsevier key and token the key route wrote (grid-accounts.yml key elsevier, s2), into a 0700 landing that only the owner's account and its grid sessions read, Elsevier's files only on the owner's word; the report names every paper not got; docs/research/literature-on-the-grid.md.\n- [Living docs](living-docs.md): the lessons under docs/learn/ run in the check (tools/living_docs.py test), their Colab notebooks are generated and must match (`notebooks`, then commit), every module needs a docstring for the Reference page, and docs.yml, run only on the owner's call since 2026-10-25, publishes the site with Pages once SYMPHONY_DOCS is pages (members only, the owner's choice of 2026-10-20).\n- [Mark a pull request ready on its own](mark-a-pull-request-ready-on-its-own.md): mark it ready in a call of its own after the body's edit and the claim file's deletion, since a draft's run that starts last fails the protocol check (#185).\n- [Morphometry on the grid](morphometry-on-the-grid.md): the word `morphometry <set>` runs bordercall's morphometry engine, pinned under grid/bordercall/ (ee495d5 since 2026-10-30), over the gold tier 3 masks: enhance, every ENHANCE.PET label map, and verse, VerSe's 374 int16 copies and TotalSegmentator's admitted subjects stacked to int16, once each on the owner's count of 2026-10-29 and once more on his of 2026-10-30 14:33 UTC (MORPHOMETRY_RERUNS, the old reports and rows kept); `morphometry covariates` joins the rows with each person's sex and age once both sets name one code stamp, each join checked against the earlier ones; reports carry aggregates alone.\n- [Notebooks](notebooks.md): drive files held together as a notebook, a private repository pointing at them by id and never moving them, with research, drafts and references.json from a citation manager's BibTeX or RIS export, citations into Word as tracked Word or Zotero fields (tools/docx_cite.py); tools/notebook.py and the notebook skill; Elsevier text and token private, never public; docs/research/notebooks.md.\n- [The Outlook digest](outlook-digest.md): progress and decisions as an Outlook actionable message answered with one press or a reply inside Outlook (tools/outlook_digest.py, sending nothing yet); Wayne State's Files consent covers no mail, so the owner's chosen route, self-sent from the owner's own mailbox, waits for C&IT's Mail.Send and Mail.Read consent; docs/research/outlook-digest.md.\n- [Profiling sessions](profiling-sessions.md): tools/profile.py reads a session's transcript and gives what it spent as counts alone, context call by call, tool calls, reads and re-reads, time, and a session's commits carry them since its last as a Symphony-Profile line, which collect sums per pull request into PROFILE.json, MCP tools folded to one name, and tools/profile_report.py writes the programme's report.md from its committed records; the research programme under docs/research/beyond-cooperation/ measures a way of working with it before changing it, names failures as MAST does, and no run that spends tokens starts without the owner's word.\n- [Protocol changes come from symphony-protocol](protocol-changes-come-from-symphony-protocol.md): the claim protocol is changed in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, whose sync pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` brings each release, once the owner has approved it, to OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template, while a repository made from the template takes each release tag by its own workflow, protocol.yml; never edit tools/sdlc_protocol, which scripts/check.sh holds to the hashes in tools/VENDORED and CI to the library's own files; the template vendors 0.5.0, merged in the library and waiting for the owner's approval, so until it is published the sync's 0.4.1 pull request is a downgrade to close, never to turn green.\n- [The research loop](research-loop.md): research.yml works the best-ranked unheld claims item every four hours with full command of grid work (the owner, 2026-10-24); RESEARCH_LOOP off stops it, RESEARCH_RUNS_PER_DAY caps it; the session can start no workflow, its grid requests run after it from a job whose token it never holds.\n- [Run the checks in a throwaway copy](run-the-checks-in-a-throwaway-copy.md): scripts/check.sh runs the lessons of docs/learn/ as commands in the working copy (tools/living_docs.py test); on 2026-10-20 it left the owner's clone with a blank git identity, on a branch claude/7 and with six stray items at the root, so run it in a scratch copy or let CI run it, and run the reading steps (check_docs, living_docs check, one test module, reproduce_table check, sources check) by hand in the clone.\n- [Runs are named by their code](runs-are-named-by-their-code.md): tools/run_code.py's digest of the files a run executes, not the commit, names flow runs, sweep states and launches, so a cut run carries on after a merge that changes none of them; a file a run newly reaches goes into its list or LEFT_OUT.\n- [SWE-bench runs from GitHub and PyPI](swe-bench-runs-from-github-and-pypi.md): the task repository SWE-bench/swe-bench-tasks, compiled by `swebench dataset build`, scores an instance where huggingface.co is unreachable; never --task-repo, which rebuilds the image; the images pull through mirror.gcr.io; OpenSpineConsortium/flowbench builds on it.\n- [Symphony versus competitors](symphony-versus-competitors.md): the comparison of 2026-10-16 is docs/research/symphony-versus-competitors.md (and a Claude Doc); its three claims are better by design and graded Inferred, no outcome is measured yet, and coordbench numbers stay out until the owner lifts the hold; the site's /symphony/compare.html sets the general powers beside familiar tools in seven kinds of work, and /symphony/use-cases.html gives the use cases (docs/research/symphony-compared-by-domain.md).\n- [The anomaly pack](the-anomaly-pack.md): stage 3 of the SPINEPS to FleXray route packs the selection as two whole-volume FleXray packages under the landing flexray_pack (schwingnet_spine for masks complete from C2 to the sacrum, schwingnet_spine_partial for the rest), every mask rewritten to the 28 canonical ids by tools/nifti_label.py, the existing relabelled SPINEPS copies through spineps_ids.py check --strict and never a segmenter run, countable measured per CT, uncounted and g3_excluded rows packed and trained on by nothing, the validation draw under data/splits/anomaly-validation.json, the admission before any byte and no HOLD (docs/research/spineps-anomaly-route.md, section 5).\n- [The control panel](the-control-panel.md): the site's /symphony/control/ drives a Symphony repository through GitHub's API as the signed-in person (the board, tasks, @claude, workers, pull requests, workflow runs) and /symphony/cost.html prices the work against the alternatives, unsorry first; Sign in with GitHub first (the App's user token through a stateless Worker), a pasted fine-grained token the fallback; it took no Claude token until the owner dropped that rule on 2026-10-18 for the Tokens page's Add a slot box, which saves a pasted token as an organization secret through the App; its assistant is an issue labelled assistant that claude.yml answers read-only in ten turns with .github/symphony/assistant.md appended, and the Ask Claude bar at the console's foot asks it and offers the panel actions its answer names as buttons the person presses (site #28, 2026-10-18; docs/research/ask-claude-bar.md); the research note is docs/research/control-panel.md.\n- [The drive organizer](drive-organizer.md): a whole drive read into a catalog on the branch drive-catalog, Claude proposing projects as epics whose tasks carry proposed and no todo until a person runs accept, which adds the folders to the sync, and the sync's run starts the tasks once the files are in; private repositories only, nothing scheduled; tools/file_digest.py reads any file as text; a session does it through the Microsoft 365 connector too (tools/drive_connector.py, the drive-organize skill), moving files only on the owner's go and undoably; Wayne State approved the Folders app for native Graph access on 2026-10-19, confirmed live 2026-10-20, so the drive is read only through Graph under the owner's sign-in and the connector walk is retired (docs/research/onedrive-direct-graph.md); docs/research/drive-organizer.md.\n- [The data catalog](the-data-catalog.md): the frame catalog of task #10 is a read-only versioned SQLite store (catalog-NNNNNN-<hash>.sqlite under versions/, 0444, VERSIONS.jsonl and CURRENT; unchanged bytes make no version) built from content-hashed shard manifests and data/registry/, with the person-keyed split under data/splits/; the data root lists sources alphabetically, tools/ingest.py turns a bank into a shard, split-check holds every version to the REFORMS split rule; images stay on the grid, Metaflow steps read a version by path and pass its hash; tools/catalog.py, docs/research/data-catalog.md, docs/research/dataset-organisation-at-scale.md.\n- [The dataset is obtained again from data/reproduce](the-dataset-is-obtained-again-from-data-reproduce.md): the manifest (211 sources) and the fetch code are byte-for-byte copies of spinesurg-ct-nnunet's data_platform at bf1432f with their SHA-256; docs/research/datasets-reproduce.md maps every registry record to its manifest records with acquisition, origin links, fetch command, checks and grid path (tools/reproduce_table.py); scripts/check.sh fails when page, map and copies disagree.\n- [The experiments panel](the-experiments-panel.md): chapter 35's figure from every recorded run under docs/research/ (the data input, every block spelled out with version 1 where a run named none, the loss by epoch, accuracy with intervals, the model, the reproducibility record, W&B), filters and a correlation view by block, and a Run a new combination panel whose request is a prefilled issue labelled experiment (or the Experiment form, or an `experiment:` line in the Ask box's answer), answered by experiment.yml with the command, the gate word `schwingnet-experiment-<issue>` and the owner's click and recorded on the branch experiments, where grid/gate.sh's word `experiment <issue>` runs it as one GPU job on the launched flow experiment under docs/research/experiments/PREREG.md; the artifact edition offers Run as me through the viewer's GitHub connector; `python3 tools/experiments_panel.py write` after recording a run, since the check fails on a stale page or form; the messages between a figure and the page that embeds it are docs/research/experiments-panel.md, section 4.\n- [The front door is one press](the-front-door-is-one-press.md): install the App, press Create on the form its Setup URL opens, merge; the run picks the repository by id, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise; the sweep is the fallback.\n- [The gate proves only what changed](the-gate-proves-only-what-changed.md): from beethoven-qa 9.0.0 gate.yml and the pre-push hook prove only the modules a change reaches since a base, and from 10.0.0 pyproject.toml is read by its keys, so an unproven entry added proves nothing (in CI the newest commit of main the gate passed on a push, in the hook where the branch left main), every module once a day; BEETHOVEN_QA_BASE=none proves all.\n- [The gold tier 3 CT dataset](gold3-ct.md): ENHANCE.PET, VerSe and TotalSegmentator's gold 3 CTs as one versioned store (tools/gold3_ct.py), with the private page Gold Tier 3 CT and its reader bench (artifacts/gold-tier-3-ct-xdgjjg/); since 2026-10-29 rule 3 admits every CT whose field shows C2's foot and the sacrum's top (tools/gold3_field.py): 1,738 gold 3: 1,558 ENHANCE.PET, 21 of VerSe's 374 series and 159 of TotalSegmentator's 1,939 subjects (5 on the CT's own extent the census read), 10 border cases, every row read from the releases; the grid census is the gate word `census gold3`; docs/research/gold3-ct/README.md; the completion's TotalSegmentator arm for C2 and the sacrum is the word `totalseg complete` (two jobs on the owner's count, the weights fetch and one GPU job, the owner's word of 2026-10-30 08:54 UTC).\n- [The grid runs a job through Beethoven](the-grid-runs-a-job-through-beethoven.md): .github/workflows/grid.yml runs one command on a WSU compute node through the runner chain that hpcworker's tools/grid_runner.sh serves as Slurm jobs (a private repository, labels self-hosted and grid, the output back as the job's log and an artifact); grid-gate.yml starts, reads or stops the chain over the ssh gate, a key whose forced command takes only those three words, which hpcworker's `grid_runner.sh --gate-setup` sets up in one command in the grid's shell; no Claude token goes there; docs/setup/grid.md has the owner's steps, docs/research/wsu-grid-and-github-actions.md the research.\n- [The organization map](the-organization-map.md): .github/symphony/organization.json, the curated record of how the organization's repositories depend on each other and touch (vendored libraries, reusable workflows, Apps, runners, data handed on) and the systems they reach, nodes with group, visibility and role, edges from, to, kind, what and by, held together by tools/organization_map.py check, drawn by the control panel's Organization tab beside what it reads live, changed by pull request when an interface changes; the epics, goals and blocked-by links of 2026-10-29 are docs/research/organization-map.md.\n- [The pipeline runs on Metaflow](the-pipeline-runs-on-metaflow.md): bank, catalog and sweeps are Metaflow flows that flows/launch.py starts, resuming the newest unfinished run of the same key first (the owner's rule of 2026-10-24); 02 and 03 hand every Slurm job to it.\n- [The plan ladder](the-plan-ladder.md): which GitHub and Claude plans unlock what, verified 2026-10-13 against docs/setup/free-or-paid.md; public repo means free Actions, the enforced gate and free scanning, a private one needs Pro or Team for the gate and an add-on for scanning; no payment method means a hard cap.\n- [The review record](the-review-record.md): every pull request marked ready, a person's or a session's, carries a section \"## Adversarial review\" in its body, held by tools/review_record.py in the required job check (drafts, bots, generated branches and pull requests opened before 2026-10-22 05:00 UTC excepted); CONTRIBUTING.md.\n- [The sample index](sample-index.md): grid/run-samples.sh builds samples.sqlite on the grid, every held file with its format, size, shape, DICOM header and sidecar rows and whether it is annotated, queried through the views sample, sample_meta and sample_header; only the aggregates in MANIFEST.md leave the grid; NHANES II is the large unannotated set for the screener; docs/research/data-manifest/README.md. The first run (2026-10-22, 140 groups, 7,008,826 files, 7.3 TB) is docs/research/data-manifest/MANIFEST.md.\n- [The size rule](the-size-rule.md): every fetch reads its exact size from its source before a byte lands (grid/fetch_size.py) and is refused whole when it would pass the CephFS quota of /rs/rs_grp_oschome after what the running fetches owe, with no floor (grid/size-rule.sh, the owner's rule of 2026-10-23); `bash ~/SchwingNet/grid/oschome-quota.sh`, the status word and wsuquota itself once grid/wsuquota.bash is sourced from ~/.bashrc print the room in wsuquota's layout; an attribute that is no count of 18 digits or fewer, or a use below the data root's, admits nothing, the volume's use then read by stat where the data root, a gigabyte or more on the volume's own mount, shows stat gives a folder's rbytes (#170), else as the sum of what its own entries hold, each read whole with no NUL and none a mount, as many as stat counts and the one that holds the data root no less than it (#172); every figure counted is read whole, owed before the room, the room twice a minute apart keeping the smaller, the second taking each of the volume's own entries at the larger of its two readings, a quota below the volume's, a data root outside it or a failed read admits nothing, and the tools stand in only under SIZE_RULE_STAND_INS=yes (#180); every quota above the volume up to the root of its mount counts too, the least room on the path binding, each folder's use read upwards from the volume's (#190), the readout showing each (#188); the bank word holds what its bank and shard may write as the set's admission while its job is live, twice the raw copy for png and dcm and six times for jpg, tif and bmp (#252); gdown, Kaggle and Redivis are sized too, and the X-ray chain's sets are each admitted in the fetch job with their banks, a landed set's bank room held for the bank job (grid/admit.sh, #163).\n- [The storage panel](the-storage-panel.md): chapter 22's interactive figure, built by tools/storage_panel.py from a committed snapshot of spine-datasets' feed (PLAN.json on its branch storage-feed; `fetch` refreshes it in docs.yml with the App's read token, `build` inlines it, `check` fails a stale page): the lab volume's and the home's use, every set with keep, remove, add and place, totals against the 20 TB target with the planner's refusals in words, and the selection it dispatches only through storage.yml, whose run opens a request pull request the owner merges; its host protocol is schwingnet.dispatch and schwingnet.read by postMessage, with the workflow's link and the string to paste where no host answers.\n- [The sync offers adopted repositories their files](the-sync-offers-adopted-repositories-their-files.md): sync.yml and tools/sync_adopted.py offer every adopted repository, on every installation, the library, the board's, tidy, metrics, Token and drive sync workflows with their modules as one pull request from symphony/sync, replacing only versions the template once held and adding only the Token and drive sync units.\n- [The synthesis keeps itself current](the-synthesis-keeps-itself-current.md): synthesis.yml sets the artifacts, notes and dataset records beside the stamp on every change with no Claude and keeps the synthesis issue saying what waits; its reads run on CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (the owner's choice of 2026-10-23), at most SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY a day (10, up to 50), into one pull request that waits for the owner's tap; what it writes is scrubbed, in the records' layout and the same on every hash seed.\n- [The X-ray fetch chain](the-xray-fetch-chain.md): grid/README.md is one paste that submits two afterany chains of reqp jobs, the X-ray sets (fetch, bank, ingest, catalog, split check) and the data platform's unfetched sources, each fetch held to the size rule; hf-get reads --include as a glob and an hf-token run that verified no file fails; CT-RATE and MIMIC-CXR-JPG are separate lines, the full NLST and PadChest's B2DROP share are fetch words on the owner's yes of 2026-10-22, and status opens with the space each set takes; a recipe field spelled \"to confirm\" stops its source; DICOM waits for pydicom.\n- [The token pool](the-token-pool.md): the branch pool holds each Claude slot's latest five-hour, seven-day and per-model (Fable) use from runs' rate_limit_event, pool.yml's probes and cswap via tokens.py usage, since a setup token cannot read the usage page; tools/pool.py pick is the auto mode over 32 slots, steered per slot (model, effort, fallback, resume, paused, id, owner) by SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_SETTINGS; issues a token worked carry token:<id> for the leaderboard; docs/research/claude-token-pool.md.\n- [The token wizard](the-token-wizard.md): python3 scripts/tokens.py stores each Claude account's subscription token as an Actions secret (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, the pool SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_1 to 8 with SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS) by running claude setup-token behind a pseudo-terminal that masks the token and piping it to gh, never shown or written, pasted only at a hidden prompt where no terminal can run claude; each slot's sign-in opens in a browser profile of its own through BROWSER, and cswap's account list names the slot, never its credentials; on Windows a copy in Credential Manager that show reads back, and share for a benchmark's repository; it also runs in a Codespace from the browser alone (scripts/codespace.sh); docs/setup/claude-tokens.md; the Tokens page's Add a Claude account mints on an Actions runner, the token leaving it only sealed (token.yml, tools/token_run.py).\n- [The textbook website](the-textbook-website.md): tools/textbook_site.py builds the Artifact Textbook into the members-only docs site (textbook/) whenever the owner runs Docs, never on a push or a schedule since 2026-10-25; public only after the filing, from an allowlist the owner approves (#196); each figure under docs/textbook/figures/ carries a JSON record of its source, shown with Explore and a Re-run issue that figure.yml answers (#215).\n- [The textbook artifact](the-textbook-artifact.md): the repository reaches claude.ai as a private front door artifact (tools/front_door.html, run by the published loader tools/front_door_loader.html, so a door merged to main needs no publish) reading main and the board live through the viewer's own GitHub connector, a custom connector with a GitHub App of the owner's; published from the owner's computer (`tools/textbook_publish.py local`, `--url` adopts a link), never from a thread; it replaced the staged reader of #294 on 2026-10-26; it serves only the owner's claude.ai organization and no reader browser-only (docs/research/reader-access.md).\n- [The textbook RAG](the-textbook-rag.md): Ask the book, a search over the textbook as blocks chosen by name (chunk, tokens, index, retrieve, answer, embed; src/schwingnet/rag.py, tools/rag.py ask, eval, check), its index built by the site and never committed, scored in the browser by a JS copy held to the bit by a shared fixture, and answered in the artifact through the viewer's own sample with no server and no key.\n- [The v1 flow](the-v1-flow.md): tools/flow.py runs the level stage locally or as Slurm jobs from a clean tree, writes RUN.json first and FINISHED.json last, after the ledger row and report, a start holding its folder by <run>/.lock, every number a ResultRow with its Wilson interval and stamp (tools/train.py, tools/evaluate.py); the run id follows from the code it runs (tools/run_code.py's row digest), the catalog and the configuration, and a run cut short resumes from its last epoch (#252); the dry run is docs/research/v1-demo/; on the grid it runs as the launched flow v1 under docs/research/v1-demo/PREREG.md; tools/ leaves sys.path before torch is imported, as tools/profile.py shadows the standard library.\n- [Training blocks](training-blocks.md): each part of training (normalise, augment, optimiser, schedule, sampler, loss weights, checkpoint metric, samples, architecture, denoise, and the prior, a frame's projected level map learnt by a training-only head, #228) is a block chosen with --block KEY=VALUE in tools/train.py and tools/flow.py, the names in src/schwingnet/blocks.py and the torch side in tools/train_blocks.py; none named is version 1's training to the bit; checkpoint.json records them and train.model_for rebuilds the model everywhere (#202, #207); --init starts a run from a parent checkpoint of the same architecture (#205); `tools/block_sweep.py parts`, `plan` and `ledger` are the optimisation loop, `check` and `cover` the smoke test of every combination (docs/research/experiment-framework.md); augment=strong is SyntheX's strong randomisation (#252).\n- [Training data licences](training-data-licences.md): every dataset classified by licence in data/licences.json, recipes under data/recipes/ cut a catalog for commercial or non-commercial networks, and every flow run writes PROVENANCE.json; only the owner loosens a class (tools/licences.py).\n- [VinDr is covered by HPR 2026-330](vindr-is-covered-by-hpr-2026-330.md): WSU IRB HPR 2026-330 (29 Sep 2026), the supplement to 2026-269, covers VinDr-SpineXR under its PhysioNet agreement; the spine-program handoff lines that call it pending or outside the determination are stale, and the owner holds permission and agreements for all the data (2026-10-14).\n- [Word revisions](word-revisions.md): a folder of Word documents is revised as tasks; tools/docx_review.py writes a review sheet and one task per document, queues new comments (papers.yml) and applies a worker's revisions as tracked changes; a connected Google Drive or OneDrive syncs both ways through drive-sync.yml (docs/research/drive-sync.md); several reviewers revise one document as authors of their own (docx_review.py panel, the lens-review skill, docs/research/lens-review.md).\n- [Workers and the coordinator](workers.md): one way of working from one person alone to several people, routines and workers in GitHub Actions, each a worker under its label worker:<name>; routines and workers in Actions are files under .github/symphony/workers/, added by the \"Add a worker\" form, and workers.yml runs those in Actions, dealt the open work once a run from one look; the coordinator releases idle claims and tells overlaps in the board's run, and plans epics in coordinator.yml."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/memory.md","type":"Project","content":"# Shared memory\n\nThe shared memory is docs/memory/, imported into every session through the line `@docs/memory/MEMORY.md` in CLAUDE.md. A claude.ai Project's own memory is private to the account that made it, so anything every account should know goes here instead.\n\n- One fact per file under docs/memory/, named in kebab-case (`how-this-repository-works.md`), with a one-line description as the file's first line.\n- One index line per fact in docs/memory/MEMORY.md, in the form `- [title](file.md): one line`. MEMORY.md stays under 200 lines and 25 KB; the limit is this project's own, because an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing from the index or the index names a file that is not there.\n- A thread that learns a fact, or finds one wrong, adds or corrects it in the same pull request as the change it learned from. Memory never travels alone and never waits for a later pull request.\n- Never a secret, never a token, never a cookie or a key. The checks look for the shapes of common secrets and refuse them.\n- What holds only for one piece of work (what was done today, what is left) is a checkpoint (.claude/skills/checkpoint), not a memory."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/writing.md","type":"Project","content":"# Writing\n\n- Plain prose that says why, in comments, commit messages, pull requests and documents alike. What the code does is read from the code.\n- Short functions; names that read as English.\n- No emoji in code, output or documents. ASCII and LF in everything under .claude/, docs/, tools/ and .github/, and in the dataset records under data/sources/, whose text the inventory page copies; prefer ASCII elsewhere.\n- No new dependencies: Python 3.12 or later with its standard library, plain bash, and GitHub's own actions and anthropics/claude-code-action, pinned to a major tag. A proposal to add one is an issue first.\n- Nothing refers to a file that does not exist.\n- In conversation, put a fact worth keeping on its own line tagged `Decision:` or `Constraint:`, so the checkpoint skill and `cswap checkpoint write` keep it word for word; the rest of a session is inferred and may be lost."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/research.md","type":"Project","content":"# Research accumulates in docs/research/\n\nWhat a session finds out is kept in the repository, under docs/research/, where every account and every later session reads it: a reply, a status line, a Claude Doc or an artifact is read once, and a claude.ai Project's files belong to one account. A handoff under docs/checkpoints/ says where the research is and never carries it, so research never waits for a checkpoint and is never lost with one.\n\n- One file or one folder per topic, named in kebab-case. A programme, a question measured over time, is a folder with a README that says where it stands; a finished study, comparison, deep-research run or fact-check is one file. docs/research/README.md lists both, one line each, and the line comes in the same pull request as the file.\n- Every file opens by naming its source: the thread's title, the date, and the link of the Claude Doc, artifact or upload it came from where there is one. A doc or artifact a session cannot read from its container is listed in the README by link and never written down from memory.\n- Research lands in the pull request of the work it came from or, when the research is the work, in a pull request of its own, before the thread's checkpoint. A note stays here once its question is settled; a pull request that settles a finding for good may move it to docs/history/, the longer record, and a fact every session should know becomes a memory (.claude/rules/memory.md) that points here.\n- ASCII and LF, as the checks hold everything under docs/ to, and plain prose that says why (.claude/rules/writing.md); no secret, no token, no transcript and no tool output, as docs/history/ holds too, and the checks refuse the shapes of common secrets and any .events.json."},{"path":"/home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/board.md","type":"Project","content":"# The board comes first\n\nBefore any change in this repository:\n\n1. Fetch: `git fetch origin`. The session-start hook does this once; do it again before you push.\n2. Read the board and docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (how work is claimed). The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow; read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is. The session-start hook prints it at the start of every session; docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout.\n3. Check the claim on the issue you mean to take: a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, means another worker has it, and a `worker:<name>` label that is not yours, with none that is, means the issue is that worker's: pick another. An issue the labels give to several workers, you among them, is each of theirs, and the first to claim it holds it. An issue the board's Given to workers section gives you comes first, then an unclaimed blocker, then the Queue; never take an epic, which the coordinator plans, nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nYou work here as a worker under a name of your own (docs/agents/WORKERS.md): in a person's session, the person's GitHub login unless the environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; in a routine or a worker in Actions, the name its prompt gives. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Claim with the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) before the first change to any other file, and open the draft pull request at once.\n\nNever push to a branch another worker claimed. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim; after a rebase, your own claim branch takes `git push --force-with-lease=claude/<issue>:<the tip you last pushed> origin claude/<issue>` (the claim skill's step 7), the branch named so that the push forces nothing else and the tip so that it never forces over a push made since, and nothing else ever does. When the board comments \"main moved\" on your pull request, bring the default branch in: rebase on it and push again, or merge it in. An issue labelled blocker is claimed like any other, and nobody fixes it in parallel.\n\nA hook enforces the push rules before they can be broken: tools/guard_push.py, wired in .claude/settings.json, refuses a push to the default or board branch, a plain --force anywhere, a + before a refspec, which forces that ref as --force does, the refspec : and patterns with *, which move branches wholesale as --all, --branches and --mirror do, a push to a name that reads as a claim and claims nothing, such as claude/03 (a claim branch is claude/ and the issue's number in one to nine ASCII digits with no leading zero, docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md, Claim), --force-with-lease outside your own claude/ branch, and, where the copy carries the fence (the template since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, and with its tools/guard_push.py where the copy's own predates symphony-template#69), --force-with-lease onto a claude/ branch whose claim file on origin names another worker, or with a branch that carries no claim file for the issue, since a lease after a fetch is the one push git itself would take onto another worker's claim. A refusal is the rule working, not an obstacle; the answer is a pull request, a rebase or another branch, never a way around the hook. Branch protection on GitHub (docs/setup/github.md) stands behind it."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# {{PROJECT_NAME}}\n\nThis repository is a Claude project kept as files: its instructions, its goal, its shared memory and its board live here, and Claude Code reads them in every session, local or cloud. The repository is the source of truth for every Claude session and every account; a claude.ai Project only links it, its own fields stay blank, and Claude Code loads this file and what it imports at the start of every session, a Project's thread and a cloud session opened on the repository alike.\n\n## Read first\n\n- The goal, the scope and who owns what, loaded into every session by this line: @docs/project.md\n- How work is claimed, done in the open and finished, loaded the same way: @docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n- Who works here: every session, routine and worker in GitHub Actions is a worker under a name of its own, labelled worker:<name> (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). A session's name is the GitHub login of the person it works for, unless its environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; a routine's or a worker in Actions' is the one its prompt gives.\n- The board: the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow from the open issues, the claim branches and the pull requests. It lives on another branch, which no import reaches: read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} only describes it.\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board, check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n\n## Shared memory\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nA fact worth keeping is one file under docs/memory/ with one index line in MEMORY.md, added in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- The board first (.claude/rules/board.md): fetch, read the board (STATE.md on the branch `board`) and PROTOCOL.md, check the claim; never push to another worker's branch; never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. A hook refuses the pushes this rule forbids, and /board (.claude/commands/board.md) reprints the whole board mid-session.\n- Memory (.claude/rules/memory.md): one fact per file, a kebab-case name, a one-line description first, one index line; never a secret.\n- Writing (.claude/rules/writing.md): plain prose that says why, short functions, names that read as English, no emoji, ASCII and LF, no new dependencies; tag a durable fact in conversation as Decision: or Constraint: so a checkpoint keeps it.\n- Research (.claude/rules/research.md): what a session finds out goes under docs/research/, one file or folder per topic that opens by naming its source, with a line in docs/research/README.md, in the same pull request as the work and before its checkpoint; a handoff under docs/checkpoints/ points to it and never carries it.\n- Native first (the owner, 2026-10-20): before building a new feature or approach, check whether GitHub, Microsoft 365 or the device already does it natively, starting with the permissions listed in docs/research/microsoft-365-permissions.md (One request, 2026-10-20) and docs/research/device-permissions-for-a-person-like-claude.md; build only what no native feature covers.\n- Skills: .claude/skills/claim, .claude/skills/remember and .claude/skills/checkpoint are the three procedures, in the order a piece of work meets them, and .claude/skills/handoff writes the page the next account resumes from when the hook says the weekly limit is at 90 percent or the owner asks; .claude/skills/drive-organize (a OneDrive sorted into projects, read through Microsoft Graph under the owner's own Microsoft sign-in), .claude/skills/lens-review (several reviewers on one Word document), .claude/skills/notebook (drive files held together as a notebook in a repository of its own, never moved) and .claude/skills/export-artifacts (one account's claude.ai artifacts copied into artifacts/ here and in SchwingNet, and shared on request) are run when the owner asks.\n- The block at the end of this file, between the cswap markers, is rewritten by the checkpoint skill and by `cswap checkpoint write`; do not edit it by hand.\n\n## Checks\n\nRun `bash scripts/check.sh` before opening or updating a pull request. CI runs the same script as the check named \"check\", and the default branch takes only pull requests that pass it. The same check fails a pull request marked ready whose body has no section `## Adversarial review` (tools/review_record.py, CONTRIBUTING.md), so write the review into the body before marking it ready.\n\n## Session checkpoint (kept by cswap)\n\nRead `.claude/session.md` at the start of a session: it holds the completed work, the current state and the remaining tasks. Tag durable facts in conversation as `Decision:` or `Constraint:` so the next `cswap checkpoint write` keeps them.\n\n### Durable decisions\n- Prefer GitHub's native, free features; no API billing: Claude in CI runs on a subscription OAuth token.\n- A protocol change is made in OpenSpineConsortium/sdlc-protocol and reaches every consumer by its sync once the owner approves its release: the release job waits in the environment release and tags nothing without that approval, and the sync then vendors the approved tag, never main's head (ecosystem.json, release.yml, sync.yml).\n- coordbench measures what CooperBench measures, as CooperBench 63b9d44 does, differing only with a stated reason (PARITY.md); model-judged measures run only on purpose, never with ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.\n- Crowdsourcing: each contributor works under their own Claude account and the project never holds anyone's credential; crowd mode (scripts/crowd.py, CLAUDE_CROWD) enforces it.\n- Field metrics: no hosted server; on by default, disclosed, one switch off; aggregates only, a repository only as a salted hash; the failure-mode judge runs client side.\n- Open core (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"publicize it to make the installation process minimal, maintaining updates trivial\"): the runtime, the protocol and the plugin are public, so setup is simple and copies never fall behind; the hosted parts, the control panel, the account pool and its switching, the QA reports and the analytics, are the paid SymphonyBoard App; the template's private status is no longer counted on, though until the runtime carries its files the template stays internal and is adopted through the public symphony-start, which holds no file of it; the protocol and the plugin stay MIT, the runtime may take a source-available licence (ELv2 or BSL) once a lawyer has read it, what was published under MIT stays MIT, and a paid product runs on each customer's own Claude subscription, never on pooled accounts.\n- The collector's salt is derived from the App's private key, with SDLC_METRICS_SALT as an optional override; rotating the key or changing the override gives every repository a new hash.\n- The metrics workflow runs at 03:43 UTC, before the collector's 04:17 read, and on a merge that changes the switch; it never replaces a branch metrics that holds other files and never runs in a fork.\n- Adoption's mode existing takes the protocol page from sdlc-protocol's spec/PROTOCOL.md at the vendored commit, and the App's tokens are minted account-first, so the public run log says nothing about a repository a stranger names.\n- A session merges the project's pull requests once their check is green, as the owner asked and the earlier sessions did; the rulesets require no review.\n- The files an adoption brings are offered under the MIT License, in sdlc-start's LICENSE; the adoption copies no LICENSE into anyone's repository.\n- An adoption's repository settings are applied by a second, setup-only App, Symphony Setup (Administration write, Metadata read, public, no webhook), which the adopter installs on the one repository for the one run and uninstalls afterwards; installing it is the consent; the main App holds Administration only for the control panel's Share tab, which invites a collaborator as the signed-in person (the owner's choice of 2026-10-18, the Apps' privacy statement version 17), and never uses it to change a setting.\n- The owner's own several Claude accounts may be coordinated from one orchestrator of theirs, workers as claude -p subprocesses each with one account's token; the owner decided this knowing the terms-of-service question, and it does not change the rule for contributors' credentials.\n- The Polyphony rename comes after the setup App: the App first, then the repositories, then the user-facing identifiers; the package sdlc_protocol and the SDLC_* variables may stay.\n- Installing the App is the request: the install's redirect (the App's Setup URL) lands on the form with the installation filled in and one press of Create is the request; the run picks the installation's newest repository with no adoption yet, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise, and the form assumes the person knows what they are doing; the sweep (sdlc-start's sweep.yml) is the fallback for an installation that never reaches the form; a webhook, if ever hosted, dispatches sweep.yml for one installation with a short grace and adds no logic.\n- A repository given by ids, from the sweep or the form, is never named on the public side (no log, run input, step output, job summary or issue): the run resolves and masks the name and answers \"your repository\"; a guessed installation number gets one answer whatever is wrong.\n- The product is Symphony; the rename is two tiers: tier one, merged, renames the product in prose and what a merge can carry (the form's title, the branch symphony/adopt, the switches .github/symphony-metrics and .github/symphony-adopt, the plugin symphony); tier two, six pull requests merged on 2026-10-11 once the owner had renamed the repositories and the Apps, flipping the repository names, the App slugs, the marketplace's name and the sync branch symphony-protocol/sync; the package sdlc_protocol, the SDLC_* variables, the VENDORED record's first token, the plugin folder plugins/sdlc, the schema ids, the check run name sdlc/protocol and the readers of the old names stay.\n- Symphony for Chrome links the new names, since it ships after the rename; the site links the current names with a flip pull request, since it is live now.\n- The Apps are named SymphonyBoard and Symphony Setup, slugs symphonyboard and symphony-setup, with no OpenSpine in the name: GitHub refuses an App name that matches an existing account (Symphony, Symph, Symph0ny) or another App (Symphony Dev); the main App's homepage is https://openspineconsortium.com/symphony/.\n- One press adopts one repository: the repository the form names, else the newest the installation reaches with no adoption yet (scripts/resolve.py, LIMIT 1), the person pressing Create again for the next; on 2026-10-11 one press from an installation on all of an account's repositories, with \"Merge the pull request for me\" ticked, merged the adoption into eight before the run was cancelled.\n- The extension's Migrate button and the post-Create banner carry the repository through GitHub's own install link, /installations/new/permissions?target_id=<owner id>&suggested_target_id=<owner id>&repository_ids[]=<repository id>&state=OWNER/NAME; GitHub returns state on the Setup URL, and the form's Repository field has the id state so it arrives filled in; the extension's note symphony.migrate (30 minutes) chains the Setup App's install after SymphonyBoard's from the form page and, on an installation's settings page reached from Migrate, offers the Setup App's install.\n- Both Apps are required on the form's two paths: the named path refuses while Symphony Setup is not installed on the repository, with its preselected install link; the installation path adopts only a repository the Setup App reaches; the sweep adopts without it.\n- The two GitHub Apps have one privacy statement, PRIVACY.md in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-start, rendered by the site at /symphony/apps-privacy.html and linked from the Apps' descriptions; the extension keeps its own (version 2 with the migrate note).\n- The Chrome Web Store release kit is its own private repository, OpenSpineConsortium/chrome-store-release-kit, called as a reusable workflow at main by every extension repository of the owner's; symphony-extension releases through it on each push to main, and the store upload waits on the five CWS_ secrets and CWS_ON_PUSH.\n- The main App's logo is GitHub's mark in the icon's warm white with the orange baton, the Setup App's a gear, the ring and tile unchanged; the owner accepted the risk under GitHub's logo policy.\n- An adopted repository carries the GitHub topic symphony: the adoption run sets it through the Setup App and scripts/setup-repo.sh adds it from a clone, and the extension reads the repository page's About sidebar for it, with no API call, to show \"Ready for Symphony\" (a link to the board) in place of \"Migrate to Symphony\"; a repository adopted before the topic existed gets it by hand under About.\n- The installs are chained on GitHub's side: SymphonyBoard's Setup URL is Symphony Setup's install page and Symphony Setup's Setup URL is the form, both with Redirect on update, so an install from the README or the App's page reaches both Apps and the form with the Setup App's number; the extension (1.3.0) steps from Symphony Setup's account picker to the page preselected for the repository its note names; whether GitHub carries the state through both hops is tested by the owner once.\n- The form's Kind and Merge are required dropdowns with a default, Auto and \"Merge the pull request for me\" preselected (an optional dropdown opens on \"None\", and GitHub's form schema gives a checkbox no default), the sharing opt-out the one box left; the kinds say what they are: the whole template makes the repository a Claude project, the protocol only adds the coordination protocol for the teammates' pull requests to existing code.\n- The extension opens the popup's links through the browser's own new-tab call, since Chrome opens a popup's plain link in the last ordinary window and a private window lost it; and its step to Symphony Setup's preselected page covers the permissions page GitHub lands on directly for an account with no organization.\n- The site's root is the lab page (what the lab is, the two features of one code base at the same time, the three aims, how the lab works, the products), with two figures drawn by tools/home_figures.py in a wide and a phone layout, and Beethoven is its own tab at /beethoven/ with its flow diagram and its children, between Symphony and Onboarding; below 900px the header opens its drawer and the figures take their phone layout.\n- From Claude Code, /symphony:new (symphony-marketplace 0.1.2) and docs/start/claude-code.md make a project: gh makes the repository, one preselected install link installs both Apps and lands on the form, the run merges, the Claude GitHub App goes on, and claude --cloud opens the session; a Project is made in claude.ai alone, belongs to one account and cannot be shared, so the repository is what is shared and another account joins with its own Project.\n- Beethoven 20.0.0 (2026-10-11, merged and live on 2026-10-12): an approved account works on every installation of the student's own; the access service keeps the ten most recently seen per account and sends the maintainer a notice with one Withdraw link and no Move link; one installation still serves one account; agreement 13, privacy statement 14.\n- A claude.ai Project holds one thing, the blank Symphony repository; after its first push two or more people and any number of sessions develop it through the same upstream, each on their own account and Project, as the template was designed to support.\n- No settings-as-code app (issue #18, closed 2026-10-11): settings come from Symphony Setup for one run, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables; docs/project.md records it under Out of scope.\n- A session merges any pull request once its checks are green, in Symphony's repositories, the site and the Beethoven repositories, on the owner's standing permission in perpetuity (2026-10-13: \"You have my permission in perpetuity to merge pr's\"), which replaces the word per pull request the site and Beethoven needed before and covers symphony-protocol's gate files; privacy pushes needed no ask already (\"all privacy pushes, im tired of approving them\"); while Symphony is in its development phase, with no users yet (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"you can also push releases without my permission and spine program\"), it covers spine-program's pull requests too, in place of its rule that only the owner merges there; approving a release stays the owner's, since no session can (Constraints).\n- The field metrics break failures out by work setting (symphony-template#49): each pull request that ended in the 30 days counts once, in one of eight settings (solo, one_session, one_person_sessions, several_people, several_people_and_sessions, undetermined, workflow_agents, automation), with its exposure (work_seconds, partners, landings_under, landings_before_opening, file_overlap) beside every failure count, and pairs at work at the same time counted by relation; a person is the pull request author's login and a session a whole line (Claude-Session, the claude.ai link, the footer, or Symphony-Session: <token> that a local or claude -p session writes); identities stay in the run's memory; the breakdown reads only after every existing key is measured and is left out when its reads would reach the board's reserve or GitHub fails one.\n- The Apps' privacy statement (symphony-start PRIVACY.md: version 3 of 2026-10-12 added what the work cost, version 4 of 2026-10-13 the Actions setting, version 5 of 2026-10-13 what the run reads to refuse a repository adopted already, its site page live since site#14) is the exhaustive list of what the collector keeps: a new shared key is listed there, and its site page is live, before the collector keeps it, and the template's change may follow in any order.\n- One way of working (symphony-protocol 0.4.0, symphony-template#56, 2026-10-12): whatever claims an issue is a worker labelled worker:<name>, a person's sessions by GitHub login or SYMPHONY_WORKER, a routine or a worker in GitHub Actions by a file under .github/symphony/workers/ added through the \"Add a worker\" form or by hand; account:, agent: and \"- Account:\" are still read; team mode and CLAUDE_TEAM, CLAUDE_WORKER and CLAUDE_MAX_ROUNDS are gone.\n- The coordinator lives in the repository: the board's own run releases idle claims (the labels off, then a comment with why, whose, what the branch holds and the plan, the labels put back when the comment fails, so a failed run is retried; symphony-protocol 0.4.1) and tells overlapping work once, on GITHUB_TOKEN; Claude in coordinator.yml plans epics into tasks for registered workers, settles blocked tasks, integrates finished epics and runs the coordinator prompt weekly; a coordinator routine is only for a repository with no Claude token.\n- What the work costs is recorded beside the work, with no routine, API key or server: a Symphony-Usage trailer on each session's commits and a symphony-usage-* artifact per Claude run in Actions; metrics.yml reads them (actions: read, at most 200 new a run, carried in the Actions cache) and writes economics and coordination, whose seven ratios are over 1 where parallel work did better (symphony-template#57; the collector keeps them, symphony-app#11).\n- One public runtime (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"i love this idea, especially if it would simplify the setup process\"): symphony-protocol, to be renamed symphony, holds all of Symphony's logic as a dispatcher workflow and a composite action that every adopted repository calls by a version through a few thin caller files (a whole-template copy from 145 files to about 38, a protocol-only one from 28 to 8); the background jobs follow v1 while the required check, the hooks and the Claude jobs run a reviewed pin; a release goes out only through an approved environment after a canary; it replaces the parked update route for copies, and claude-code-action's workflow validation and nested permissions are measured before it is built.\n- The update route for an existing copy is parked; the old test repositories are deleted and made again from scratch, to test the path a future user takes (the owner, 2026-10-13).\n- Symphony Setup and scripts/setup-repo.sh tick \"Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests\" in a whole-template adoption (symphony-start#36, symphony-template#59).\n- A protocol-only adoption brings the workers and the coordinator too, and registers a default worker unless a box opts out; the control panel is a site page with a small stateless sign-in service acting through the App's user token (the owner, 2026-10-13; not built yet).\n- Every pull request an autonomous worker makes passes a required QA gate with a report a person can read: two required checks pinned to GitHub Actions, check and qa, where qa runs from the default branch, reads the pull request only through the API and never runs its code, and folds in GitHub's own CodeQL, dependency review and secret scanning where the plan has them; beethoven-qa stays the Python design-by-contract lane (not built yet).\n- As few moving parts as possible: no server of ours; the Apps mint tokens with their webhooks off, and the webhook server, which neither manifest turns on, is to be deleted, since CI's protocol check covers it; every schedule is a fallback, never the only trigger.\n- The owner's accounts form one pool, one organization secret per account and a list of their names, picked by pure code in the library with records in the Actions cache, and then CooperBench runs on Symphony in coordbench's Actions, in an environment named bench (not built yet).\n- The protocol is checked by a validation harness like the jinja pilot, every interleaving of a few workers against an oracle written from the spec's text, with mutants proving the checks bite and a concolic lane run by hand (symphony-protocol validation/DESIGN.md); what it finds is fixed (the owner: \"if you find anything using those tests please fix them also\"): F1, F3, F5, F6, F10 and F11 in symphony-protocol 0.4.1, released and synced on 2026-10-13; for the rest the owner decided on 2026-10-13 the library fix on every finding, correctness over cost, no deadlock, no missed work, no orphaned worker, one pull request each in the order F7, F12, F4, F14, F9, F13, F2, F8, then the stuck claim, the last raising the version to 0.5.0 for the owner's approval; F7 (symphony-protocol#20) and F12 (#22) are on main and unreleased on 2026-10-14.\n- A copy runs the template's tests: tests/test_filled_copy.py fills copies, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, and runs their checks, so a test that holds only in the template fails in the template first (symphony-template#60, #61).\n- The plugin's push guard runs behind scripts/guard.sh, which refuses a command that may push when Python cannot run, since Claude Code reads a hook's failure other than exit 2 as no objection (symphony-marketplace#10, plugin 0.1.7, from spine-program's handoff).\n- Only people with write access queue work for a worker: the coordinator's tasks from an epic the owner marked keep todo, its tidy's proposals wait for the owner, and a todo anyone else adds starts no worker (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- GitHub stops a worker changing what steers workers (its own file, the prompts, .claude/, tools/, scripts/, .github/): the qa check runs from the default branch's copy, and code-owner review covers those paths (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- The Claude token in an organization secret is best one of an account nobody uses by hand, since the workers share its five-hour window; that is advice and never enforced, since some people have only one Claude account, whose workers then share its window with its owner (the owner, 2026-10-13).\n- At most 10 Claude runs a day per repository, the coordinator's included, set by a repository variable, and none while the default branch is red or after three failures in a row (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- Each release of symphony-protocol, and later of the runtime, waits for the owner's OK: its release job waits in the environment release, whose required reviewer is the owner, and the sync carries only a published release, from the commit that raised main to its version (2026-10-13, from 0.4.1, whose release the owner approved that day, the first). For the development phase the owner allowed releases without that OK (2026-10-13); since no session can approve or publish one, each still waits for the owner's click until the owner says, in the owner's own words, to switch the approval off in release.yml, a change that merges only while no release waits, since GitHub refuses GITHUB_TOKEN a tag at a commit whose workflow files differ from main's.\n- The owner's handoff of 2026-10-14 (docs/checkpoints/2026-10-14/HANDOFF.md) sets how the sessions work: one thread per item, at most three at once, the owner told as each lands; one pull request per change from the thread's own branch, bash scripts/check.sh first where the repository has it, an adversarial review before each substantive pull request, merge once green on the standing permission, never approve, publish or change a symphony-protocol release or its approval gate without the owner's own words, never push to or force-push a default branch, no API key or Claude credential anywhere, say exactly what is missing and stop, ask before anything marked the owner's, every manual step with its exact link and what to click.\n### Constraints\n- The cloud environment blocks cooperbench.com, arxiv.org, cursor.com and docs.github.com; CooperBench's paper and data come from the GitHub repository cooperbench/website.\n- The cloud environment withholds CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN; coordbench reads COORDBENCH_CLAUDE_TOKEN, each account its own.\n- sdlc-template is internal to its enterprise; outsiders need a public copy or sdlc-start.\n- sdlc-start and its Actions logs are public: nothing of the template may be committed or printed there.\n- The cloud session's proxy refuses the Actions variables and secrets API paths, and the auto-mode permission classifier can refuse an outward write (a label through gh api, a repository attached mid-session); the GitHub tools' pull request and merge calls were allowed.\n- A cloud session's tools reach only its own account's sessions, environments and routines; there is no cross-account messaging, and Remote Control drives a session on the owner's own computer.\n- A cloud session's container has 4 CPUs, 15 GB of memory and about 30 GB of free disk; Docker's daemon is installed and starts on demand; a background command stops after two hours.\n- A GitHub App's installation on an organization has one repository list: the install link opened with \"Only select repositories\" replaces it; a repository of the organization's is added under Configure, and the main App stays on \"All repositories\".\n- GitHub hosts no always-on compute a webhook can reach: Actions runs on repository events, a schedule or an API call with a token, and a webhook delivery carries none; a newly added schedule may wait long for its first run; the proxy refuses deleting a branch and the organization's installations endpoints.\n- The sweep never touches the organization's own repositories, since the main App is on \"All repositories\" there; a heredoc on a command also fed by a pipe takes the pipe's place as stdin, so a workflow writes data to a file and reads it by path.\n- The proxy refuses renaming a repository and creating one in the organization, and the GitHub tools cannot create one either (403); both are the owner's; openspineconsortium.com is blocked too, so the site is checked by its own build and a local render.\n- A restart of the session's worker keeps /home/user, the scratchpad and the finished agents' outputs but kills running agents and workflows.\n- Parallel Bash calls in a cloud session share one working directory, so a cd in one call can land another call's commands in the wrong repository; calls that depend on the working directory run one at a time, or use git -C and absolute paths.\n- github.com draws the repository header with Primer React: the action list is ul[data-testid=\"repo-header-actions\"], the Star button a[data-testid=\"star-button\"], the owner's id and login are octolytics meta tags, and #repository-container-header and ul.pagehead-actions are gone; github.com/new needs a login to read.\n- docs.github.com and dagshub.com are unreachable from the cloud session (DNS and the proxy); GitHub's documentation is read from the github/docs repository's content files on raw.githubusercontent.com, and github.com's HTML pages answer 403 to curl.\n- GitHub's install link takes target_id (what GitHub's own buttons send), suggested_target_id and repository_ids[] (the OAuth-to-App migration guide; up to 100; omitted means all repositories) and state, which GitHub returns on the Setup URL after an install; an issue form fills the field whose id matches a query parameter.\n- The GitHub tools take the session's scoped repository names, the old ones (sdlc-*), and gh api on a renamed repository redirects to a repositories/{id} path the proxy refuses; git pushes to the old names redirect fine; a reusable workflow in a private repository needs Actions access \"Accessible from repositories in the organization\", and a run that failed at workflow resolution cannot be re-run (403), only pushed again.\n- A cd in one of several parallel Bash calls resets the shell's working directory for the others (\"Shell cwd was reset\"): every parallel call uses absolute paths or git -C.\n- A Project on claude.ai/code belongs to one user and cannot be shared, is on Pro and Max only, runs its threads in parallel (200 new threads a day), and no API or CLI makes one; the only HTTP way to start a cloud session is a routine's fire endpoint; claude --cloud starts a session from a clone's remote branch and bundles the clone when the Claude GitHub App is not on the repository.\n- In a cloud session a cd inside a Bash call can be lost to the shell's working-directory reset, so a commit meant for one checkout lands in another: every git command takes -C with the absolute path; and the proxy refuses git push --delete, so a stray branch (claude/new-skill on symphony-template, 2026-10-11) is the owner's to delete.\n- The site's build reads the sibling Beethoven checkouts as checked out: build master against the extension's main through a worktree named by BEETHOVEN_REPO while a branch is checked out, or the branch's texts reach master.\n- The auto-mode permission classifier counts a merge that a repository's rules leave to the maintainer as an unrequested commit unless the owner's words ask for that merge, and it refused a read right after such merges.\n- A cloud session's GITHUB_TOKEN runs tools/metrics.py collect on a repository the proxy reaches under its current name (OpenSpineConsortium/sdlc-template-test: 33 calls on 2026-10-12).\n- beethoven-qa and beethoven-agents carry Claude Code hooks under .claude/; a session doing other work reads them as files and registers no repository root for them.\n- The PR subscription tool can warn that the Claude GitHub App is not installed on a repository and still deliver that pull request's events.\n- Python's urllib sends a redirected request every header of the first, Authorization included, to whatever host the redirect names, so a download from GitHub (an artifact's zip) is asked without following the redirect and the storage address is fetched with no token.\n- In a single-branch (shallow) clone a pushed branch has no remote-tracking ref, so a stop hook calls it unpushed: add the branch to remote.origin.fetch, fetch, and set its upstream.\n- The site's build needs beethoven-extension, beethoven-vscode and symphony-extension checked out beside it (attached read-only, no repository root registered), and the site has no CI; a build moves every page's stamp.\n- \"Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests\" cascades from the enterprise to the organization to the repository, and a 409 on the repository's setting means a policy above it; a cloud session cannot read or set the repository's workflow permissions.\n- /install-github-app needs gh signed in as the account that may install on the repository.\n- The owner installed the Claude GitHub App on all of the organization's repositories on 2026-10-13; the subscription tool may still say it is missing under a repository's old name and deliver its events anyway, so a session also reads a pull request's checks itself before acting on silence.\n- /usage's dollars are a list-price estimate, not a bill.\n- The front door's adopt-run job runs template code beside both Apps' keys; the runtime's design splits the staging into a job that names no secret.\n- Python 3.11 and later refuse int() of a string of more than 4,300 digits with ValueError, so every reader of digits someone else writes (a branch name, a marker, a trailer) bounds them first, in ASCII digits (claims.number_in, usage.count_in).\n- On the owner's Windows machine gh and git fetch stall about 11 s per GitHub call on DNS, so they run through WSL there, and python3 may be the Windows Store's stub, which exits 9009 for every script.\n- A cloud session can neither approve a deployment nor publish a release: GitHub's \"Review pending deployments\" needs deployments: write, which the session's token lacks (403, 2026-10-13), and the session's proxy refuses creating, editing or deleting a release (\"not permitted for this session type\"); a release is made by release.yml's GITHUB_TOKEN once the owner approves its run, or by the owner by hand.\n- After a session's approval and release calls were refused, the auto-mode check refused related reads too (a docs fetch, a fetch of the consumers, a search of the session's own notes) as a bypass of the release gate; a change to the gate needs the owner's explicit words first.\n- git does not pass GIT_CONFIG_COUNT to the receive-pack and upload-pack it starts for a local remote, and git 2.55's automatic maintenance runs detached and races a test's cleanup, so a test that makes repositories sets gc.auto=0, gc.autoDetach=false, maintenance.auto=false, maintenance.autoDetach=false and receive.autogc=false both in the environment and in each scratch repository's config (symphony-template#63, symphony-marketplace#12).\n- The code scanning alerts API answers a session 403, so a CodeQL finding is read by running the CodeQL bundle locally: codeql database create --language=actions, then database analyze with codeql/actions-queries:codeql-suites/actions-code-scanning.qls.\n- gh api reaches a renamed repository under its new name once add_repo has attached it with push access (symphony-protocol, 2026-10-13); the others stay reachable through the GitHub tools under their old names.\n- The GitHub tools' merge took an expectedHeadSha that was not the pull request's head and merged anyway (symphony-template#63, 2026-10-13): a sha is copied from git, never retyped, and main's tree is checked after a merge.\n- gh api through the proxy refuses a repository's collaborators, invitations and Actions workflow-permission paths and the organization's secrets, but the GitHub tools' list_repository_collaborators answers; branch deletion is refused by both the delete-ref call and git push --delete (403, 2026-10-14), so a session deletes branches only through the owner's own machine (Remote Control).\n- GitHub marks a draft pull request merged, not closed, when another pull request merges the draft's branch into the default branch (symphony-template#71 and #72 through #73, 2026-10-14), so such a draft needs no closing.\n- GitHub runs no pull_request workflow on a pull request that has a merge conflict, so a conflicted draft's head shows no required check, only what other events start (symphony-protocol#21, 2026-10-14); the conflict is resolved first.\n- git 2.55.0 on GitHub's runners prints a UTC date in %cI as ...Z where git 2.43 prints ...+00:00, so a test compares the parsed instant and not the string (symphony-protocol#24's canary test, 2026-10-14).\n- A cloud session cannot read a GitHub App's settings pages (an App's permissions and description, the organization's installations, the Actions box, the secrets pages), so each click there is given to the owner with its exact link (the handoff of 2026-10-14).\n- The grid reaches GitHub from a compute node (a Beethoven session cloned public repositories over HTTPS on 2026-10-14), and a private repository such as spine-program needs the student's own token, which Claude never handles."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/docs/project.md","type":"Project","content":"# {{PROJECT_NAME}}: goal and settings\n\nThis file is the project's goal and the settings every account's thin Project shares. It is the one place where the goal is written: CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session, and the claude.ai Projects carry no copy of it. It changes by pull request like everything else.\n\n## Goal\n\nNot yet written. One paragraph: what this project makes, for whom, and what done looks like. The owner writes it, or asks a session to, when ticking the \"Set up this project\" issue's box for this file, and merges it by pull request.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope: the areas this project owns, each in one line; docs/agents/OWNERSHIP.md maps them to workers.\n\nOut of scope, by decision: what this project does not do, each with the reason, so nobody proposes it again.\n\n- A settings-as-code app (github/safe-settings, or the Repository Settings App reading .github/settings.yml), decided no on 2026-10-11 (issue #18): it is a dependency with admin rights on every repository it covers, and a server of our own in one case, for settings that Symphony Setup applies for one run from the front door, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables for what an organization makes.\n\n## Repositories\n\n- {{REPO}}, this one: the shared plane for every account and every session; the default branch is {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}.\n- Other repositories this project reads or uses, four, each owned by the OpenSpineConsortium organization, whose owners merge to its default branch:\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, public: the claim protocol as a Python library with no dependencies (the board, the protocol check, team mode's clerk, the push guard's rules and the merge gate). It is vendored here under tools/sdlc_protocol, with tools/VENDORED recording the version, the commit and the sha256 of every copied file, and scripts/check.sh fails when the copy differs from that record, or, on CI, which clones the library, from the library's files at that commit. A change to the rules is made there, never here: once the owner has approved and published a release there that carries it, its sync workflow opens the pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template with that release's copy, and the template's check decides. A repository made from the template is not on the sync's list; its own .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each new release in instead, weekly, as a pull request from a branch of the same name, or as an issue linking that pull request where GitHub Actions may not open one. By hand it is `python3 <symphony-protocol>/scripts/vendor.py <this repository> --consumer OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template` and a pull request. Its ecosystem.json is the one list of which repository takes which modules, and its ECOSYSTEM.md, generated from that list, maps all five repositories.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/coordbench, public: the playground and benchmark that compares ways of coordinating Claude Code agents on CooperBench's tasks, this protocol among them; its sdlc arm runs on the library's merge gate, vendored under coordbench/vendor. It began as experiment/ on OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template's branch claude/10.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-marketplace, public: the Claude Code plugin marketplace with one plugin, symphony, which brings the skills, /board and the hooks to a repository not made from this template. .claude/settings.json names the marketplace and enables nothing, since this repository wires the same hooks itself.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-app, private: the GitHub App SymphonyBoard (slug symphonyboard). Its installation token lets template-init's first commit start the Checks and the Board, which a push with GITHUB_TOKEN cannot, and the symphony-protocol sync opens its pull requests in every consumer with it; its webhook server, where it is run, posts the protocol check as a check run of its own, sdlc/protocol.\n\n## Model and effort\n\nEvery account's Project uses the same model and the same effort, so the work reads the same whoever did it. Record the choice here and set it in each Project's settings when you make the Project (docs/setup/claude-project.md).\n\n- Model: not yet chosen.\n- Effort: not yet chosen.\n\n## Crowd mode\n\n- Crowd mode: off. To open the project to volunteers who each work on their own Claude account, the owner runs `python3 scripts/crowd.py setup` (docs/setup/crowdsourcing.md), whose pull request turns this line on with the decisions that come with it; from then on the required check holds the repository to them.\n\n## Owners\n\n- Repository owner: {{OWNER}}, who merges to {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}; .github/CODEOWNERS names {{CODE_OWNER}}, the owner's own login, or in an organization's copy the person who made the copy, because GitHub asks a user or a team for review and never an organization (docs/setup/github.md, section 7).\n- Workers: each person's sessions, under the person's GitHub login, and the routines and workers in GitHub Actions the registry .github/symphony/workers/ names (docs/agents/WORKERS.md); none registered yet. Each worker's label is worker:<name>, made when it first needs it.\n- The Claude token: the secret CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, from one account's `claude setup-token`, on whose plan @claude, the workers in Actions and the coordinator workflow run (docs/setup/claude-action.md); not set until the setup issue's second box is ticked."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md","type":"Project","content":"# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on {{REPO}}. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by .github/workflows/board.yml, which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or a worker in GitHub Actions, which .github/workflows/workers.yml runs on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike. docs/agents/WORKERS.md says how they share the repository.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session's environment sets `SYMPHONY_WORKER` to another; a person needs no file to work here. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. .github/symphony/workers/README.md lists the settings, and `bash scripts/check.sh` fails on any file that does not hold together (`python3 tools/workers.py check`). A worker is added with the \"Add a worker\" issue form, whose workflow .github/workflows/add-worker.yml opens the pull request that adds its file, or by adding the file by hand in a pull request of its own. A person may register too, with `\"runs\": \"session\"`, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take as Pick says.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done (Blockers).\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. Where the labels of several workers name one issue, it is theirs alone in the same way: given to each of them, and held by the first of them to claim it, whose Announce takes the others' labels off. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below). The board's Queue leaves an epic out, since nobody takes one whole.\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\ntemplate-init makes every label above but worker:<name>, six in all, when the repository is made, and scripts/setup-repo.sh makes them again from a clone. A worker's label is made when the worker first needs it: the claim skill makes it before putting it on an issue, and the coordinator when it gives a worker a task.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first. Else take a blocker, with or without todo, that the Blockers section lists, that nobody has claimed and that no worker's label gives to another, since a blocker comes before the Queue. Else take an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of. Of the blockers and the Queue, take only one in your area when your file under .github/symphony/workers/ gives you one; an issue that lists no files fits every area (Planning). Wherever an issue is listed, never take an epic, which the coordinator plans (Planning), nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link, on the template in docs/agents/claims/README.md. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue. The board reads two parts of the claim file: the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix (a line with nothing after it but markup or a note in parentheses names nobody: the board shows such a claim as unnamed, since the file is there to be corrected, while the clerk and a release, which must name a worker, take the one the issue's label names, as they do once the pull request has deleted the file), and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes its `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off every other worker's label, one an earlier claim left there or one that gave the issue to several workers, so that from the claim on the labels name the holder alone, as the claim file does; and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. GitHub opens one pull request at a time between the same two branches, so where one that someone who may not write opened from the branch stands in the way, which is no claim's (Rules that hold everywhere), close it, as anyone who may write can, and open your own. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, which it does in whichever run finds the branch behind or conflicting, once per commit of the default branch, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. The board reads a branch as behind where GitHub calls its pull request behind, as it does under a rule that requires an up-to-date branch, or, where GitHub's word is blocked or draft, which says only that something else stops the merge, a rule still holding the pull request or the draft itself, where the comparison of the branch with the default branch says so. A pull request GitHub calls clean, unstable or has_hooks, a draft among them, since GitHub gives a draft the word its branch has, is not held for being behind under the repository's rules, so it hears nothing however far the default branch has moved; one GitHub calls dirty, in conflict with the default branch, hears it. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch. Since a worker that has fetched holds a remote-tracking ref that matches origin, git itself would take its lease onto any claim, so before a lease onto `claude/N` the push guard reads the claim file `docs/agents/claims/N.md` on the branch the lease holds against (the commit the lease names, else origin's `claude/N` as the fetch left it) and on the branch pushed, each at its tip or, once the branch has deleted the file, where its history last added it, and refuses a lease that would take another worker's claim: one whose two files name different workers, or whose branch pushed carries none; a claim file git cannot show leaves the lease to GitHub's rules, as the guard leaves everything it cannot read (the template carries it since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy takes it once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, whose Given to workers heading 0.5.0 changes, since the copy's protocol.yml brings only tools/, and with the template's tools/guard_push.py where its own predates symphony-template#69, since an older one hands the guard no claim files).\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes `bash scripts/check.sh` and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `\"lands\": \"auto-merge\"`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. A worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, such as a cloud session whose `gh pr` calls are refused, keeps the claim file instead, pushes the finished work with `bash scripts/check.sh` passing, comments \"finished; ready to mark\" on its draft and stops, leaving the steps above to a person who can take them; until then its claim is not released (Release). Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself (docs/setup/github.md). An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: .github/workflows/tidy.yml deletes `claude/<issue>` once no open pull request uses it, and with it any `released/<issue>-<sha>` a released claim left (Release).\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. The board lists every open issue labelled blocker under Blockers, claimed or not, and a worker takes one there before the Queue, as Pick says. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it held waits for it, helps on the one pull request by review, or takes other work it does not stand in the way of (Pick).\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready says it is done (Finish). When a branch's last commit came, here and below, is when GitHub received the push that set its head, as the repository's activity records that push, so no worker's clock decides it; an entry that set the branch to another head, or deleted it, dates it too when it came after the run began, a push after the run read the branch, while an older one is stale and lists no push that set the head; the head's committer date stands in where the activity, read once more after a pause when the first read lists no push that set the head or GitHub gives it no answer that holds, still lists none, or where GitHub refuses to read it, since the push a run was started by is the one the activity is likeliest not to list yet, but never earlier than the newest entry either read listed for the branch, which the push that set the head came no sooner than. In the run that a push to the branch started, which the workflow's event names, a head no read dates is dated at the run's start instead, and so is one a read dates by an entry that set it more than a few minutes before the run began, an older push of the same head that the activity lists while it does not list that push yet, or that push itself where the run, queued behind others, began that much after it, for that run alone, since that push came just before the run, whatever the two reads list, so no claim is released in the run its own push started; the next run reads the activity again. The coordinator here is a step of the board's own run, in .github/workflows/board.yml, on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). Before it releases, it frees each reopened issue whose labels still speak of a claim that ended with its close (Finish), so the in-review that claim left finishes no claim, and a claim branch still standing for the issue is released in that same run once it has gone a day without a commit. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off, and puts the label todo on it when it carries none of todo, epic and blocker, so the Queue lists it again even when a worker's label alone gave it to the worker, while a blocker is listed under Blockers without it; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails at the labels, taking one off or putting todo on, puts back the labels it took off, even one whose taking off GitHub answered with an error, unless GitHub refused it with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of taking it off, since GitHub may have taken it off and a label put back that is still on changes nothing, and takes off the todo it put on, even one whose putting on GitHub answered with an error, since GitHub may have written it, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. A run whose comment fails reads the issue's comments, and the comment there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, makes the release whole. Where they list none and GitHub refused the comment, with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of writing anything, the run puts back the labels it took off and takes off the todo it put on, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. Where they list none after any other answer to the comment, a rate limit's refusal, a 429 or a 403 that names a rate limit or a wait in retry-after, on the last try or on one the run made the comment again after, which GitHub's documentation never says it gives in place of acting on the call, a server error, a redirect, an answer the run could not read or no answer at all, none of which says GitHub wrote nothing, and GitHub may list a comment it wrote only later, or where the run cannot read them, whatever GitHub answered the comment with, the run cannot tell whether a release comment stands, its own, an earlier run's or one someone else wrote: it leaves the labels as the release left them and counts no release of its own, so its page lists the claim as held and the branch keeps its name, since labels put back beside a comment that stands would hold the claim for good, the comment telling every later coordinator the claim is released and the label claimed telling the board it is held, and the next run, reading the comments, finds the release whole where a release comment that counts stands, or releases the claim again where none does, unless its holder pushed since, which keeps the claim, its labels as the release left them, naming no holder, until it finishes, a person puts them back or it goes a day without a commit and is released again, or a person put the label claimed back beside a comment that stands, which undoes that release (below), though a branch at a head a release already kept is released in that same run by its latest release comment, where one that counts stands, which counts for such a branch whenever it came, with no word on the issue of this run's release where GitHub wrote none. Where GitHub refuses the putting back too the labels are left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; so, running on every board run, it releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, todo put on as the coordinator puts it, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest. A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue as it lists any unclaimed one, in the Queue or, for a blocker, under Blockers, though until the same run has renamed the branch Pick reads the branch as another worker's and the clerk deals the issue to nobody (Planning), and .github/workflows/board.yml renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file (.claude/skills/claim says how). Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept, one a branch `released/<issue>-<short sha>` stands at, brings nothing new: its latest release comment counts for it whenever it came, before that push or after, so the board deletes it instead of renaming it, since its work is already kept, and the coordinator releases it only where the board counts no release of it, no release comment counting or the label claimed put back, once it has gone a day without a commit. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules, and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target (docs/setup/github.md).\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest, the work that began first, when GitHub says the push that made its branch came, or, for a pull request from a fork or from a branch it says nothing of, when the pull request was opened; work whose beginning nothing says, which can only be a claim with no pull request yet, counts as the newest, since a claim opens its draft at once (Work in the open), and the number breaks a tie. In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it; a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. The marker names each piece by its identity: a claim and its own pull requests are one piece, the issue's number, told on that issue, so opening a pull request tells nothing again, and a marker on one of the claim's own open pull requests counts as one on the issue; a pull request that is no claim's is a piece of its own, its number, told on itself. An issue's number names each claim on it in turn, so a marker tells a claim's work, or tells of it, only when it came no earlier than the push that made that claim's branch, as GitHub dates that push for the order above, and every marker counts for a claim whose push GitHub does not date. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude in .github/workflows/coordinator.yml on the repository's Claude token (docs/agents/WORKERS.md), plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it todo and `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue, labelled todo alone. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: tools/team.py. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run of .github/workflows/workers.yml do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes its own claim that needs work, one whose claim file names it, whatever the issue's labels say, or, where no claim file names a worker, one whose issue's label names it, a worker:<name> label before the older spellings and the first by name among labels of one spelling, as a release names whose the claim was, but never a claim that counts as released, which no worker carries and none takes until the board has renamed its branch (Pick, Release), nor a claim labelled blocked, whose holder waits on a peer or the coordinator (Labels) and so has nothing else to do; else the task it was given, one given to it alone first, and a task the labels of several workers give going to the first of them, in the order the registry lists the workers in Actions, with nothing else to do; else the next unclaimed blocker or queued issue, blockers first, whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area). The order is the team's: every worker the deal is for, in .github/workflows/workers.yml every registered worker in Actions, whether or not the run starts it, so a worker outside the run keeps its share, and two runs that look at one state deal no issue to two workers. The workers of one run are dealt from one look at the repository, taken once for the run and handed to each worker (`python3 tools/team.py deal`), since two workers looking for themselves at different moments, one while a peer's ready pull request is still clean and one after the default branch has moved past it, could be dealt one issue; a worker that asks alone (`python3 tools/team.py next`) is dealt by the same rule from its own look.\n\n## Locks\n\nThis section is off unless the switch says otherwise, and nothing above depends on it: the environment's `SYMPHONY_LOCKS`, else the file `.github/symphony-locks`, says off, advise or enforce, and anything else is off (sdlc_protocol.locks.mode_of).\n\nA claim locks an issue; a lock is how a claim says what it works on, so that two claims never edit one function, one section or one resource with no merge at once, the rule a coordinator otherwise applies by hand by naming one owner. A key names what is locked: `path` the whole file; `path#name` one symbol of it, a Python function or class or `Class.method`, read with the standard library's ast from its first decorator to its last line, or a Markdown section by its headings from the top, as `Title > Section`; `path#<top>` everything in the file outside its named symbols; and `scheme:id`, such as `doc:a1cb9900`, a resource outside git, always whole. Any other file is locked whole. Two keys meet when they name one resource and either is the whole of it, or they name one symbol, or one symbol holds the other. A lock is a write or a read: two entries conflict when their keys meet, at least one is a write, and they belong to different claims. A claim's write on a Python symbol comes with reads on what that symbol's code names directly, the file's other symbols, its class's methods through self and names taken from the repository's own modules by a module-level `from ... import` (sdlc_protocol.locks.plan), so a change to what another claim builds on waits; what a reference does not show, a dynamic call or a dependency through data, is the required check's to catch on the merged result.\n\nThe locks are entries in one table, LOCKS.json on the branch `locks`, which moves only by fast-forward pushes and is never forced or deleted: a worker reads the table, decides on the tip it read, and pushes one commit on that tip, which git refuses once the tip has moved, so the push is a compare-and-swap, and a refused worker reads again and decides again (sdlc_protocol.adapters.git_locks.change). The compare-and-swap is the clients' alone until a ruleset on `locks` blocks forced pushes and deletions, which the switch needs before enforce: the push guard refuses a forced push anywhere but does not yet refuse deleting `locks`, which would drop every lock. Locks taken at the claim are pushed with the claim branch in one atomic push, `git push --atomic origin locks claude/<n>`, the claim branch under a lease that expects no branch at all, so a worker gets the issue and every lock it asked for, or nothing, and never adds commits to a claim already there; a claim branch the remote already has means the issue is held, and any other refusal that the table moved. A claim takes locks later only once its branch is on origin, since until then its locks would count as ended. A worker never waits while it holds locks: refused at the claim, it picks other work; refused when it asks for more mid-work, it finishes what it holds and files the rest as an issue of its own that names the holder's. Nobody waiting while holding means no deadlock.\n\nA lock lives while its claim does: the branch `claude/<n>` is on origin and its head is not yet in the default branch, read by the branch and not by a commit, since a worker may rebase its own claim. The board's run also ends the locks of a claim whose issue is closed or whose release it counts, which git alone cannot see. A lock that has ended is dropped, by the board's run or by any worker in the commit that takes its key, and never comes back: a claim whose release is undone takes again what it held, and may be refused. A new claim of the same issue inherits what is left under its number.\n\nGit cannot keep an edit inside its grant, so a check holds each change to the locks (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks verify`): the change from the merge base, hunk by hunk on the base version, gives the keys it touches, an insertion at a symbol's edge touching the symbol and `<top>` both, since a three-way merge conflicts on edits that only abut. A touched key that another live claim holds is a problem for any pull request; for a claim's, so is a touched key it holds no write on, except in a file the change adds. At advise the check prints its problems, at enforce it fails on them. The table is trusted to everyone who may write the repository, as the claim branches and the board are.\n\nThe same reading tells a change whether it must bring the default branch in (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks overlap --base origin/main`), with no lock table and for any pull request, a claim's or not: what the default branch changed since the change's fork from it, with the reads its edited Python symbols take, is set beside what the change edits, with its own reads, and the change brings the default branch in only where the two meet with a write on either side, the default branch having edited what the change edits or builds on, or built on what the change edits. A Python file the change writes whole, or outside its symbols, reads what the whole file depends on, since such an edit builds on whatever the file does. Where they do not meet, the change lands as it is, and what a reference does not show is caught by the default branch's own check after the merge, as the required check on a pull request runs on a test merge made when it last ran and never at the merge itself; a repository that runs it leaves off GitHub's rule that a branch be up to date before it merges, which would ask every change to bring the default branch in whatever it changed. Judged at GitHub's test merge, the checkout a pull_request event gives, it reads the branch's own side, and in a shallow clone it deepens until it finds the fork. It obeys the same switch: at advise it prints the meetings, at enforce it fails on them.\n\nHere the switch is .github/symphony-locks, at advise since 2026-10-20 (the owner's word that the rule requiring an up-to-date branch be off, docs/setup/github.md), and each `python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks` above is `python3 tools/locks.py`, the shim over the library vendored under tools/: .github/workflows/ci.yml runs `python3 tools/locks.py overlap` against the branch the pull request is onto in every pull request's own run, as the job overlap, and .github/workflows/overlap.yml asks every open pull request again whenever {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} moves, writing the answer on its head as the check run overlap (tools/overlap.py), so that a ruleset requiring that check, once the switch says enforce, holds only the pull requests that must bring {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} in. The claim skill takes no locks at the claim yet, and the ruleset on the branch locks that enforce needs is not applied yet.\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard, tools/guard_push.py, wired as a hook in .claude/settings.json, refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it (docs/setup/github.md).\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from (.claude/rules/memory.md).\n- `bash scripts/check.sh` passes before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (tools/check_protocol.py, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in this repository, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it.\n- A claim and its pull request live in this repository, so a pull request from a fork is no claim's, whatever its branch is called: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename, which closes the claim's own pull request, leaves it open.\n- A claim's pull request is opened by the repository's owner, a member of its organization, a collaborator or anyone else who may write to the repository, or by a GitHub App, which opens one only where it is installed, as the workers in Actions open theirs; a pull request anyone else opens from a claim's branch, as GitHub's documentation says anyone who may read the repository can, is no claim's either: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename closes it with the claim's own, since GitHub closes each open pull request whose branch it renames. Where GitHub will not answer whether someone may write, a pull request they opened stays the claim's, and a release comment or an overlap marker they wrote counts for nothing, so a lost answer never frees a finished claim and never keeps work from being told."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/docs/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"Project","content":"# Memory index\n\nOne line per fact under docs/memory/: a dash, the fact's title as a link to its file, a colon and one line saying what it is. CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session; a fact's own file is read when its line says it matters. Add or correct a fact with the remember skill (.claude/skills/remember): one file, one line here, in the same pull request as the change it came from. This index stays under 200 lines and 25 KB, a limit of this project's own: an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing here or a line names a file that is not there.\n\n- [A copy runs the template's tests](a-copy-runs-the-templates-tests.md): every copy's required check runs the template's tests over the filled files, sharing perhaps off; tests/test_filled_copy.py runs a filled copy's check, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, so a test that holds only in the template fails there first.\n- [A new repository starts with the owner's commit](a-new-repository-starts-with-the-owners-commit.md): the push guard refuses a push to main in any repository a session's git touches, so the owner creates a new repository with \"Add a README\" ticked, or adds one from its empty page, and sessions bring everything else by pull request.\n- [A Project with several repositories runs no hooks](a-project-with-several-repositories-runs-no-hooks.md): GitHub's ruleset, the claim lock, the required check, /board and the claim skill still hold; the push guard, the board at session start and the Symphony-Usage trailer do not, so keep one repository per Project.\n- [A template copies no settings](a-template-copies-no-settings.md): the SymphonyBoard App applies a copy's GitHub side on its own (.github/workflows/settings.yml runs scripts/setup-repo.sh with the App's token on template-init's push, a ruleset change and by hand), the owner's rule of 2026-10-20; Symphony Setup and setup-repo.sh by hand are only fallbacks, scripts/crowd.py setup for a crowd repository.\n- [Agents](agents.md): agents are Claude Code subagent files under .claude/agents/ with .github/symphony/agents.json, imported by pull request (tools/agents.py, agents.yml), run in sessions, Actions and as workers on the tokens accounts.json gives their names, and measured as they are by coordbench --agent-file and a flowbench stage's agent; docs/agents/AGENTS.md.\n- [Artifacts are kept as files](artifacts-are-kept-as-files.md): the artifacts in the claude.ai gallery of each of the owner's accounts live in artifacts/ of their repository, less what the export's screen holds back for the owner (Symphony and Beethoven here, SchwingNet and spine work in SchwingNet), a page as index.html with its files, a Docs document as index.md, one row each in artifacts/README.md; each new artifact and each other account's are added the same way, an account's all at once by /export-artifacts, and artifacts-grid.yml keeps both folders in ~/claude-artifacts on the grid for every account (the owner, 2026-10-22).\n- [beethoven-qa in a Symphony repository](beethoven-qa-in-a-symphony-repository.md): a repository made from the template carries beethoven-qa 6.0.0 or later, the newest 10.0.0 (the gate proves only what a change reaches), beside Symphony (the claim protocol on one side, the contracts gate and code standards on the other, a draft with a claim file satisfying both); the kit hook is beethoven-qa-session-start.sh since the old name collided with the board hook; check.sh runs the copy's vendor-check only under pixi; the steps are docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md.\n- [Beethoven's worker and Claude projects](beethoven-worker-and-claude-projects.md): a project reaches the worker only through its own Work locally, never through the worker's link, so Beethoven 21.0.0 (beethoven-extension#17) opens the project's page named in Settings; the research is docs/research/beethoven-worker-opening.md, with what is not yet measured (the successor's resume, --spawn worktree); a merge to beethoven-extension's main can be a Chrome Web Store release (CWS_ON_PUSH).\n- [CI runs Python 3.12](ci-python-is-3-12.md): the checks run on GitHub's 3.12, so tools and tests use no API newer than that; test with python3.12 where it exists.\n- [Claim branches push by name](claim-branches-push-by-name.md): a claim branch is made with --no-track, a clone made for one branch is told to fetch every branch, every push names its branch and the lease the tip pushed, since git's push.default, a single-branch clone and a lease after a fetch each sent a push somewhere the push guard cannot see.\n- [@claude runs on the main account's subscription](claude-mentions-run-on-the-main-accounts-subscription.md): mentions run .github/workflows/claude.yml on the token from `claude setup-token`, spending plan limits, never an API key; in crowd mode it never runs.\n- [Code owners are users or teams](code-owners-are-users-or-teams.md): GitHub never asks an organization for review, so template-init writes the owner, or in an organization's copy the person who made it, into .github/CODEOWNERS; check with the codeowners/errors API.\n- [Crowd mode](crowd-mode.md): scripts/crowd.py sets a repository up for volunteers on their own Claude accounts; the variable CLAUDE_CROWD, the required check's crowd guard and queue.yml then keep the Claude workflows off, Claude secrets out, checkpoints out of commits and the queue to maintainers.\n- [Field metrics](field-metrics.md): .github/workflows/metrics.yml writes aggregates only (no name, title or text) to the branch metrics with GITHUB_TOKEN; a maintainer's own claude -p adds App F counts; a breakdown by work setting (one person's several sessions apart from several people) and in pairs; what the work cost, from sessions' Symphony-Usage trailers and the Claude workflows' usage artifacts, and seven alone-versus-parallel ratios, over 1 where parallel did better; PROFILE.json beside it sums the Symphony-Profile lines and the runs' profiles the same ways, unread by the collector; the switch is .github/symphony-metrics or SDLC_METRICS=off; the release marker and the board's words it counts.\n- [Flat-rate subscriptions come first](flat-rate-subscription-first.md): every feature runs entirely on flat-rate subscription accounts, and an allowed Claude API key is used only when a setting puts it ahead of the subscription (the owner, 2026-10-25); no key is ever pasted: a credential a service makes at sign-in comes from a one-click sign-in the owner's own app completes, and only a value no sign-in can make is an encrypted GitHub secret, under secret scanning and push protection, carried to the grid by the key route.\n- [Handoff at the weekly limit](handoff-at-the-weekly-limit.md): at 90 percent of the weekly window the hooks of tools/handoff.py tell a session once to write the page the next account resumes from (the handoff skill); the status line and the grid's files are the only sensors, so a claude.ai cloud session hands off on the person's word; docs/research/automatic-handoff.md.\n- [How this repository works](how-this-repository-works.md): the repository is the shared plane for every Claude account and session; what lives where.\n- [Issue dependency graphs](issue-dependency-graphs.md): GitHub holds the epic hierarchy and the \"blocked by\" dependencies natively and draws no graph, Linear draws none at issue level and Jira only on Premium; the recommendation of 2026-10-17, awaiting the owner's choices, is native blocked_by written by the coordinator and a Mermaid section drawn by the board, with Linear as an optional one-way tier (docs/research/control-board-graph.md).\n- [Living docs](living-docs.md): the lessons and their Colab notebooks live in the public symphony-protocol (lessons/, notebooks/, scripts/lessons.py), chosen by the owner on 2026-10-20 over an export from here, and are changed there; here every module needs a docstring for the Reference page, and docs.yml, run only on the owner's call since 2026-10-25, publishes the site where Pages deploys from Actions and SYMPHONY_DOCS is pages, both set by scripts/setup-repo.sh (members only here, the owner's choice of 2026-10-20).\n- [Notebooks](notebooks.md): drive files held together as a notebook, a private repository pointing at them by id and never moving them, with research, drafts and references.json from a citation manager's BibTeX or RIS export, citations into Word as tracked Word or Zotero fields (tools/docx_cite.py); tools/notebook.py and the notebook skill; Elsevier text and token private, never public; docs/research/notebooks.md.\n- [One API key, for AI Scientist alone](one-api-key-for-ai-scientist.md): the owner allowed one Claude Console key on 2026-10-25, for AI Scientist alone, paid from the monthly Console credits with no cap, made by Claude Code's own Console sign-in and never pasted, and used only when a setting puts it ahead of the subscription; every other no-key rule stands, and SchwingNet's \"usage credits\" means a subscription's extra usage; the plan and the owner's decisions are docs/research/ai-scientist-pro-plan.md.\n- [The Outlook digest](outlook-digest.md): the digest runs on GitHub alone (digest.yml, tools/github_digest.py): a daily comment mentioning the recipient, which GitHub emails, answered by replying with a line such as \"12 Accept\"; the Outlook card with one-press buttons (tools/outlook_digest.py) waits on Wayne State's mail consent; docs/research/outlook-digest.md.\n- [Polished papers over a hidden raw layer](polished-papers-over-a-hidden-raw-layer.md): the owner's standing brief of 2026-10-25 for AI Scientist Pro and future sessions: readers get a polished, abridged, themed paper, dense with references, drawn in the field's figures and argued definitively from recorded runs, with no links out to raw artifacts; the textbook stays whole behind it, each result tied to its experiment's CLAIM-compliant ledger for one-click reproduction.\n- [Profiling sessions](profiling-sessions.md): tools/profile.py reads a session's transcript and gives what it spent as counts alone, context call by call, tool calls, reads and re-reads, time, and a session's commits carry them since its last as a Symphony-Profile line, which collect sums per pull request into PROFILE.json, MCP tools folded to one name, and tools/profile_report.py writes the programme's report.md from its committed records; the research programme under docs/research/beyond-cooperation/ measures a way of working with it before changing it, names failures as MAST does, and no run that spends tokens starts without the owner's word.\n- [Protocol changes come from symphony-protocol](protocol-changes-come-from-symphony-protocol.md): the claim protocol is changed in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, where the merge that raises the version is the release unless Developer mode is on; its sync pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` brings each published release to OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template, while a repository made from the template takes each release tag by its own workflow, protocol.yml, whose pull request waits for the owner where more checks are required; never edit tools/sdlc_protocol, which scripts/check.sh holds to the hashes in tools/VENDORED and CI to the library's own files; a sync below VENDORED's version is a downgrade to close.\n- [Stale-branch statistics](stale-branch-statistics.md): tools/staleness.py counts, from GitHub's history, each pull request's catch-ups of the default branch, its check runs redone on them and how long it sat green but stale; staleness.yml records every merge on the branch staleness with a snapshot of open pull requests and the up-to-date rule's state; docs/research/stale-branch-failures.md.\n- [Subagent token figures are context, not spend](subagent-token-figures-are-context-not-spend.md): the tokens the session's tools report for a subagent or a workflow are each agent's context at its last turn; what a step spent is summed per message id from its agents' transcripts, tokens written and read with the cached share, as tools/profile.py counts a session; docs/research/skeptic-reads-and-lens-reviews.md.\n- [SWE-bench runs from GitHub and PyPI](swe-bench-runs-from-github-and-pypi.md): the task repository SWE-bench/swe-bench-tasks, compiled by `swebench dataset build`, scores an instance where huggingface.co is unreachable; never --task-repo, which rebuilds the image; the images pull through mirror.gcr.io; OpenSpineConsortium/flowbench builds on it.\n- [Symphony versus competitors](symphony-versus-competitors.md): the comparison of 2026-10-16 is docs/research/symphony-versus-competitors.md (and a Claude Doc); its three claims are better by design and graded Inferred, no outcome is measured yet, and coordbench numbers stay out until the owner lifts the hold; the site's /symphony/compare.html sets the general powers beside familiar tools in seven kinds of work, and /symphony/use-cases.html gives the use cases (docs/research/symphony-compared-by-domain.md).\n- [The check runs in parts](the-check-runs-in-parts.md): ci.yml's job parts runs check.sh without its tests and the tests in four parts by tests/durations.json (tools/test_shard.py), side by side, and the required job check waits for them; re-measure with `python3 tools/test_shard.py measure` when a slow test lands.\n- [The control panel](the-control-panel.md): the site's /symphony/control/ drives a Symphony repository through GitHub's API as the signed-in person (the board, tasks, @claude, workers, pull requests, workflow runs) and /symphony/cost.html prices the work against the alternatives, unsorry first; Sign in with GitHub first (the App's user token through a stateless Worker), a pasted fine-grained token the fallback; it took no Claude token until the owner dropped that rule on 2026-10-18 for the Tokens page's Add a slot box, which saves a pasted token as an organization secret through the App; its assistant is an issue labelled assistant that claude.yml answers read-only in ten turns with .github/symphony/assistant.md appended, and the floating Claude button at the top right asks it (or opens Add Token where no Claude token is set) and offers the panel actions its answer names as buttons the person presses (site #28, 2026-10-18; docs/research/ask-claude-bar.md; the button since 2026-10-20, docs/research/claude-drives-the-panel.md, with the plan for Claude driving the panel by named actions and a command-line tool, parked as a future feature); the research note is docs/research/control-panel.md.\n- [The drive organizer](drive-organizer.md): a whole drive read into a catalog on the branch drive-catalog, Claude proposing projects as epics whose tasks carry proposed and no todo until a person runs accept, which adds the folders to the sync, and the sync's run starts the tasks once the files are in; private repositories only, nothing scheduled; tools/file_digest.py reads any file as text; a session does it through the Microsoft 365 connector too (tools/drive_connector.py, the drive-organize skill), moving files only on the owner's go and undoably; Wayne State approved the Folders app for native Graph access on 2026-10-19, confirmed live 2026-10-20, so the drive is read only through Graph under the owner's sign-in and the connector walk is retired (docs/research/onedrive-direct-graph.md); docs/research/drive-organizer.md.\n- [The front door is one press](the-front-door-is-one-press.md): install the App, press Create on the form its Setup URL opens, merge; the run picks the repository by id, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise; the sweep is the fallback.\n- [The grid runs a job through Beethoven](the-grid-runs-a-job-through-beethoven.md): .github/workflows/grid.yml runs one command on a WSU compute node through the runner chain that hpcworker's tools/grid_runner.sh serves as Slurm jobs (a private repository, labels self-hosted and grid, the output back as the job's log and an artifact); grid-gate.yml starts, reads or stops the chain over the ssh gate, a key whose forced command takes only those three words, which hpcworker's `grid_runner.sh --gate-setup` sets up in one command in the grid's shell; fixed job words through grid-accounts.yml's action job (update, the spine-datasets promoter, SchwingNet's own grid/gate.sh words) so a session runs grid commands in the owner's place; no Claude token goes there; docs/setup/grid.md has the owner's steps, docs/research/wsu-grid-and-github-actions.md the research.\n- [The overlap check](the-overlap-check.md): the rule requiring an up-to-date branch is off everywhere (the owner, 2026-10-20); `python3 tools/locks.py overlap` asks a pull request to bring the default branch in only where what the default branch changed meets what it edits or builds on, run by ci.yml on each pull request and by overlap.yml on every open one when the default branch moves, as the check run overlap; the switch is .github/symphony-locks, at advise.\n- [The plan ladder](the-plan-ladder.md): which GitHub and Claude plans unlock what, verified 2026-10-13 against docs/setup/free-or-paid.md; public repo means free Actions, the enforced gate and free scanning, a private one needs Pro or Team for the gate and an add-on for scanning; no payment method means a hard cap.\n- [The review record](the-review-record.md): every pull request marked ready, a person's or a session's, carries a section \"## Adversarial review\" in its body, held by tools/review_record.py in the required job check (drafts, bots and pull requests opened before 2026-10-22 05:00 UTC excepted); CONTRIBUTING.md; docs/research/same-ci-for-every-contributor.md.\n- [The sync offers adopted repositories their files](the-sync-offers-adopted-repositories-their-files.md): sync.yml and tools/sync_adopted.py offer every adopted repository, on every installation, the library, the board's, tidy, metrics, Token and drive sync workflows with their modules as one pull request from symphony/sync, replacing only versions the template once held and adding only the Token and drive sync units.\n- [The token pool](the-token-pool.md): the branch pool holds each Claude slot's latest five-hour, seven-day and per-model (Fable) use from runs' rate_limit_event, pool.yml's probes and cswap via tokens.py usage, since a setup token cannot read the usage page; tools/pool.py pick is the auto mode over 32 slots, steered per slot (model, effort, fallback, resume, paused, id, owner) by SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_SETTINGS; issues a token worked carry token:<id> for the leaderboard; docs/research/claude-token-pool.md.\n- [The token wizard](the-token-wizard.md): python3 scripts/tokens.py stores each Claude account's subscription token as an Actions secret (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, the pool SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_1 to 8 with SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS) by running claude setup-token behind a pseudo-terminal that masks the token and piping it to gh, never shown or written, pasted only at a hidden prompt where no terminal can run claude; each slot's sign-in opens in a browser profile of its own through BROWSER, and cswap's account list names the slot, never its credentials; on Windows a copy in Credential Manager that show reads back, and share for a benchmark's repository; it also runs in a Codespace from the browser alone (scripts/codespace.sh); docs/setup/claude-tokens.md; the Tokens page's Add a Claude account mints on an Actions runner, the token leaving it only sealed (token.yml, tools/token_run.py).\n- [Word revisions](word-revisions.md): a folder of Word documents is revised as tasks; tools/docx_review.py writes a review sheet and one task per document, queues new comments (papers.yml) and applies a worker's revisions as tracked changes; a connected Google Drive or OneDrive syncs both ways through drive-sync.yml (docs/research/drive-sync.md); several reviewers revise one document as authors of their own (docx_review.py panel, the lens-review skill, docs/research/lens-review.md).\n- [Workers and the coordinator](workers.md): one way of working from one person alone to several people, routines and workers in GitHub Actions, each a worker under its label worker:<name>; routines and workers in Actions are files under .github/symphony/workers/, added by the \"Add a worker\" form, and workers.yml runs those in Actions, dealt the open work once a run from one look; the coordinator releases idle claims and tells overlaps in the board's run, and plans epics in coordinator.yml."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/memory.md","type":"Project","content":"# Shared memory\n\nThe shared memory is docs/memory/, imported into every session through the line `@docs/memory/MEMORY.md` in CLAUDE.md. A claude.ai Project's own memory is private to the account that made it, so anything every account should know goes here instead.\n\n- One fact per file under docs/memory/, named in kebab-case (`how-this-repository-works.md`), with a one-line description as the file's first line.\n- One index line per fact in docs/memory/MEMORY.md, in the form `- [title](file.md): one line`. MEMORY.md stays under 200 lines and 25 KB; the limit is this project's own, because an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing from the index or the index names a file that is not there.\n- A thread that learns a fact, or finds one wrong, adds or corrects it in the same pull request as the change it learned from. Memory never travels alone and never waits for a later pull request.\n- Never a secret, never a token, never a cookie or a key. The checks look for the shapes of common secrets and refuse them.\n- What holds only for one piece of work (what was done today, what is left) is a checkpoint (.claude/skills/checkpoint), not a memory."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/writing.md","type":"Project","content":"# Writing\n\n- Plain prose that says why, in comments, commit messages, pull requests and documents alike. What the code does is read from the code.\n- Short functions; names that read as English.\n- No emoji in code, output or documents. ASCII and LF in everything under .claude/, docs/, tools/ and .github/; prefer ASCII elsewhere.\n- No new dependencies: Python 3.12 or later with its standard library, plain bash, and GitHub's own actions and anthropics/claude-code-action, pinned to a major tag. A proposal to add one is an issue first.\n- Nothing refers to a file that does not exist.\n- In conversation, put a fact worth keeping on its own line tagged `Decision:` or `Constraint:`, so the checkpoint skill and `cswap checkpoint write` keep it word for word; the rest of a session is inferred and may be lost."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/research.md","type":"Project","content":"# Research accumulates in docs/research/\n\nWhat a session finds out is kept in the repository, under docs/research/, where every account and every later session reads it: a reply, a status line, a Claude Doc or an artifact is read once, and a claude.ai Project's files belong to one account. A handoff under docs/checkpoints/ says where the research is and never carries it, so research never waits for a checkpoint and is never lost with one.\n\n- One file or one folder per topic, named in kebab-case. A programme, a question measured over time, is a folder with a README that says where it stands; a finished study, comparison, deep-research run or fact-check is one file. docs/research/README.md lists both, one line each, and the line comes in the same pull request as the file.\n- Every file opens by naming its source: the thread's title, the date, and the link of the Claude Doc, artifact or upload it came from where there is one. A doc or artifact a session cannot read from its container is listed in the README by link and never written down from memory.\n- Research lands in the pull request of the work it came from or, when the research is the work, in a pull request of its own, before the thread's checkpoint. A note stays here once its question is settled; a pull request that settles a finding for good may move it to docs/history/, the longer record, and a fact every session should know becomes a memory (.claude/rules/memory.md) that points here.\n- ASCII and LF, as the checks hold everything under docs/ to, and plain prose that says why (.claude/rules/writing.md); no secret, no token, no transcript and no tool output, as docs/history/ holds too, and the checks refuse the shapes of common secrets and any .events.json."},{"path":"/home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/board.md","type":"Project","content":"# The board comes first\n\nBefore any change in this repository:\n\n1. Fetch: `git fetch origin`. The session-start hook does this once; do it again before you push.\n2. Read the board and docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (how work is claimed). The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow; read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is. The session-start hook prints it at the start of every session; docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout.\n3. Check the claim on the issue you mean to take: a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, means another worker has it, and a `worker:<name>` label that is not yours, with none that is, means the issue is that worker's: pick another. An issue the labels give to several workers, you among them, is each of theirs, and the first to claim it holds it. An issue the board's Given to workers section gives you comes first, then an unclaimed blocker, then the Queue; never take an epic, which the coordinator plans, nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nYou work here as a worker under a name of your own (docs/agents/WORKERS.md): in a person's session, the person's GitHub login unless the environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; in a routine or a worker in Actions, the name its prompt gives. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Claim with the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) before the first change to any other file, and open the draft pull request at once.\n\nNever push to a branch another worker claimed. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim; after a rebase, your own claim branch takes `git push --force-with-lease=claude/<issue>:<the tip you last pushed> origin claude/<issue>` (the claim skill's step 7), the branch named so that the push forces nothing else and the tip so that it never forces over a push made since, and nothing else ever does. When the board comments \"main moved\" on your pull request, bring the default branch in: rebase on it and push again, or merge it in. An issue labelled blocker is claimed like any other, and nobody fixes it in parallel.\n\nA hook enforces the push rules before they can be broken: tools/guard_push.py, wired in .claude/settings.json, refuses a push to the default or board branch, a plain --force anywhere, a + before a refspec, which forces that ref as --force does, the refspec : and patterns with *, which move branches wholesale as --all, --branches and --mirror do, a push to a name that reads as a claim and claims nothing, such as claude/03 (a claim branch is claude/ and the issue's number in one to nine ASCII digits with no leading zero, docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md, Claim), --force-with-lease outside your own claude/ branch, and, where the copy carries the fence (the template since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, and with its tools/guard_push.py where the copy's own predates symphony-template#69), --force-with-lease onto a claude/ branch whose claim file on origin names another worker, or with a branch that carries no claim file for the issue, since a lease after a fetch is the one push git itself would take onto another worker's claim. A refusal is the rule working, not an obstacle; the answer is a pull request, a rebase or another branch, never a way around the hook. Branch protection on GitHub (docs/setup/github.md) stands behind it."},{"path":"/home/user/spine-program/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# spine-program: the root working context\n\nThis is one private repository that seeds a set of Claude Code projects, one per numbered folder; each folder is\nself-contained (its own `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `STATUS.md`, `TODO.md`, `MEMORY.md` + `memory/`, three manifests and the\nsalvaged material). Open the folder's `CLAUDE.md` first: the rules below bind everywhere, and the folder adds its own\ntraps. Nothing here is imaging data or a weight; the grid and the named repositories hold those. Since 2026-10-13 the salvaged record, each folder's `results/`, `figures/`, `manuscripts/` and `regulatory/`, lives on the grid too, under `/rs/rs_grp_oschome/shared/spine-program/archive/`, and the folders in the tree are symlinks to it: `ARCHIVE.md` says how a session reads a file from either place and how a task brings one back into its branch (issue #87).\n\n## Read first\n\n- `_plan/PLAN.md` sections 1-2 (the division and the builders' rules) and section 9 (the corrections of the final pass).\n- The folder's `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `STATUS.md`, `TODO.md`.\n- `<folder>/docs/handoff/README.md` section 5 (the ten rules as the handoff wrote them) and\n  `<folder>/docs/handoff/RESULTS_CATALOG.md` \"How to read a row\" (every folder carries the student-readable handoff files).\n- `<folder>/DATA.md` (every grid path the folder uses, who may read it, a read-only check line); its access facts of\n  2026-10-11 supersede older statements in the `docs/handoff` copies.\n\n## The map\n\n```\n01_carm_level_detection      Greg-only   the C-arm path to a hospital test (SchwingNet as it stands -> service -> PREREG -> real fluoroscopy)\n02_transitional_naming_truth Greg-only   the transitional truth file, reader protocol, power table (paper 2); feeds 01's PREREG\n03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset   student     release v11, the dataset article, the NeurIPS camera-ready, Zenodo and HF\n04_openspinetoolkit          student     ostk's v11 defects and their one-hour tests; blocks 05\n05_ct_morphometry_parity     student     the spinopelvic estimator, the level atlas, 3-D to 2-D parity\n06_transitional_ct_census    student     the harvest over 21,622 CTs, the registry, ruleclass arms, the review tree\n07_plain_film_level_id       Greg-only   the radiograph corner detector, XRSpinoPelvic1K, the film-naming line\n08_render_devices            Greg-only   DRR banks, device cards, PolyPose, synthetic-to-real; serves 01 and 05\n09_wrong_level_clinical_qi   Greg-only   the iliac-crest paper, the scoping review, the QI protocol (01's per-case form)\n10_data_platform_grid        student     211 sources, the Ceph migration, identity and leakage, the registry, grid rules; the dependency of all\n11_students_grid_access      mixed       Beethoven, onboarding, the lab recipe, the med-ed record; waits on 10\n12_website_pacs_demo         student     the public site, the PACS demo, the level-service client; waits on 01 S2, 04, 05\n13_aidas_program             mixed       the AIDaS program map: workflow, IRB, data and literature mining, C-arm training, recruitment, the LLM; REFORMS\n99_archive                   Greg-only   superseded, refuted, dormant; never run, never quoted\n_plan/                                   the division, the disposition of every row, the exclusions, the inventories, the build record\n```\nEvery Write holder may read all 13 folders (Greg's answer of 2026-10-12 to `OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` A29, issue #68); the\nlabel governs claiming and hand-outs, not reading. `Greg-only` means Greg works the folder first, and a student takes an\nissue there only after Greg hands it out (his comment on the issue naming the student's login, or the label\n`greg-first` removed); `student` means a hand-out can be cut from the folder after the redactions it records. The\nrepository itself is private whatever the label.\n\n**How the folders relate.** 10 is the dependency of all (data, identity tables, the registry, the access gate). 04 blocks\n05. 02 feeds 01's PREREG and consumes 06. 08 serves 01 and 05. 09's per-case form is 01's S5 / S8 scaffold. 12 waits on 01\nS2, 04 and 05. 11 waits on 10. 99 is never run. The one circularity is stated on both sides: 01 depends on 12 for the\nservice client while 12 waits on 01 S2.\n\n## The standing rules (every folder's CLAUDE.md restates these in its own words)\n\n1. **Words and style.** Never the banned word, the French-derived word for where data came from: write origin, lineage,\n   source, where it came from. No fractional version numbers; fold a change into the next integer version. Figure captions\n   at most 60 words, the argument in the text. Plain sentences. Matrix tables in solid black with at most one outlined mark;\n   slides never use sideways bar charts. The affiliation is General Surgery (Wayne State University School of Medicine /\n   Detroit Medical Center), never Neurosurgery. Ashley Schehr is a person (senior scientist, co-PI, a published co-author),\n   never a product name; the product is Beethoven. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`, `memory/writing-preferences.md`,\n   `memory/user-affiliation-general-surgery.md`.)\n2. **Every number carries its file.** A count, date, path or claim names the file it comes from; what no file records is\n   written \"not recorded\"; say plainly what is not known rather than guess. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md` \"How to be useful here\".)\n3. **Check outside the computation.** The recurring failure in this programme is a number that is wrong but plausible:\n   three estimators produced finite, reasonable values from the wrong bone, the wrong surface or the wrong label map, and\n   internal consistency (PI = SS + PT) held throughout because both sides shared one bad landmark. Compare with published\n   values, an independent implementation, the spread of the distribution (not its median), a published prevalence; render\n   the mask; when a tidy explanation presents itself, test it before believing it. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`,\n   `ctsp:docs/SPINOPELVIC_ESTIMATOR.md`, `ctsp:docs/LEVEL_MORPHOMETRY.md`.)\n4. **Labels and orientation.** Two label schemes exist and mixing them does not raise: release v11 is VerSe-native\n   (L1 = 20 ... L5 = 24, sacrum 26, hips 30 / 31, femurs 32 / 33); the legacy map puts L1 at 1 and the femurs at 11 / 12,\n   and \"femur_left\" resolved against the wrong one comes back T4. Call `ostk.labels.labels_for(volume)`; never import a\n   fixed map. Identifier 29 (the carved S1) is retired in v11 and dissolved into 26; code that gates on 29 is retired with\n   it. Case ids are zero-padded (`zfill(4)`) in the release and were not in the old CSVs. Canonicalise to RAS to analyse;\n   never reorient when writing a label (`as_closest_canonical` + `nib.save` transposes a label away from its CT); CT volumes\n   are stored P, I, R. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`, `memory/label-scheme-v4-vs-ostk.md`, `memory/never-reorient-when-writing-labels.md`.)\n5. **Held out.** Held out means the person was never seen by the checkpoint or any ancestor; a render inherits its CT's\n   person; unknown is not clean. Every quoted score goes through `ssn:tools/eval_gate.py`. Never quote the leaked banks\n   (allspine v2, v6, v7, v8 for VerSe, `anchor_drr`, `vindrps`) or any number marked LEAKED, SUPERSEDED or RECORD ONLY in\n   `docs/handoff/RESULTS_CATALOG.md`. (`docs/handoff/LEAKAGE.md`.)\n6. **Pre-register, then score.** Write the rule and its success criterion before the run, hash it, stamp every output row\n   with the hash; a number produced before the freeze is calibration; comparators are pre-specified from the literature and\n   never tuned; manuscript numbers enter through a manifest key, never typed. (`docs/handoff/RECIPES.md`.)\n7. **The login node runs nothing.** Every computation, render, plot and download is an `sbatch` job; warrior kills python,\n   rsync, node, pip and conda by name; no container command on warrior and nothing of ours under its `/tmp`. `sbatch -D <dir>`\n   (a relative `--output` resolves against the submit directory); `afterany`, not `afterok`; IPC under `/dev/shm` (compute-node\n   `/tmp` is swept); large outputs on Ceph `/rs/rs_grp_oschome/go2432/`, not HOME; record a job before cancelling it; write grid\n   bash for bash 4.2; partition `reqp --qos=requeue` for CPU, `gmsap` / `gvohp` for GPU; Greg's python with nibabel and\n   scipy is `~/mambaforge/envs/spineps/bin/python`, in his HOME, which students cannot read; a student's environment is\n   `11_students_grid_access/STUDENT_ENV.md` (written by issue #66). Submit, estimate the wall time, write the job ids and\n   the ETA to the resume file, stop. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md` \"Grid\", `memory/no-jobs-on-login-node.md`,\n   `memory/submit-and-sleep-orchestration.md`.)\n8. **What stays private.** SchwingNet and all level-identification code, text, figures and weights; FluoroCap; the\n   `spinesurg-ct-nnunet` and `XRSpinoPelvic1K` repositories; this repository. Nothing about them goes public until Greg says\n   so; run `ssn:tools/disclosure_audit.py` before any talk, upload or submission; for the CNS talk use only\n   `CNS2026_Schwing_NotAllSpines_public_v12.pptx`. (`memory/fluorocap-repo-private.md`, `memory/schwingnet-disclosure-scrub.md`.)\n9. **Data directives.** Use all of a public dataset; a download budget is not a criterion; every truncation is fixed or\n   justified in writing before a rerun; a leaked initialisation is fixed by retraining, never disclosed as a caveat;\n   FluoroCap is out of training (a test set at most); identity comes from metadata first and the file name last; the IRB\n   umbrella (HPR 2026-269) covers only public, de-identified data downloadable without an agreement.\n   (`memory/no-unexplained-subsets-fix-not-disclose.md`, `memory/data-directives-2026-09-23.md`.)\n10. **Git, network, credentials.** In spine-program the worker doing the work, a person's sessions under the person's\n    GitHub login, commits and pushes only its own branch `claude/<issue>`, as `docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md` says; it never\n    commits to or pushes `main`; Greg merges. The claim push\n    is a plain `git push -u origin claude/<N>` in the Bash tool, never wrapped in `wsl`. In the code repositories\n    (`CTSpinoPelvic1K`, `OpenSpineToolkit`, `spinesurg-ct-nnunet`, `XRSpinoPelvic1K`, `openspineconsortium.github.io`,\n    `beethoven`) and on Greg's laptop: commit only when Greg asks; `CTSpinoPelvic1K` commits directly to master, no\n    feature branches, and pushes to `origin` and `osc`; `OpenSpineToolkit`: branch `main`, both remotes. The WSL network rule is Greg's Windows laptop's: there, network (git push, ssh, rsync, Hugging Face)\n    goes through `wsl -e bash -lc '...'`, and `ctsp:paper/mpda/make_submission.py` is the one exception and runs from\n    Windows. The git clauses of the folder `CLAUDE.md` files mean the repositories those folders name, never this one.\n    Tokens are read from files, never echoed, never on a command line or in\n    `sbatch --export`; nothing under the laptop vendor folder is read. Write scripts as files and run them by path (inline\n    heredocs collapse backslashes); never name a scratch script `numbers.py`, `io.py` or `pdb.py`.\n    (`memory/wsl-for-network.md`, `memory/bash-heredoc-backslashes.md`, `memory/scratchpad-shadows-stdlib.md`.)\n11. **The page limit** of the dataset article is 10 published pages and only `build.sh --reprint` measures it; read the\n    whole build output, because LaTeX emits a PDF and a page count on top of a broken table. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`.)\n12. **Personal data.** No student name or username, no researcher or personal e-mail address enters any file of this\n    repository; where a source carries one, the redacted copy is used and `MANIFEST_LOCAL.md` records the redaction by kind.\n    Three kinds of keep are recorded, not hidden: the author lists inside submitted or public manuscript sources (the record\n    of authorship; Greg's to confirm, `_plan/PLAN.md` section 5), Greg's own institutional corresponding-author address in\n    his manuscript sources and package metadata, and the maintainer addresses printed in third-party template files\n    (`neurips_2026.sty`, `medphy.bst`). Role mailboxes (`hpc@`, `IRBQuestions@`) and the ssh login `go2432@grid.wayne.edu` are\n    not personal data and stay. The worker name the claim protocol needs (the `worker:<login>` label, the claim file's\n    `- Worker:` line, the board) is working metadata like commit authorship, not the personal data this rule is about.\n\n## Prefixes and markers in the copied documents\n\n`ssn:` = spinesurg-ct-nnunet at 097933a; `ctsp:` = CTSpinoPelvic1K at 7ddfe38; `ostk:` = OpenSpineToolkit at cb43197;\n`xr:` = XRSpinoPelvic1K at 4f22935; `bth:` = OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven at 2f90319. Copied files spell where they came\nfrom with markers, never with a laptop path: `<laptop:Desktop>` (the Desktop root that held every checkout, so\n`<laptop:Desktop>/spinesurg-ct-nnunet` is `ssn:`), `<laptop:Temp scratchpad>` (a swept session folder; nothing there is a\ndependency), `<laptop memory folder>` (the live memory notes, copied redacted into each `memory/`), `<laptop vendor folder,\nnever read>`, `<laptop home>`. Only `MANIFEST_LOCAL.md` files and `_plan/` keep the laptop spellings, as the record of\nwhere each copy came from. A marker is history; the repository and commit are the dependency.\n\n## How to be useful here\n\nCheck against something outside the computation. When a tidy explanation presents itself, test it before believing it.\nSay plainly what is not known; `ctsp:docs/SPINOPELVIC_ESTIMATOR.md` records an unexplained failure in 128 records rather\nthan a guess at its cause, and that is the preferred form. Render the mask: twice in this programme one render settled\nwhat two or three rounds of reasoning from numbers got wrong.\n\n## What a cloud session reaches\n\nA claude.ai Project thread or a cloud session has this clone and GitHub, through its proxy, and nothing else: no grid,\nno ssh, no `sbatch`, no `wsl`, no access to Greg's HOME and no clone of the other repositories. The record folders the symlinks\npoint at are on the grid, so such a session reads one of their files from git history (`ARCHIVE.md`). Grid steps run from the\nBeethoven worker or by Greg, and their job ids and outputs come back to the claim branch. A change to another repository\nlands as `<folder>/patches/<N>-<slug>.patch` (`git format-patch` against the commit `MANIFEST_CODE.md` names) in the\nclaim's pull request, and Greg applies it.\n\n## How work is claimed\n\n@docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md` (or `/board`), and check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Never push to the default branch or to another worker's claim branch. The required check \"check\" (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `bash scripts/check.sh`) holds every pull request to the claim protocol and the review record (`CONTRIBUTING.md`); run it before a pull request. The push guard is the symphony plugin's and runs in local sessions only.\n\nThe protocol is the release of OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol that `tools/VENDORED` records, vendored under `tools/sdlc_protocol`; `.github/workflows/protocol.yml` brings each later release in, weekly and by hand, as a pull request from the branch `symphony-protocol/sync` (or an issue linking one where GitHub Actions may not open a pull request), and nobody vendors by hand. Every worker here is a person's sessions, named by the GitHub login: the registry under `.github/symphony/workers/`, the workers' and the coordinator's workflows that PROTOCOL.md names are the template's and are not here; the board's own run releases idle claims and tells overlapping work."},{"path":"/home/user/spine-program/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md","type":"Project","content":"# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on the repository. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by the board workflow (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol board`, whose rules are sdlc_protocol.board), which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to the default branch, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`; a repository that follows this protocol also prints it from its session-start hook, so every session begins with it. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill that ships with the template and with the symphony plugin is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or runs in GitHub Actions on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session is given another. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. sdlc_protocol.workers reads and checks those files, and `python3 -m sdlc_protocol workers check` fails on any file that does not hold together. A person may register too, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone until it is done.\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below).\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nsdlc_protocol.labels spells these names and the prefixes once, with the colour and description of every label a repository makes up front; a worker's label is made when the worker first needs it.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first; else an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of, one in your area first when you have one. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue.\n\nThe claim file takes this shape, with N the issue's number and NAME the worker's name. The board reads two parts of it (sdlc_protocol.claims): the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix, and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes the `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n```\n# Claim: #N\n\n- Issue: https://github.com/OWNER/NAME/issues/N\n- Worker: NAME\n- Branch: claude/N\n\n## Plan\n\nTwo to five sentences: what will change and why, in the order it will land.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n- the paths this work expects to change, one per line\n```\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off any other worker's label an earlier claim left there, and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch.\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes the repository's checks and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself. An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: once no open pull request uses `claude/<issue>`, the repository's tidy workflow deletes it.\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it waits, or helps on the one pull request by review.\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours, by the committer date of its head, is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, a cloud session whose gh pr calls are refused, says it is done, for a maintainer to mark. The coordinator is a step of the board's own run (sdlc_protocol.coordinator, through sdlc_protocol.adapters.github.coordinate), on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails between the two puts back the labels it took off, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over, unless the comment is there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, which makes the release whole; only when GitHub refuses the putting back too are the labels left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; sdlc_protocol.claims.release_due is that rule, so a coordinator that runs on every board run releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest (sdlc_protocol.board.released_keys). A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue in the Queue again, and renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file. Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept is deleted instead, since its work is already kept. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules (sdlc_protocol.board.rename_refused_text), and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target.\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest (sdlc_protocol.board.overlaps). In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it (sdlc_protocol.coordinator.overlap_comments_due); a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude, in the repository's coordinator workflow or a coordinator routine, plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: sdlc_protocol.team, run as `python3 -m sdlc_protocol team`. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes the task it was given, else the next unclaimed issue whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area).\n\n## Policy and worlds\n\nThe rules above are the same wherever they run. Two things change from one place to another, and sdlc_protocol.gate names them: a Policy, which says how the protocol behaves, and a World, which is where the branches live and which an adapter implements. The gate's decisions (claim, open a pull request, mark it ready, report its status, draw the board) are functions of the policy, the world and a queue of issues and pull requests, and they run no git and no HTTP themselves.\n\nThe policy's fields, with the values this document describes as the defaults:\n\n- claim_prefix, `claude/`: what a claim branch's name starts with, before the issue's number.\n- required_check, `check`: the check that must be green on the branch's head before the gate merges; empty where merging cleanly is the whole check.\n- bring_main_in, `rebase`: how a branch takes in a default branch that has moved. `rebase` is a session's way, above, with the one `--force-with-lease`; `merge` is a worker in Actions', merging the default branch in so nobody force-pushes. The \"main moved\" answer says the steps in the policy's own way (sdlc_protocol.gate.main_moved_instruction).\n- who_merges, `owner`: who moves the default branch. `owner` is the repository's owner by hand, as above; `gate` is the gate itself, when a pull request is marked ready; `auto-merge` is GitHub, once the required check is green, as a worker whose file says so lands.\n- idle_after, 24 hours: how long a claim may go without a commit before it is released.\n\nTwo presets name the two ways of landing above: ACCOUNT_POLICY (rebase, the owner merges) and TEAM_POLICY (merge, auto-merge). A policy also carries remote and default_branch, `origin` and `main` unless the place calls them otherwise, which are only the names its answers use, so an instruction reads `origin/main` or `team/main` as the place does.\n\nA world answers six questions: the default branch's commit (main_sha), a branch's commit (branch_sha), how a branch stands to the default branch (standing: clean, behind, conflicting or unknown), the state of the required check on its head (check: green, red, pending or none), the merge itself (merge, which returns the default branch's new commit or raises why not: MainMoved, the default branch left as it was; MergeRefused, for a reason the branch's author fixes; GateFailed, the world could not act), and the time (now). What each adapter supplies:\n\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.github, a repository on GitHub through the REST API, with a workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN or an App installation token: the standing from the pull request's mergeable_state, told apart further by mergeable and by how far the head is behind its base where GitHub says only that something blocks the merge, and unknown for a branch with no open pull request; the check from the check runs on the branch's head that carry the required check's name; the merge through GitHub's merge API, pinned to the head the gate looked at, and only when who_merges is `gate`, any other policy being refused with NotAllowed; the clock as given, or the wall clock.\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.bare_git, a bare repository that agents share, as coordbench's sdlc arm does: the standing from git itself, in a scratch clone, with the conflicting files named and two more words a git world can tell apart (no branch, nothing new); no checks, so the check is always none; the merge as `git merge --no-ff` under the gate's own committer identity, pushed without force, a conflict or a refused push being MainMoved; the wall clock.\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.memory, a world held in memory for tests and simulations: standings and checks set by hand, a clock that moves only when told, so a simulation can hold a check pending for as long as it means CI to take, and a record of every merge.\n\nA refusal carries the code its command exits with, so a script can tell one kind from another: 1 refused (claim first, push, commit something), 2 someone else's (another holds the claim, the issue or the pull request), 3 main moved (bring the default branch in and try again), 4 gate failed (the world could not act).\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard (sdlc_protocol.guard, run as a Claude Code hook through `python3 -m sdlc_protocol guard`) refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it.\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n- The repository's checks pass before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (sdlc_protocol.protocol_check, run as `python3 -m sdlc_protocol check`, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in the repository itself, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it."},{"path":"/home/user/spine-program/docs/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"Project","content":"# Shared memory index\n\nEach project folder keeps its own MEMORY.md and its own memory/ (one fact per file); a fact about one project lives\nthere, not here. This file indexes only the facts that hold for the repository as a whole. The 13 project indexes:\n\n- [01_carm_level_detection](../../01_carm_level_detection/MEMORY.md)\n- [02_transitional_naming_truth](../../02_transitional_naming_truth/MEMORY.md)\n- [03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset](../../03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset/MEMORY.md)\n- [04_openspinetoolkit](../../04_openspinetoolkit/MEMORY.md)\n- [05_ct_morphometry_parity](../../05_ct_morphometry_parity/MEMORY.md)\n- [06_transitional_ct_census](../../06_transitional_ct_census/MEMORY.md)\n- [07_plain_film_level_id](../../07_plain_film_level_id/MEMORY.md)\n- [08_render_devices](../../08_render_devices/MEMORY.md)\n- [09_wrong_level_clinical_qi](../../09_wrong_level_clinical_qi/MEMORY.md)\n- [10_data_platform_grid](../../10_data_platform_grid/MEMORY.md)\n- [11_students_grid_access](../../11_students_grid_access/MEMORY.md)\n- [12_website_pacs_demo](../../12_website_pacs_demo/MEMORY.md)\n- [99_archive](../../99_archive/MEMORY.md)\n\n## Cross-project facts\n\nOne line per fact, `- [title](file-name.md): one line`, in alphabetical order of file name, added with the remember\nskill (.claude/skills/remember/SKILL.md) in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n- [The claim protocol updates itself](the-claim-protocol-updates-itself.md): tools/sdlc_protocol is symphony-protocol at the release tools/VENDORED records, and .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each later release in as a pull request from the branch symphony-protocol/sync; how to vendor by hand, and what of the template's worker machinery is not here.\n- [The record material is on the grid](the-record-material-is-on-the-grid.md): each folder's results/, figures/, manuscripts/ and regulatory/ are symlinks to /rs/rs_grp_oschome/shared/spine-program/archive/ since 2026-10-13, the files stay in git history at main 5597197, and ARCHIVE.md says how to read or bring one back."}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"c9e14da5-54ac-4e80-90c5-ab2db41998de","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:10:32.761Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# SchwingNet\n\nThis repository is a Claude project kept as files: its instructions, its goal, its shared memory and its board live here, and Claude Code reads them in every session, local or cloud. The repository is the source of truth for every Claude session and every account; a claude.ai Project only links it, its own fields stay blank, and Claude Code loads this file and what it imports at the start of every session, a Project's thread and a cloud session opened on the repository alike.\n\n## Read first\n\n- The goal, the scope and who owns what, loaded into every session by this line: @docs/project.md\n- How work is claimed, done in the open and finished, loaded the same way: @docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n- Who works here: every session, routine and worker in GitHub Actions is a worker under a name of its own, labelled worker:<name> (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). A session's name is the GitHub login of the person it works for, unless its environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; a routine's or a worker in Actions' is the one its prompt gives.\n- The board: the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow from the open issues, the claim branches and the pull requests. It lives on another branch, which no import reaches: read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on main only describes it.\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board, check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n\n## Shared memory\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nA fact worth keeping is one file under docs/memory/ with one index line in MEMORY.md, added in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- The board first (.claude/rules/board.md): fetch, read the board (STATE.md on the branch `board`) and PROTOCOL.md, check the claim; never push to another worker's branch; never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. A hook refuses the pushes this rule forbids, and /board (.claude/commands/board.md) reprints the whole board mid-session.\n- Memory (.claude/rules/memory.md): one fact per file, a kebab-case name, a one-line description first, one index line; never a secret.\n- Writing (.claude/rules/writing.md): plain prose that says why, short functions, names that read as English, no emoji, ASCII and LF, no new dependencies; tag a durable fact in conversation as Decision: or Constraint: so a checkpoint keeps it.\n- Research (.claude/rules/research.md): what a session finds out goes under docs/research/, one file or folder per topic that opens by naming its source, with a line in docs/research/README.md, in the same pull request as the work and before its checkpoint; a handoff under docs/checkpoints/ points to it and never carries it.\n- Skills: .claude/skills/claim, .claude/skills/remember and .claude/skills/checkpoint are the three procedures, in the order a piece of work meets them; .claude/skills/drive-organize (a OneDrive sorted into projects, read through Microsoft Graph under the owner's own Microsoft sign-in), .claude/skills/lens-review (several reviewers on one Word document) and .claude/skills/notebook (drive files held together as a notebook in a repository of its own, never moved) are run when the owner asks.\n- The block at the end of this file, between the cswap markers, is rewritten by the checkpoint skill and by `cswap checkpoint write`; do not edit it by hand.\n\n## Checks\n\nRun `bash scripts/check.sh` before opening or updating a pull request. CI runs the same script as the check named \"check\", and the default branch takes only pull requests that pass it. The same check fails a pull request marked ready whose body has no section `## Adversarial review` (tools/review_record.py, CONTRIBUTING.md), so write the review into the body before marking it ready.\n\n## Session checkpoint (kept by cswap)\n\nRead `.claude/session.md` at the start of a session: it holds the completed work, the current state and the remaining tasks. Tag durable facts in conversation as `Decision:` or `Constraint:` so the next `cswap checkpoint write` keeps them.\n\n### Durable decisions\n- The goal (the owner, 2026-10-20): low-latency, high-accuracy vertebral classification on fluoroscopic video or images; the data workflow first, one model per stage as version 1, evaluation on real frames visible from the first checkpoint, no ensembles until one model works, no comparison to the old baseline.\n- Every grid job and every training waits for the owner's word and the number of jobs; usage credits are never used; CT-RATE (21.3 TB) is never submitted without his TB number.\n- Dataset request letters go from the owner's Wayne State Outlook on his word, subject tag [XR/CT Imaging Research], short, the opening line \"I study the application of computer science to improving spinal health, and my current work needs ...\", addresses verified from institutional pages or papers, he signs any DUA; no external text says or implies that a model, a neural network, machine learning, AI or training is built or used.\n- The unheld datasets are obtained with collaborators in a separate private repository worked through Symphony (OpenSpineConsortium/spine-datasets, the owner's ask of 2026-10-21); SchwingNet stays confidential until the provisional is filed, and the talk shows results, not methods.\n- Radiopaedia is never fetched by script.\n### Constraints\n- A cloud session cannot create a repository, approve a deployment or publish a release (403), and its proxy refuses figshare, Zenodo, Mendeley, Hugging Face, openi.nlm.nih.gov, doi.org and most publishers, so no real fetch runs in a container; the grid paste in grid/README.md is the first real run.\n- The pre-push hook runs the whole gate and refuses a push from a tree with anything uncommitted; a cloud session exports PATH=\"$HOME/.pixi/bin:$PATH\" before pushing.\n- Scripts under tools/ drop tools/ from sys.path before importing torch (tools/profile.py shadows the standard library's profile); functions that take a pydantic model are listed under [tool.beethoven-qa.unproven].\n- The Microsoft 365 connector sends only a draft created first (create, then send); a direct send is refused.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/docs/project.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# SchwingNet: goal and settings\n\nThis file is the project's goal and the settings every account's thin Project shares. It is the one place where the goal is written: CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session, and the claude.ai Projects carry no copy of it. It changes by pull request like everything else.\n\n## Goal\n\nConfidential: this file describes unpublished work with a provisional patent in preparation, and stays in this private repository; nothing of it is copied to a public place. SchwingNet names vertebral levels on intraoperative fluoroscopy; the reported stack and its numbers are spine-program's 01_carm_level_detection (README.md, STATUS.md, docs/MODEL_CARD.md). This project makes the next SchwingNet as a workflow that grows: low-latency, high-accuracy vertebral classification on fluoroscopic video or images, built through Symphony's claim protocol and beethoven-qa's definition of done between 2026-10-20 and the CNS talk of 2 November 2026 (mini-oral 66629), so that every number the talk shows is reproduced by a command from a record in this repository. Done is: a dataset added by one pull request and one incremental run, no epoch run twice; version 1 of SchwingNet, one model per stage trained to work on the registry as it stands, with its card; an evaluation page that draws fixed real witness frames at every checkpoint and tables every version with intervals and its latency, in Weights and Biases and on this repository's docs site; and the recorded C-arm sweep with the overlay and the measured latency, ready to show once the provisional is filed (by 30 October). The plan is docs/research/retraining-plan.md (version 2, the owner's words of 2026-10-20); no training job is submitted before the owner gives the word and the number for it.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope: the areas this project owns, each in one line; docs/agents/OWNERSHIP.md maps them to workers.\n\n- The retraining, once the owner has stated the measure: the pre-registered plan, the grid jobs through Beethoven (docs/setup/grid.md), the clean encoder rebuild, the split check, the eval gate; every number carries its run id, commit and result file.\n- The measurement: held-out C-ArmLSpine, the transitional stratum with two readers, the synthetic anomaly arm with exact truth, never pooled with the real one.\n- The demo: the recorded sweep (C-ArmLSpine case134 and case39), the overlay, the measured latency, the slides' figures.\n- The quality gate: new code to beethoven-qa's definition of done; the legacy tools of the reported pipeline listed under unproven, never refactored before the talk, since a refactor voids the parity gate.\n- The synthesis of the artifacts (artifacts/_synthesis/), the record the Artifact Textbook is written from, kept current by .github/workflows/synthesis.yml: it looks with no Claude at every change of an artifact, a research note or a dataset record, within minutes of a merge a person or an App makes and at the next daily run for one GitHub's auto-merge makes, and runs the Claude reads a change needs, 10 runs a day unless the variable SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY says otherwise, 50 at most, each adding what it read to the one open synthesis pull request, or opening it (#166).\n\nOut of scope, by decision: what this project does not do, each with the reason, so nobody proposes it again.\n\n- Anything public before the provisional is filed: no push to a public repository, no preprint, no slide of the method; the talk shows what SchwingNet does, not how (the owner, 2026-10-20).\n- A live run at the podium: no laptop latency exists and the CPU path took seconds per frame; the demo is a recording of a causal run with its latency on screen.\n- A grid job, a Claude run or a training without the owner's word and the number of jobs; usage credits are never used. The synthesis reads of .github/workflows/synthesis.yml have that word and number: the owner chose on 2026-10-23 that they start on their own, 10 runs a day unless the variable SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY says otherwise, 50 at most, and the variable SYNTHESIS_READS set to off stops them (#166). The repository's private claude.ai front door, tools/front_door.html, runs no workflow and no Claude run of the project's: no GitHub Action's session carries the Artifact tool and a cloud thread's checks refuse to publish a page that declares a connector, so the owner publishes it from the owner's own computer with `python3 tools/textbook_publish.py local`, the owner's choice of 2026-10-25 (#236, #277), once and again only when the page changes, the page being since 2026-10-27 the door's loader, tools/front_door_loader.html, which runs the door main holds at every open, so a change to the door needs no publish (the owner, 2026-10-27); opened, it reads the repository itself, main and the board, through the viewer's own GitHub connector in claude.ai, with the viewer's own access, so nothing is staged or republished when the repository moves, the owner's word of 2026-10-26 (it replaced the reader of #294, which read a copy a thread staged into the Claude project's files, since the Artifact tool publishes only what Claude has read whole). The textbook's research loop, .github/workflows/research.yml, has a standing exception for grid jobs, fetches and training: the owner asked on 2026-10-24 at 16:17 UTC for a Claude integration that continues the research \"AUTONOMOUSLY ... INDEPDENTLY OF HUMAN INTERACTION\", and at 17:36 UTC, told it would ask first, answered \"no i dont want this restriction ... give it full command to direct autonmous research\" (#220). It runs every four hours, at most RESEARCH_RUNS_PER_DAY runs a day (6 unless it says otherwise, 50 at most), the variable RESEARCH_LOOP set to off stops it, and it still keeps the size rule, the size readers for gdown, Kaggle and Redivis (#163), no CT-RATE without the owner's terabytes, no Radiopaedia by script, no usage credits, the claim protocol and nothing public; letters, e-mails and the gate words it cannot reach stay the owner's (.github/symphony/research.md).\n- A settings-as-code app (github/safe-settings, or the Repository Settings App reading .github/settings.yml), decided no on 2026-10-11 (symphony-template#18): it is a dependency with admin rights on every repository it covers, and a server of our own in one case, for settings that Symphony Setup applies for one run from the front door, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables for what an organization makes.\n\n## Repositories\n\n- OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet, this one: the shared plane for every account and every session; the default branch is main.\n- Repositories this project reads, all private, each a record and never changed from here (a change lands as a patch in the claim's pull request, as spine-program's CLAUDE.md says):\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/spine-program, folder 01_carm_level_detection: the reported SchwingNet, its STATUS.md numbers, MODEL_CARD.md, the cine demo build plan and the handoff; 02_transitional_naming_truth for the transitional truth file and the frozen G3 split; 07_plain_film_level_id for the CNS manuscript rows.\n  - Gregory-Schwing-MD-PhD/spinesurg-ct-nnunet, branch L6_Class: the training and evaluation code (tools/level_prototype.py, tools/eval_gate.py, the slurm/ scripts); the demo tools cine_eval.py and cine_latency.py are untracked there and live as copies in spine-program's 01_carm_level_detection/code/.\n  - The grid, Wayne State's, reached through Beethoven's runner chain (docs/setup/grid.md): the corpora under /rs/rs_grp_oschome/go2432/data/, C-ArmLSpine among them; nothing of them is ever copied here.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven-qa, private, to be taken as a pinned copy under vendor/beethoven-qa once its 6.0.0 is merged and tagged (issue #5; the steps are symphony-template's docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md, symphony-template#280), and OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven-research, whose split check the measurement uses.\n- The template's own repositories, four, each owned by the OpenSpineConsortium organization, whose owners merge to its default branch:\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, public: the claim protocol as a Python library with no dependencies (the board, the protocol check, team mode's clerk, the push guard's rules and the merge gate). It is vendored here under tools/sdlc_protocol, with tools/VENDORED recording the version, the commit and the sha256 of every copied file, and scripts/check.sh fails when the copy differs from that record, or, on CI, which clones the library, from the library's files at that commit. A change to the rules is made there, never here: once the owner has approved and published a release there that carries it, its sync workflow opens the pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template with that release's copy, and the template's check decides. A repository made from the template is not on the sync's list; its own .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each new release in instead, weekly, as a pull request from a branch of the same name, or as an issue linking that pull request where GitHub Actions may not open one. By hand it is `python3 <symphony-protocol>/scripts/vendor.py <this repository> --consumer OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template` and a pull request. Its ecosystem.json is the one list of which repository takes which modules, and its ECOSYSTEM.md, generated from that list, maps all five repositories.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/coordbench, public: the playground and benchmark that compares ways of coordinating Claude Code agents on CooperBench's tasks, this protocol among them; its sdlc arm runs on the library's merge gate, vendored under coordbench/vendor. It began as experiment/ on OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template's branch claude/10.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-marketplace, public: the Claude Code plugin marketplace with one plugin, symphony, which brings the skills, /board and the hooks to a repository not made from this template. .claude/settings.json names the marketplace and enables nothing, since this repository wires the same hooks itself.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-app, private: the GitHub App SymphonyBoard (slug symphonyboard). Its installation token lets template-init's first commit start the Checks and the Board, which a push with GITHUB_TOKEN cannot, and the symphony-protocol sync opens its pull requests in every consumer with it; its webhook server, where it is run, posts the protocol check as a check run of its own, sdlc/protocol.\n\n## Model and effort\n\nEvery account's Project uses the same model and the same effort, so the work reads the same whoever did it. Record the choice here and set it in each Project's settings when you make the Project (docs/setup/claude-project.md).\n\n- Model: not yet chosen.\n- Effort: not yet chosen.\n\n## Crowd mode\n\n- Crowd mode: off. To open the project to volunteers who each work on their own Claude account, the owner runs `python3 scripts/crowd.py setup` (docs/setup/crowdsourcing.md), whose pull request turns this line on with the decisions that come with it; from then on the required check holds the repository to them.\n\n## Owners\n\n- Repository owner: OpenSpineConsortium, who merges to main; .github/CODEOWNERS names Gregory-Schwing-MD-PhD, the owner's own login, or in an organization's copy the person who made the copy, because GitHub asks a user or a team for review and never an organization (docs/setup/github.md, section 7).\n- Workers: each person's sessions, under the person's GitHub login, and the routines and workers in GitHub Actions the registry .github/symphony/workers/ names (docs/agents/WORKERS.md); none registered yet. Each worker's label is worker:<name>, made when it first needs it.\n- The Claude token: the secret CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, from one account's `claude setup-token`, on whose plan @claude, the workers in Actions, the coordinator workflow, the synthesis reads and the research loop run (docs/setup/claude-action.md); not set until the setup issue's second box is ticked, or until the owner adds it for the synthesis reads, the one Claude credential the project keeps, by the owner's choice of 2026-10-23 (#166).\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by .github/workflows/board.yml, which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to main, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or a worker in GitHub Actions, which .github/workflows/workers.yml runs on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike. docs/agents/WORKERS.md says how they share the repository.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session's environment sets `SYMPHONY_WORKER` to another; a person needs no file to work here. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. .github/symphony/workers/README.md lists the settings, and `bash scripts/check.sh` fails on any file that does not hold together (`python3 tools/workers.py check`). A worker is added with the \"Add a worker\" issue form, whose workflow .github/workflows/add-worker.yml opens the pull request that adds its file, or by adding the file by hand in a pull request of its own. A person may register too, with `\"runs\": \"session\"`, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take as Pick says.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done (Blockers).\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. Where the labels of several workers name one issue, it is theirs alone in the same way: given to each of them, and held by the first of them to claim it, whose Announce takes the others' labels off. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below). The board's Queue leaves an epic out, since nobody takes one whole.\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\ntemplate-init makes every label above but worker:<name>, six in all, when the repository is made, and scripts/setup-repo.sh makes them again from a clone. A worker's label is made when the worker first needs it: the claim skill makes it before putting it on an issue, and the coordinator when it gives a worker a task.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first. Else take a blocker, with or without todo, that the Blockers section lists, that nobody has claimed and that no worker's label gives to another, since a blocker comes before the Queue. Else take an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of. Of the blockers and the Queue, take only one in your area when your file under .github/symphony/workers/ gives you one; an issue that lists no files fits every area (Planning). Wherever an issue is listed, never take an epic, which the coordinator plans (Planning), nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link, on the template in docs/agents/claims/README.md. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue. The board reads two parts of the claim file: the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix (a line with nothing after it but markup or a note in parentheses names nobody: the board shows such a claim as unnamed, since the file is there to be corrected, while the clerk and a release, which must name a worker, take the one the issue's label names, as they do once the pull request has deleted the file), and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes its `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off every other worker's label, one an earlier claim left there or one that gave the issue to several workers, so that from the claim on the labels name the holder alone, as the claim file does; and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. GitHub opens one pull request at a time between the same two branches, so where one that someone who may not write opened from the branch stands in the way, which is no claim's (Rules that hold everywhere), close it, as anyone who may write can, and open your own. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, which it does in whichever run finds the branch behind or conflicting, once per commit of the default branch, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. The board reads a branch as behind where GitHub calls its pull request behind, as it does under a rule that requires an up-to-date branch, or, where GitHub's word is blocked or draft, which says only that something else stops the merge, a rule still holding the pull request or the draft itself, where the comparison of the branch with the default branch says so. A pull request GitHub calls clean, unstable or has_hooks, a draft among them, since GitHub gives a draft the word its branch has, is not held for being behind under the repository's rules, so it hears nothing however far the default branch has moved; one GitHub calls dirty, in conflict with the default branch, hears it. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch. Since a worker that has fetched holds a remote-tracking ref that matches origin, git itself would take its lease onto any claim, so before a lease onto `claude/N` the push guard reads the claim file `docs/agents/claims/N.md` on the branch the lease holds against (the commit the lease names, else origin's `claude/N` as the fetch left it) and on the branch pushed, each at its tip or, once the branch has deleted the file, where its history last added it, and refuses a lease that would take another worker's claim: one whose two files name different workers, or whose branch pushed carries none; a claim file git cannot show leaves the lease to GitHub's rules, as the guard leaves everything it cannot read (the template carries it since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy takes it once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, whose Given to workers heading 0.5.0 changes, since the copy's protocol.yml brings only tools/, and with the template's tools/guard_push.py where its own predates symphony-template#69, since an older one hands the guard no claim files).\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes `bash scripts/check.sh` and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `\"lands\": \"auto-merge\"`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. A worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, such as a cloud session whose `gh pr` calls are refused, keeps the claim file instead, pushes the finished work with `bash scripts/check.sh` passing, comments \"finished; ready to mark\" on its draft and stops, leaving the steps above to a person who can take them; until then its claim is not released (Release). Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself (docs/setup/github.md). An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: .github/workflows/tidy.yml deletes `claude/<issue>` once no open pull request uses it, and with it any `released/<issue>-<sha>` a released claim left (Release).\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. The board lists every open issue labelled blocker under Blockers, claimed or not, and a worker takes one there before the Queue, as Pick says. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it held waits for it, helps on the one pull request by review, or takes other work it does not stand in the way of (Pick).\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready says it is done (Finish). When a branch's last commit came, here and below, is when GitHub received the push that set its head, as the repository's activity records that push, so no worker's clock decides it; an entry that set the branch to another head, or deleted it, dates it too when it came after the run began, a push after the run read the branch, while an older one is stale and lists no push that set the head; the head's committer date stands in where the activity, read once more after a pause when the first read lists no push that set the head or GitHub gives it no answer that holds, still lists none, or where GitHub refuses to read it, since the push a run was started by is the one the activity is likeliest not to list yet, but never earlier than the newest entry either read listed for the branch, which the push that set the head came no sooner than. In the run that a push to the branch started, which the workflow's event names, a head no read dates is dated at the run's start instead, and so is one a read dates by an entry that set it more than a few minutes before the run began, an older push of the same head that the activity lists while it does not list that push yet, or that push itself where the run, queued behind others, began that much after it, for that run alone, since that push came just before the run, whatever the two reads list, so no claim is released in the run its own push started; the next run reads the activity again. The coordinator here is a step of the board's own run, in .github/workflows/board.yml, on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). Before it releases, it frees each reopened issue whose labels still speak of a claim that ended with its close (Finish), so the in-review that claim left finishes no claim, and a claim branch still standing for the issue is released in that same run once it has gone a day without a commit. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off, and puts the label todo on it when it carries none of todo, epic and blocker, so the Queue lists it again even when a worker's label alone gave it to the worker, while a blocker is listed under Blockers without it; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails at the labels, taking one off or putting todo on, puts back the labels it took off, even one whose taking off GitHub answered with an error, unless GitHub refused it with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of taking it off, since GitHub may have taken it off and a label put back that is still on changes nothing, and takes off the todo it put on, even one whose putting on GitHub answered with an error, since GitHub may have written it, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. A run whose comment fails reads the issue's comments, and the comment there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, makes the release whole. Where they list none and GitHub refused the comment, with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of writing anything, the run puts back the labels it took off and takes off the todo it put on, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. Where they list none after any other answer to the comment, a rate limit's refusal, a 429 or a 403 that names a rate limit or a wait in retry-after, on the last try or on one the run made the comment again after, which GitHub's documentation never says it gives in place of acting on the call, a server error, a redirect, an answer the run could not read or no answer at all, none of which says GitHub wrote nothing, and GitHub may list a comment it wrote only later, or where the run cannot read them, whatever GitHub answered the comment with, the run cannot tell whether a release comment stands, its own, an earlier run's or one someone else wrote: it leaves the labels as the release left them and counts no release of its own, so its page lists the claim as held and the branch keeps its name, since labels put back beside a comment that stands would hold the claim for good, the comment telling every later coordinator the claim is released and the label claimed telling the board it is held, and the next run, reading the comments, finds the release whole where a release comment that counts stands, or releases the claim again where none does, unless its holder pushed since, which keeps the claim, its labels as the release left them, naming no holder, until it finishes, a person puts them back or it goes a day without a commit and is released again, or a person put the label claimed back beside a comment that stands, which undoes that release (below), though a branch at a head a release already kept is released in that same run by its latest release comment, where one that counts stands, which counts for such a branch whenever it came, with no word on the issue of this run's release where GitHub wrote none. Where GitHub refuses the putting back too the labels are left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; so, running on every board run, it releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, todo put on as the coordinator puts it, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest. A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue as it lists any unclaimed one, in the Queue or, for a blocker, under Blockers, though until the same run has renamed the branch Pick reads the branch as another worker's and the clerk deals the issue to nobody (Planning), and .github/workflows/board.yml renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file (.claude/skills/claim says how). Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept, one a branch `released/<issue>-<short sha>` stands at, brings nothing new: its latest release comment counts for it whenever it came, before that push or after, so the board deletes it instead of renaming it, since its work is already kept, and the coordinator releases it only where the board counts no release of it, no release comment counting or the label claimed put back, once it has gone a day without a commit. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules, and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target (docs/setup/github.md).\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest, the work that began first, when GitHub says the push that made its branch came, or, for a pull request from a fork or from a branch it says nothing of, when the pull request was opened; work whose beginning nothing says, which can only be a claim with no pull request yet, counts as the newest, since a claim opens its draft at once (Work in the open), and the number breaks a tie. In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it; a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. The marker names each piece by its identity: a claim and its own pull requests are one piece, the issue's number, told on that issue, so opening a pull request tells nothing again, and a marker on one of the claim's own open pull requests counts as one on the issue; a pull request that is no claim's is a piece of its own, its number, told on itself. An issue's number names each claim on it in turn, so a marker tells a claim's work, or tells of it, only when it came no earlier than the push that made that claim's branch, as GitHub dates that push for the order above, and every marker counts for a claim whose push GitHub does not date. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude in .github/workflows/coordinator.yml on the repository's Claude token (docs/agents/WORKERS.md), plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it todo and `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue, labelled todo alone. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: tools/team.py. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run of .github/workflows/workers.yml do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes its own claim that needs work, one whose claim file names it, whatever the issue's labels say, or, where no claim file names a worker, one whose issue's label names it, a worker:<name> label before the older spellings and the first by name among labels of one spelling, as a release names whose the claim was, but never a claim that counts as released, which no worker carries and none takes until the board has renamed its branch (Pick, Release), nor a claim labelled blocked, whose holder waits on a peer or the coordinator (Labels) and so has nothing else to do; else the task it was given, one given to it alone first, and a task the labels of several workers give going to the first of them, in the order the registry lists the workers in Actions, with nothing else to do; else the next unclaimed blocker or queued issue, blockers first, whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area). The order is the team's: every worker the deal is for, in .github/workflows/workers.yml every registered worker in Actions, whether or not the run starts it, so a worker outside the run keeps its share, and two runs that look at one state deal no issue to two workers. The workers of one run are dealt from one look at the repository, taken once for the run and handed to each worker (`python3 tools/team.py deal`), since two workers looking for themselves at different moments, one while a peer's ready pull request is still clean and one after the default branch has moved past it, could be dealt one issue; a worker that asks alone (`python3 tools/team.py next`) is dealt by the same rule from its own look.\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard, tools/guard_push.py, wired as a hook in .claude/settings.json, refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it (docs/setup/github.md).\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from (.claude/rules/memory.md).\n- `bash scripts/check.sh` passes before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (tools/check_protocol.py, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in this repository, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it.\n- A claim and its pull request live in this repository, so a pull request from a fork is no claim's, whatever its branch is called: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename, which closes the claim's own pull request, leaves it open.\n- A claim's pull request is opened by the repository's owner, a member of its organization, a collaborator or anyone else who may write to the repository, or by a GitHub App, which opens one only where it is installed, as the workers in Actions open theirs; a pull request anyone else opens from a claim's branch, as GitHub's documentation says anyone who may read the repository can, is no claim's either: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename closes it with the claim's own, since GitHub closes each open pull request whose branch it renames. Where GitHub will not answer whether someone may write, a pull request they opened stays the claim's, and a release comment or an overlap marker they wrote counts for nothing, so a lost answer never frees a finished claim and never keeps work from being told.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/docs/memory/MEMORY.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Memory index\n\nOne line per fact under docs/memory/: a dash, the fact's title as a link to its file, a colon and one line saying what it is. CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session; a fact's own file is read when its line says it matters. Add or correct a fact with the remember skill (.claude/skills/remember): one file, one line here, in the same pull request as the change it came from. This index stays under 200 lines and 25 KB, a limit of this project's own: an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing here or a line names a file that is not there.\n\n- [A copy runs the template's tests](a-copy-runs-the-templates-tests.md): every copy's required check runs the template's tests over the filled files, sharing perhaps off; tests/test_filled_copy.py runs a filled copy's check, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, so a test that holds only in the template fails there first.\n- [A new repository starts with the owner's commit](a-new-repository-starts-with-the-owners-commit.md): the push guard refuses a push to main in any repository a session's git touches, so the owner creates a new repository with \"Add a README\" ticked, or adds one from its empty page, and sessions bring everything else by pull request.\n- [A Project with several repositories runs no hooks](a-project-with-several-repositories-runs-no-hooks.md): GitHub's ruleset, the claim lock, the required check, /board and the claim skill still hold; the push guard, the board at session start and the Symphony-Usage trailer do not, so keep one repository per Project.\n- [A template copies no settings](a-template-copies-no-settings.md): what \"Use this template\" leaves to the owner (one run of scripts/setup-repo.sh, the token from claude setup-token, or scripts/crowd.py setup for a crowd repository) and what an organization on Team or above can set once for every repository it makes.\n- [Agents](agents.md): agents are Claude Code subagent files under .claude/agents/ with .github/symphony/agents.json, imported by pull request (tools/agents.py, agents.yml), run in sessions, Actions and as workers on the tokens accounts.json gives their names, and measured as they are by coordbench --agent-file and a flowbench stage's agent; docs/agents/AGENTS.md.\n- [An auto-merge starts main's workflows only when the App armed it](auto-merge-starts-no-workflow.md): from beethoven-qa 7.0.0 the kit's automerge.yml arms with the SymphonyBoard App's token, so its merge starts main's push workflows; a pull request that changes a workflow file, or one armed while the App's token cannot be made, is still armed with the workflow token, whose merge starts none and leaves its issue open: close it by hand and Run workflow what must follow.\n- [Artifacts are kept as files](artifacts-are-kept-as-files.md): the artifacts in the claude.ai gallery of each of the owner's accounts live in artifacts/ of their repository, less what the export's screen holds back for the owner (SchwingNet and spine work here, Symphony and Beethoven in symphony-template), a page as index.html with its files, a Docs document as index.md, one row each in artifacts/README.md; each new artifact and each other account's are added the same way, an account's all at once by symphony-template's /export-artifacts, and its artifacts-grid.yml keeps both repositories' folders in ~/claude-artifacts on the grid for every account (the owner, 2026-10-22).\n- [beethoven-qa in a Symphony repository](beethoven-qa-in-a-symphony-repository.md): a repository made from the template carries beethoven-qa 6.0.0 or later beside Symphony (the claim protocol on one side, the contracts gate and code standards on the other, a draft with a claim file satisfying both); the kit hook is beethoven-qa-session-start.sh since the old name collided with the board hook; check.sh runs the copy's vendor-check only under pixi; the steps are docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md.\n- [Beethoven's worker and Claude projects](beethoven-worker-and-claude-projects.md): a project reaches the worker only through its own Work locally, never through the worker's link, so Beethoven 21.0.0 (beethoven-extension#17) opens the project's page named in Settings; the research is docs/research/beethoven-worker-opening.md, with what is not yet measured (the successor's resume, --spawn worktree); a merge to beethoven-extension's main can be a Chrome Web Store release (CWS_ON_PUSH).\n- [bordercall on the grid](bordercall-on-the-grid.md): the word `bordercall <pilot|full>` runs bordercall over the census's existing SPINEPS masks counted from C2, and `bordercall complete` (SchwingNet #470, one CPU job on the owner's count of 2026-10-29 15:22 UTC) runs its matcher over those masks for the gold tier 3 completion, the report with no data-root path (no C2, no number; never TotalSegmentator, never a re-run; ribs from the Moller table as the independent arm) on the owner's count of two CPU jobs, the copy under grid/bordercall/ held to its VENDORED record by check.sh; `update`, the status word, the pilot, then `full`; docs/research/bordercall-corpus.md.\n- [Carried folders](carried-folders.md): files a cloud session holds reach the grid as a commit on a private branch, a record grid/carry/<id>.txt and the word `fetch <id>`, which git lands on the login node, checked against SHA256SUMS, with no job; the first is the project symphony_4's shared folder (2026-10-28).\n- [CI runs Python 3.12](ci-python-is-3-12.md): the checks run on GitHub's 3.12, so tools and tests use no API newer than that; test with python3.12 where it exists.\n- [Claim branches push by name](claim-branches-push-by-name.md): a claim branch is made with --no-track, a clone made for one branch is told to fetch every branch, every push names its branch and the lease the tip pushed, since git's push.default, a single-branch clone and a lease after a fetch each sent a push somewhere the push guard cannot see.\n- [@claude runs on the main account's subscription](claude-mentions-run-on-the-main-accounts-subscription.md): mentions run .github/workflows/claude.yml on the token from `claude setup-token`, spending plan limits, never an API key; in crowd mode it never runs.\n- [Code owners are users or teams](code-owners-are-users-or-teams.md): GitHub never asks an organization for review, so template-init writes the owner, or in an organization's copy the person who made it, into .github/CODEOWNERS; check with the codeowners/errors API.\n- [Crowd mode](crowd-mode.md): scripts/crowd.py sets a repository up for volunteers on their own Claude accounts; the variable CLAUDE_CROWD, the required check's crowd guard and queue.yml then keep the Claude workflows off, Claude secrets out, checkpoints out of commits and the queue to maintainers.\n- [Dataset mail is read with Junk Email](dataset-mail-is-read-with-junk-email.md): a read of the [XR/CT Imaging Research] mail searches Junk Email for returns too, since the ReXGradient-160K bounce of 2026-10-24 sat there unread; the connector cannot open a bounce's transcript.\n- [DICOM banks and leak checks](dicom-banks-and-leak-checks.md): DICOM decodes only in the train environment; NIH ChestX-ray14 is checked against Academic Torrents piece hashes since NIH gives no checksum; the leak checks run as grid/run-leaks.sh after stage 3; tests needing optional dependencies go under tests/ as unittest, since the gate refuses skips under tests/schwingnet; a CrossHair counterexample on catalog.changed_keys once flaked on CI.\n- [DRR domain-gap study](drr-domain-gap.md): tools/domain_gap.py, calibrated separation tests of FleXray's and other synthetic DRRs against the held fluoroscopy and film corpora with the replication test of FleXray's renderer and the segmenter block; bench flexray_drr_seg makes MOOSE's masks (flow drr_seg), bench flexray_drr_gap runs prepare, an array of 16 tasks on any GPU claiming units and a merge, each its own drr_gap flow run under the frozen PREREG.md with DEVIATIONS.md, the merge's writing the CLAIM report, every step a timestamped run log that status, the bench report and the CLAIM report show; results only in docs/research/drr-domain-gap/README.md.\n- [Every deep learning job runs on a launched flow](every-deep-learning-job-runs-on-a-launched-flow.md): the owner's rule of 2026-10-29: a flow flows/launch.py starts, listed in flows/registry.txt with its frozen PREREG (exit 90 when missing or moved, a Covers: line for the parts it covers), the CLAIM and REFORMS report checked in end (flows/stage.py) and a progress page under <datastore>/progress/, shown by status and drawn off the grid by tools/progress_page.py; the sweep, the FluXray reproduction's edit and stats, and the DRR study with its MOOSE step keep it.\n- [Evaluation and W&B](evaluation-and-wandb.md): witness frames chosen by schwingnet.witness and frozen by `flow.py record --freeze`, drawn at every checkpoint from epoch 0 (validation by the trainer, test over every kept epoch by the evaluator); W&B online where the grid's key is found (WANDB_API_KEY, ~/.wandb/token.env, ~/.netrc), offline pushed by the flow after each step otherwise, loud when not pushed; the page docs/research/evaluation/README.md from the recorded runs, never on Pages.\n- [Flux realism](flux-realism-edit.md): no CSAIL-trained realism model exists, FluXray is zero-shot FLUX.2 klein-9B over MOOSE DRRs; the reproduction on ENHANCE.PET CTs at FluXray's poses is frozen in docs/research/flux-realism/PREREG.md and runs as RealismFlow through flows/launch.py, edit then stats, with its CLAIM and REFORMS report and progress page; the count of 2026-10-29 is three fetches (sent 02:57 UTC, never again) and two GPU jobs, lora and eval refused until a count and PREREG of their own; resumed 2026-10-29 12:27 UTC, the review's 17 findings fixed in #456, the two GPU jobs sent after it merges.\n- [From 3D to X-ray](from-3d-to-x-ray.md): the programme carrying the segmented CTs to X-ray (docs/research/xr-from-3d/, issues #56 to #70, #127 and #135; the benchmarks to beat, FluoroSAM and Zeng 2024 among them, in docs/research/benchmarks-to-beat.md and docs/research/fluoroscopy-list-prior-art.md; the lumbosacral-to-C2 feasibility in docs/research/lumbosacral-to-cervical-extrapolation.md): the corner annotation format, anomalies called on CT as the counting prior's odds, settings chosen on validation only, the covariate census, COBRA non-commercial.\n- [Field metrics](field-metrics.md): .github/workflows/metrics.yml writes aggregates only (no name, title or text) to the branch metrics with GITHUB_TOKEN; a maintainer's own claude -p adds App F counts; a breakdown by work setting (one person's several sessions apart from several people) and in pairs; what the work cost, from sessions' Symphony-Usage trailers and the Claude workflows' usage artifacts, and seven alone-versus-parallel ratios, over 1 where parallel did better; PROFILE.json beside it sums the Symphony-Profile lines and the runs' profiles the same ways, unread by the collector; the switch is .github/symphony-metrics or SDLC_METRICS=off; the release marker and the board's words it counts.\n- [Grid jobs go through hpcworker job submit](grid-jobs-go-through-hpcworker-job-submit.md): every grid job is queued by grid/hpcw.sh through hpcworker's `job submit` with a class (batch for CPU, gpu for GPU), checked with --check first, no partition, QoS or output line in any .sbatch header; logs are in hpcworker's log folder, live jobs still read from squeue (issue #77).\n- [How this repository works](how-this-repository-works.md): the repository is the shared plane for every Claude account and session; what lives where.\n- [Issue dependency graphs](issue-dependency-graphs.md): GitHub holds the epic hierarchy and the \"blocked by\" dependencies natively and draws no graph, Linear draws none at issue level and Jira only on Premium; the recommendation of 2026-10-17, awaiting the owner's choices, is native blocked_by written by the coordinator and a Mermaid section drawn by the board, with Linear as an optional one-way tier (docs/research/control-board-graph.md).\n- [Labels and words that start a Claude run](labels-and-words-that-start-a-claude-run.md): epic starts coordinator.yml's plan unless the body holds `<!-- team:plan -->` first; todo, blocker and worker:<name> start workers.yml; \"@claude\" in an issue, a comment or a review starts claude.yml; goal starts nothing, and experiment.yml and figure.yml answer only their forms' issues; so a session marks an epic before labelling it and puts none of the others on without the owner's word.\n- [Latency and the recorded sweep](latency-and-the-recorded-sweep.md): task #13: no latency number without ONNX parity for the same file, the 97 ms budget, latency/<arm>.json and evaluate/latency.json in the run directory, truth boxes in v1 sweeps, the grid jobs in grid/run-latency-sweep.sh, each network part since 2026-10-29 the launched flow latency (flows/latency.py) under the frozen latency-and-sweep/PREREG.md; docs/research/latency-and-sweep/.\n- [Literature on the grid](literature-on-the-grid.md): the word `literature <list>` fetches a reading list of grid/literature/ onto the grid under the size rule with the Elsevier key and token the key route wrote (grid-accounts.yml key elsevier, s2), into a 0700 landing that only the owner's account and its grid sessions read, Elsevier's files only on the owner's word; the report names every paper not got; docs/research/literature-on-the-grid.md.\n- [Living docs](living-docs.md): the lessons under docs/learn/ run in the check (tools/living_docs.py test), their Colab notebooks are generated and must match (`notebooks`, then commit), every module needs a docstring for the Reference page, and docs.yml, run only on the owner's call since 2026-10-25, publishes the site with Pages once SYMPHONY_DOCS is pages (members only, the owner's choice of 2026-10-20).\n- [Mark a pull request ready on its own](mark-a-pull-request-ready-on-its-own.md): mark it ready in a call of its own after the body's edit and the claim file's deletion, since a draft's run that starts last fails the protocol check (#185).\n- [Morphometry on the grid](morphometry-on-the-grid.md): the word `morphometry <set>` runs bordercall's morphometry engine, pinned under grid/bordercall/ (ee495d5 since 2026-10-30), over the gold tier 3 masks: enhance, every ENHANCE.PET label map, and verse, VerSe's 374 int16 copies and TotalSegmentator's admitted subjects stacked to int16, once each on the owner's count of 2026-10-29 and once more on his of 2026-10-30 14:33 UTC (MORPHOMETRY_RERUNS, the old reports and rows kept); `morphometry covariates` joins the rows with each person's sex and age once both sets name one code stamp, each join checked against the earlier ones; reports carry aggregates alone.\n- [Notebooks](notebooks.md): drive files held together as a notebook, a private repository pointing at them by id and never moving them, with research, drafts and references.json from a citation manager's BibTeX or RIS export, citations into Word as tracked Word or Zotero fields (tools/docx_cite.py); tools/notebook.py and the notebook skill; Elsevier text and token private, never public; docs/research/notebooks.md.\n- [The Outlook digest](outlook-digest.md): progress and decisions as an Outlook actionable message answered with one press or a reply inside Outlook (tools/outlook_digest.py, sending nothing yet); Wayne State's Files consent covers no mail, so the owner's chosen route, self-sent from the owner's own mailbox, waits for C&IT's Mail.Send and Mail.Read consent; docs/research/outlook-digest.md.\n- [Profiling sessions](profiling-sessions.md): tools/profile.py reads a session's transcript and gives what it spent as counts alone, context call by call, tool calls, reads and re-reads, time, and a session's commits carry them since its last as a Symphony-Profile line, which collect sums per pull request into PROFILE.json, MCP tools folded to one name, and tools/profile_report.py writes the programme's report.md from its committed records; the research programme under docs/research/beyond-cooperation/ measures a way of working with it before changing it, names failures as MAST does, and no run that spends tokens starts without the owner's word.\n- [Protocol changes come from symphony-protocol](protocol-changes-come-from-symphony-protocol.md): the claim protocol is changed in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, whose sync pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` brings each release, once the owner has approved it, to OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template, while a repository made from the template takes each release tag by its own workflow, protocol.yml; never edit tools/sdlc_protocol, which scripts/check.sh holds to the hashes in tools/VENDORED and CI to the library's own files; the template vendors 0.5.0, merged in the library and waiting for the owner's approval, so until it is published the sync's 0.4.1 pull request is a downgrade to close, never to turn green.\n- [The research loop](research-loop.md): research.yml works the best-ranked unheld claims item every four hours with full command of grid work (the owner, 2026-10-24); RESEARCH_LOOP off stops it, RESEARCH_RUNS_PER_DAY caps it; the session can start no workflow, its grid requests run after it from a job whose token it never holds.\n- [Run the checks in a throwaway copy](run-the-checks-in-a-throwaway-copy.md): scripts/check.sh runs the lessons of docs/learn/ as commands in the working copy (tools/living_docs.py test); on 2026-10-20 it left the owner's clone with a blank git identity, on a branch claude/7 and with six stray items at the root, so run it in a scratch copy or let CI run it, and run the reading steps (check_docs, living_docs check, one test module, reproduce_table check, sources check) by hand in the clone.\n- [Runs are named by their code](runs-are-named-by-their-code.md): tools/run_code.py's digest of the files a run executes, not the commit, names flow runs, sweep states and launches, so a cut run carries on after a merge that changes none of them; a file a run newly reaches goes into its list or LEFT_OUT.\n- [SWE-bench runs from GitHub and PyPI](swe-bench-runs-from-github-and-pypi.md): the task repository SWE-bench/swe-bench-tasks, compiled by `swebench dataset build`, scores an instance where huggingface.co is unreachable; never --task-repo, which rebuilds the image; the images pull through mirror.gcr.io; OpenSpineConsortium/flowbench builds on it.\n- [Symphony versus competitors](symphony-versus-competitors.md): the comparison of 2026-10-16 is docs/research/symphony-versus-competitors.md (and a Claude Doc); its three claims are better by design and graded Inferred, no outcome is measured yet, and coordbench numbers stay out until the owner lifts the hold; the site's /symphony/compare.html sets the general powers beside familiar tools in seven kinds of work, and /symphony/use-cases.html gives the use cases (docs/research/symphony-compared-by-domain.md).\n- [The anomaly pack](the-anomaly-pack.md): stage 3 of the SPINEPS to FleXray route packs the selection as two whole-volume FleXray packages under the landing flexray_pack (schwingnet_spine for masks complete from C2 to the sacrum, schwingnet_spine_partial for the rest), every mask rewritten to the 28 canonical ids by tools/nifti_label.py, the existing relabelled SPINEPS copies through spineps_ids.py check --strict and never a segmenter run, countable measured per CT, uncounted and g3_excluded rows packed and trained on by nothing, the validation draw under data/splits/anomaly-validation.json, the admission before any byte and no HOLD (docs/research/spineps-anomaly-route.md, section 5).\n- [The control panel](the-control-panel.md): the site's /symphony/control/ drives a Symphony repository through GitHub's API as the signed-in person (the board, tasks, @claude, workers, pull requests, workflow runs) and /symphony/cost.html prices the work against the alternatives, unsorry first; Sign in with GitHub first (the App's user token through a stateless Worker), a pasted fine-grained token the fallback; it took no Claude token until the owner dropped that rule on 2026-10-18 for the Tokens page's Add a slot box, which saves a pasted token as an organization secret through the App; its assistant is an issue labelled assistant that claude.yml answers read-only in ten turns with .github/symphony/assistant.md appended, and the Ask Claude bar at the console's foot asks it and offers the panel actions its answer names as buttons the person presses (site #28, 2026-10-18; docs/research/ask-claude-bar.md); the research note is docs/research/control-panel.md.\n- [The drive organizer](drive-organizer.md): a whole drive read into a catalog on the branch drive-catalog, Claude proposing projects as epics whose tasks carry proposed and no todo until a person runs accept, which adds the folders to the sync, and the sync's run starts the tasks once the files are in; private repositories only, nothing scheduled; tools/file_digest.py reads any file as text; a session does it through the Microsoft 365 connector too (tools/drive_connector.py, the drive-organize skill), moving files only on the owner's go and undoably; Wayne State approved the Folders app for native Graph access on 2026-10-19, confirmed live 2026-10-20, so the drive is read only through Graph under the owner's sign-in and the connector walk is retired (docs/research/onedrive-direct-graph.md); docs/research/drive-organizer.md.\n- [The data catalog](the-data-catalog.md): the frame catalog of task #10 is a read-only versioned SQLite store (catalog-NNNNNN-<hash>.sqlite under versions/, 0444, VERSIONS.jsonl and CURRENT; unchanged bytes make no version) built from content-hashed shard manifests and data/registry/, with the person-keyed split under data/splits/; the data root lists sources alphabetically, tools/ingest.py turns a bank into a shard, split-check holds every version to the REFORMS split rule; images stay on the grid, Metaflow steps read a version by path and pass its hash; tools/catalog.py, docs/research/data-catalog.md, docs/research/dataset-organisation-at-scale.md.\n- [The dataset is obtained again from data/reproduce](the-dataset-is-obtained-again-from-data-reproduce.md): the manifest (211 sources) and the fetch code are byte-for-byte copies of spinesurg-ct-nnunet's data_platform at bf1432f with their SHA-256; docs/research/datasets-reproduce.md maps every registry record to its manifest records with acquisition, origin links, fetch command, checks and grid path (tools/reproduce_table.py); scripts/check.sh fails when page, map and copies disagree.\n- [The experiments panel](the-experiments-panel.md): chapter 35's figure from every recorded run under docs/research/ (the data input, every block spelled out with version 1 where a run named none, the loss by epoch, accuracy with intervals, the model, the reproducibility record, W&B), filters and a correlation view by block, and a Run a new combination panel whose request is a prefilled issue labelled experiment (or the Experiment form, or an `experiment:` line in the Ask box's answer), answered by experiment.yml with the command, the gate word `schwingnet-experiment-<issue>` and the owner's click and recorded on the branch experiments, where grid/gate.sh's word `experiment <issue>` runs it as one GPU job on the launched flow experiment under docs/research/experiments/PREREG.md; the artifact edition offers Run as me through the viewer's GitHub connector; `python3 tools/experiments_panel.py write` after recording a run, since the check fails on a stale page or form; the messages between a figure and the page that embeds it are docs/research/experiments-panel.md, section 4.\n- [The front door is one press](the-front-door-is-one-press.md): install the App, press Create on the form its Setup URL opens, merge; the run picks the repository by id, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise; the sweep is the fallback.\n- [The gate proves only what changed](the-gate-proves-only-what-changed.md): from beethoven-qa 9.0.0 gate.yml and the pre-push hook prove only the modules a change reaches since a base, and from 10.0.0 pyproject.toml is read by its keys, so an unproven entry added proves nothing (in CI the newest commit of main the gate passed on a push, in the hook where the branch left main), every module once a day; BEETHOVEN_QA_BASE=none proves all.\n- [The gold tier 3 CT dataset](gold3-ct.md): ENHANCE.PET, VerSe and TotalSegmentator's gold 3 CTs as one versioned store (tools/gold3_ct.py), with the private page Gold Tier 3 CT and its reader bench (artifacts/gold-tier-3-ct-xdgjjg/); since 2026-10-29 rule 3 admits every CT whose field shows C2's foot and the sacrum's top (tools/gold3_field.py): 1,738 gold 3: 1,558 ENHANCE.PET, 21 of VerSe's 374 series and 159 of TotalSegmentator's 1,939 subjects (5 on the CT's own extent the census read), 10 border cases, every row read from the releases; the grid census is the gate word `census gold3`; docs/research/gold3-ct/README.md; the completion's TotalSegmentator arm for C2 and the sacrum is the word `totalseg complete` (two jobs on the owner's count, the weights fetch and one GPU job, the owner's word of 2026-10-30 08:54 UTC).\n- [The grid runs a job through Beethoven](the-grid-runs-a-job-through-beethoven.md): .github/workflows/grid.yml runs one command on a WSU compute node through the runner chain that hpcworker's tools/grid_runner.sh serves as Slurm jobs (a private repository, labels self-hosted and grid, the output back as the job's log and an artifact); grid-gate.yml starts, reads or stops the chain over the ssh gate, a key whose forced command takes only those three words, which hpcworker's `grid_runner.sh --gate-setup` sets up in one command in the grid's shell; no Claude token goes there; docs/setup/grid.md has the owner's steps, docs/research/wsu-grid-and-github-actions.md the research.\n- [The organization map](the-organization-map.md): .github/symphony/organization.json, the curated record of how the organization's repositories depend on each other and touch (vendored libraries, reusable workflows, Apps, runners, data handed on) and the systems they reach, nodes with group, visibility and role, edges from, to, kind, what and by, held together by tools/organization_map.py check, drawn by the control panel's Organization tab beside what it reads live, changed by pull request when an interface changes; the epics, goals and blocked-by links of 2026-10-29 are docs/research/organization-map.md.\n- [The pipeline runs on Metaflow](the-pipeline-runs-on-metaflow.md): bank, catalog and sweeps are Metaflow flows that flows/launch.py starts, resuming the newest unfinished run of the same key first (the owner's rule of 2026-10-24); 02 and 03 hand every Slurm job to it.\n- [The plan ladder](the-plan-ladder.md): which GitHub and Claude plans unlock what, verified 2026-10-13 against docs/setup/free-or-paid.md; public repo means free Actions, the enforced gate and free scanning, a private one needs Pro or Team for the gate and an add-on for scanning; no payment method means a hard cap.\n- [The review record](the-review-record.md): every pull request marked ready, a person's or a session's, carries a section \"## Adversarial review\" in its body, held by tools/review_record.py in the required job check (drafts, bots, generated branches and pull requests opened before 2026-10-22 05:00 UTC excepted); CONTRIBUTING.md.\n- [The sample index](sample-index.md): grid/run-samples.sh builds samples.sqlite on the grid, every held file with its format, size, shape, DICOM header and sidecar rows and whether it is annotated, queried through the views sample, sample_meta and sample_header; only the aggregates in MANIFEST.md leave the grid; NHANES II is the large unannotated set for the screener; docs/research/data-manifest/README.md. The first run (2026-10-22, 140 groups, 7,008,826 files, 7.3 TB) is docs/research/data-manifest/MANIFEST.md.\n- [The size rule](the-size-rule.md): every fetch reads its exact size from its source before a byte lands (grid/fetch_size.py) and is refused whole when it would pass the CephFS quota of /rs/rs_grp_oschome after what the running fetches owe, with no floor (grid/size-rule.sh, the owner's rule of 2026-10-23); `bash ~/SchwingNet/grid/oschome-quota.sh`, the status word and wsuquota itself once grid/wsuquota.bash is sourced from ~/.bashrc print the room in wsuquota's layout; an attribute that is no count of 18 digits or fewer, or a use below the data root's, admits nothing, the volume's use then read by stat where the data root, a gigabyte or more on the volume's own mount, shows stat gives a folder's rbytes (#170), else as the sum of what its own entries hold, each read whole with no NUL and none a mount, as many as stat counts and the one that holds the data root no less than it (#172); every figure counted is read whole, owed before the room, the room twice a minute apart keeping the smaller, the second taking each of the volume's own entries at the larger of its two readings, a quota below the volume's, a data root outside it or a failed read admits nothing, and the tools stand in only under SIZE_RULE_STAND_INS=yes (#180); every quota above the volume up to the root of its mount counts too, the least room on the path binding, each folder's use read upwards from the volume's (#190), the readout showing each (#188); the bank word holds what its bank and shard may write as the set's admission while its job is live, twice the raw copy for png and dcm and six times for jpg, tif and bmp (#252); gdown, Kaggle and Redivis are sized too, and the X-ray chain's sets are each admitted in the fetch job with their banks, a landed set's bank room held for the bank job (grid/admit.sh, #163).\n- [The storage panel](the-storage-panel.md): chapter 22's interactive figure, built by tools/storage_panel.py from a committed snapshot of spine-datasets' feed (PLAN.json on its branch storage-feed; `fetch` refreshes it in docs.yml with the App's read token, `build` inlines it, `check` fails a stale page): the lab volume's and the home's use, every set with keep, remove, add and place, totals against the 20 TB target with the planner's refusals in words, and the selection it dispatches only through storage.yml, whose run opens a request pull request the owner merges; its host protocol is schwingnet.dispatch and schwingnet.read by postMessage, with the workflow's link and the string to paste where no host answers.\n- [The sync offers adopted repositories their files](the-sync-offers-adopted-repositories-their-files.md): sync.yml and tools/sync_adopted.py offer every adopted repository, on every installation, the library, the board's, tidy, metrics, Token and drive sync workflows with their modules as one pull request from symphony/sync, replacing only versions the template once held and adding only the Token and drive sync units.\n- [The synthesis keeps itself current](the-synthesis-keeps-itself-current.md): synthesis.yml sets the artifacts, notes and dataset records beside the stamp on every change with no Claude and keeps the synthesis issue saying what waits; its reads run on CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN (the owner's choice of 2026-10-23), at most SYNTHESIS_READS_PER_DAY a day (10, up to 50), into one pull request that waits for the owner's tap; what it writes is scrubbed, in the records' layout and the same on every hash seed.\n- [The X-ray fetch chain](the-xray-fetch-chain.md): grid/README.md is one paste that submits two afterany chains of reqp jobs, the X-ray sets (fetch, bank, ingest, catalog, split check) and the data platform's unfetched sources, each fetch held to the size rule; hf-get reads --include as a glob and an hf-token run that verified no file fails; CT-RATE and MIMIC-CXR-JPG are separate lines, the full NLST and PadChest's B2DROP share are fetch words on the owner's yes of 2026-10-22, and status opens with the space each set takes; a recipe field spelled \"to confirm\" stops its source; DICOM waits for pydicom.\n- [The token pool](the-token-pool.md): the branch pool holds each Claude slot's latest five-hour, seven-day and per-model (Fable) use from runs' rate_limit_event, pool.yml's probes and cswap via tokens.py usage, since a setup token cannot read the usage page; tools/pool.py pick is the auto mode over 32 slots, steered per slot (model, effort, fallback, resume, paused, id, owner) by SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_SETTINGS; issues a token worked carry token:<id> for the leaderboard; docs/research/claude-token-pool.md.\n- [The token wizard](the-token-wizard.md): python3 scripts/tokens.py stores each Claude account's subscription token as an Actions secret (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, the pool SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_1 to 8 with SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS) by running claude setup-token behind a pseudo-terminal that masks the token and piping it to gh, never shown or written, pasted only at a hidden prompt where no terminal can run claude; each slot's sign-in opens in a browser profile of its own through BROWSER, and cswap's account list names the slot, never its credentials; on Windows a copy in Credential Manager that show reads back, and share for a benchmark's repository; it also runs in a Codespace from the browser alone (scripts/codespace.sh); docs/setup/claude-tokens.md; the Tokens page's Add a Claude account mints on an Actions runner, the token leaving it only sealed (token.yml, tools/token_run.py).\n- [The textbook website](the-textbook-website.md): tools/textbook_site.py builds the Artifact Textbook into the members-only docs site (textbook/) whenever the owner runs Docs, never on a push or a schedule since 2026-10-25; public only after the filing, from an allowlist the owner approves (#196); each figure under docs/textbook/figures/ carries a JSON record of its source, shown with Explore and a Re-run issue that figure.yml answers (#215).\n- [The textbook artifact](the-textbook-artifact.md): the repository reaches claude.ai as a private front door artifact (tools/front_door.html, run by the published loader tools/front_door_loader.html, so a door merged to main needs no publish) reading main and the board live through the viewer's own GitHub connector, a custom connector with a GitHub App of the owner's; published from the owner's computer (`tools/textbook_publish.py local`, `--url` adopts a link), never from a thread; it replaced the staged reader of #294 on 2026-10-26; it serves only the owner's claude.ai organization and no reader browser-only (docs/research/reader-access.md).\n- [The textbook RAG](the-textbook-rag.md): Ask the book, a search over the textbook as blocks chosen by name (chunk, tokens, index, retrieve, answer, embed; src/schwingnet/rag.py, tools/rag.py ask, eval, check), its index built by the site and never committed, scored in the browser by a JS copy held to the bit by a shared fixture, and answered in the artifact through the viewer's own sample with no server and no key.\n- [The v1 flow](the-v1-flow.md): tools/flow.py runs the level stage locally or as Slurm jobs from a clean tree, writes RUN.json first and FINISHED.json last, after the ledger row and report, a start holding its folder by <run>/.lock, every number a ResultRow with its Wilson interval and stamp (tools/train.py, tools/evaluate.py); the run id follows from the code it runs (tools/run_code.py's row digest), the catalog and the configuration, and a run cut short resumes from its last epoch (#252); the dry run is docs/research/v1-demo/; on the grid it runs as the launched flow v1 under docs/research/v1-demo/PREREG.md; tools/ leaves sys.path before torch is imported, as tools/profile.py shadows the standard library.\n- [Training blocks](training-blocks.md): each part of training (normalise, augment, optimiser, schedule, sampler, loss weights, checkpoint metric, samples, architecture, denoise, and the prior, a frame's projected level map learnt by a training-only head, #228) is a block chosen with --block KEY=VALUE in tools/train.py and tools/flow.py, the names in src/schwingnet/blocks.py and the torch side in tools/train_blocks.py; none named is version 1's training to the bit; checkpoint.json records them and train.model_for rebuilds the model everywhere (#202, #207); --init starts a run from a parent checkpoint of the same architecture (#205); `tools/block_sweep.py parts`, `plan` and `ledger` are the optimisation loop, `check` and `cover` the smoke test of every combination (docs/research/experiment-framework.md); augment=strong is SyntheX's strong randomisation (#252).\n- [Training data licences](training-data-licences.md): every dataset classified by licence in data/licences.json, recipes under data/recipes/ cut a catalog for commercial or non-commercial networks, and every flow run writes PROVENANCE.json; only the owner loosens a class (tools/licences.py).\n- [VinDr is covered by HPR 2026-330](vindr-is-covered-by-hpr-2026-330.md): WSU IRB HPR 2026-330 (29 Sep 2026), the supplement to 2026-269, covers VinDr-SpineXR under its PhysioNet agreement; the spine-program handoff lines that call it pending or outside the determination are stale, and the owner holds permission and agreements for all the data (2026-10-14).\n- [Word revisions](word-revisions.md): a folder of Word documents is revised as tasks; tools/docx_review.py writes a review sheet and one task per document, queues new comments (papers.yml) and applies a worker's revisions as tracked changes; a connected Google Drive or OneDrive syncs both ways through drive-sync.yml (docs/research/drive-sync.md); several reviewers revise one document as authors of their own (docx_review.py panel, the lens-review skill, docs/research/lens-review.md).\n- [Workers and the coordinator](workers.md): one way of working from one person alone to several people, routines and workers in GitHub Actions, each a worker under its label worker:<name>; routines and workers in Actions are files under .github/symphony/workers/, added by the \"Add a worker\" form, and workers.yml runs those in Actions, dealt the open work once a run from one look; the coordinator releases idle claims and tells overlaps in the board's run, and plans epics in coordinator.yml.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/memory.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Shared memory\n\nThe shared memory is docs/memory/, imported into every session through the line `@docs/memory/MEMORY.md` in CLAUDE.md. A claude.ai Project's own memory is private to the account that made it, so anything every account should know goes here instead.\n\n- One fact per file under docs/memory/, named in kebab-case (`how-this-repository-works.md`), with a one-line description as the file's first line.\n- One index line per fact in docs/memory/MEMORY.md, in the form `- [title](file.md): one line`. MEMORY.md stays under 200 lines and 25 KB; the limit is this project's own, because an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing from the index or the index names a file that is not there.\n- A thread that learns a fact, or finds one wrong, adds or corrects it in the same pull request as the change it learned from. Memory never travels alone and never waits for a later pull request.\n- Never a secret, never a token, never a cookie or a key. The checks look for the shapes of common secrets and refuse them.\n- What holds only for one piece of work (what was done today, what is left) is a checkpoint (.claude/skills/checkpoint), not a memory.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/writing.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Writing\n\n- Plain prose that says why, in comments, commit messages, pull requests and documents alike. What the code does is read from the code.\n- Short functions; names that read as English.\n- No emoji in code, output or documents. ASCII and LF in everything under .claude/, docs/, tools/ and .github/, and in the dataset records under data/sources/, whose text the inventory page copies; prefer ASCII elsewhere.\n- No new dependencies: Python 3.12 or later with its standard library, plain bash, and GitHub's own actions and anthropics/claude-code-action, pinned to a major tag. A proposal to add one is an issue first.\n- Nothing refers to a file that does not exist.\n- In conversation, put a fact worth keeping on its own line tagged `Decision:` or `Constraint:`, so the checkpoint skill and `cswap checkpoint write` keep it word for word; the rest of a session is inferred and may be lost.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/research.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Research accumulates in docs/research/\n\nWhat a session finds out is kept in the repository, under docs/research/, where every account and every later session reads it: a reply, a status line, a Claude Doc or an artifact is read once, and a claude.ai Project's files belong to one account. A handoff under docs/checkpoints/ says where the research is and never carries it, so research never waits for a checkpoint and is never lost with one.\n\n- One file or one folder per topic, named in kebab-case. A programme, a question measured over time, is a folder with a README that says where it stands; a finished study, comparison, deep-research run or fact-check is one file. docs/research/README.md lists both, one line each, and the line comes in the same pull request as the file.\n- Every file opens by naming its source: the thread's title, the date, and the link of the Claude Doc, artifact or upload it came from where there is one. A doc or artifact a session cannot read from its container is listed in the README by link and never written down from memory.\n- Research lands in the pull request of the work it came from or, when the research is the work, in a pull request of its own, before the thread's checkpoint. A note stays here once its question is settled; a pull request that settles a finding for good may move it to docs/history/, the longer record, and a fact every session should know becomes a memory (.claude/rules/memory.md) that points here.\n- ASCII and LF, as the checks hold everything under docs/ to, and plain prose that says why (.claude/rules/writing.md); no secret, no token, no transcript and no tool output, as docs/history/ holds too, and the checks refuse the shapes of common secrets and any .events.json.\n\nContents of /home/user/SchwingNet/.claude/rules/board.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# The board comes first\n\nBefore any change in this repository:\n\n1. Fetch: `git fetch origin`. The session-start hook does this once; do it again before you push.\n2. Read the board and docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (how work is claimed). The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow; read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is. The session-start hook prints it at the start of every session; docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout.\n3. Check the claim on the issue you mean to take: a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, means another worker has it, and a `worker:<name>` label that is not yours, with none that is, means the issue is that worker's: pick another. An issue the labels give to several workers, you among them, is each of theirs, and the first to claim it holds it. An issue the board's Given to workers section gives you comes first, then an unclaimed blocker, then the Queue; never take an epic, which the coordinator plans, nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nYou work here as a worker under a name of your own (docs/agents/WORKERS.md): in a person's session, the person's GitHub login unless the environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; in a routine or a worker in Actions, the name its prompt gives. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Claim with the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) before the first change to any other file, and open the draft pull request at once.\n\nNever push to a branch another worker claimed. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim; after a rebase, your own claim branch takes `git push --force-with-lease=claude/<issue>:<the tip you last pushed> origin claude/<issue>` (the claim skill's step 7), the branch named so that the push forces nothing else and the tip so that it never forces over a push made since, and nothing else ever does. When the board comments \"main moved\" on your pull request, bring the default branch in: rebase on it and push again, or merge it in. An issue labelled blocker is claimed like any other, and nobody fixes it in parallel.\n\nA hook enforces the push rules before they can be broken: tools/guard_push.py, wired in .claude/settings.json, refuses a push to the default or board branch, a plain --force anywhere, a + before a refspec, which forces that ref as --force does, the refspec : and patterns with *, which move branches wholesale as --all, --branches and --mirror do, a push to a name that reads as a claim and claims nothing, such as claude/03 (a claim branch is claude/ and the issue's number in one to nine ASCII digits with no leading zero, docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md, Claim), --force-with-lease outside your own claude/ branch, and, where the copy carries the fence (the template since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, and with its tools/guard_push.py where the copy's own predates symphony-template#69), --force-with-lease onto a claude/ branch whose claim file on origin names another worker, or with a branch that carries no claim file for the issue, since a lease after a fetch is the one push git itself would take onto another worker's claim. A refusal is the rule working, not an obstacle; the answer is a pull request, a rebase or another branch, never a way around the hook. Branch protection on GitHub (docs/setup/github.md) stands behind it.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# {{PROJECT_NAME}}\n\nThis repository is a Claude project kept as files: its instructions, its goal, its shared memory and its board live here, and Claude Code reads them in every session, local or cloud. The repository is the source of truth for every Claude session and every account; a claude.ai Project only links it, its own fields stay blank, and Claude Code loads this file and what it imports at the start of every session, a Project's thread and a cloud session opened on the repository alike.\n\n## Read first\n\n- The goal, the scope and who owns what, loaded into every session by this line: @docs/project.md\n- How work is claimed, done in the open and finished, loaded the same way: @docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n- Who works here: every session, routine and worker in GitHub Actions is a worker under a name of its own, labelled worker:<name> (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). A session's name is the GitHub login of the person it works for, unless its environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; a routine's or a worker in Actions' is the one its prompt gives.\n- The board: the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow from the open issues, the claim branches and the pull requests. It lives on another branch, which no import reaches: read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} only describes it.\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board, check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n\n## Shared memory\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nA fact worth keeping is one file under docs/memory/ with one index line in MEMORY.md, added in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n## Conventions\n\n- The board first (.claude/rules/board.md): fetch, read the board (STATE.md on the branch `board`) and PROTOCOL.md, check the claim; never push to another worker's branch; never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. A hook refuses the pushes this rule forbids, and /board (.claude/commands/board.md) reprints the whole board mid-session.\n- Memory (.claude/rules/memory.md): one fact per file, a kebab-case name, a one-line description first, one index line; never a secret.\n- Writing (.claude/rules/writing.md): plain prose that says why, short functions, names that read as English, no emoji, ASCII and LF, no new dependencies; tag a durable fact in conversation as Decision: or Constraint: so a checkpoint keeps it.\n- Research (.claude/rules/research.md): what a session finds out goes under docs/research/, one file or folder per topic that opens by naming its source, with a line in docs/research/README.md, in the same pull request as the work and before its checkpoint; a handoff under docs/checkpoints/ points to it and never carries it.\n- Native first (the owner, 2026-10-20): before building a new feature or approach, check whether GitHub, Microsoft 365 or the device already does it natively, starting with the permissions listed in docs/research/microsoft-365-permissions.md (One request, 2026-10-20) and docs/research/device-permissions-for-a-person-like-claude.md; build only what no native feature covers.\n- Skills: .claude/skills/claim, .claude/skills/remember and .claude/skills/checkpoint are the three procedures, in the order a piece of work meets them, and .claude/skills/handoff writes the page the next account resumes from when the hook says the weekly limit is at 90 percent or the owner asks; .claude/skills/drive-organize (a OneDrive sorted into projects, read through Microsoft Graph under the owner's own Microsoft sign-in), .claude/skills/lens-review (several reviewers on one Word document), .claude/skills/notebook (drive files held together as a notebook in a repository of its own, never moved) and .claude/skills/export-artifacts (one account's claude.ai artifacts copied into artifacts/ here and in SchwingNet, and shared on request) are run when the owner asks.\n- The block at the end of this file, between the cswap markers, is rewritten by the checkpoint skill and by `cswap checkpoint write`; do not edit it by hand.\n\n## Checks\n\nRun `bash scripts/check.sh` before opening or updating a pull request. CI runs the same script as the check named \"check\", and the default branch takes only pull requests that pass it. The same check fails a pull request marked ready whose body has no section `## Adversarial review` (tools/review_record.py, CONTRIBUTING.md), so write the review into the body before marking it ready.\n\n## Session checkpoint (kept by cswap)\n\nRead `.claude/session.md` at the start of a session: it holds the completed work, the current state and the remaining tasks. Tag durable facts in conversation as `Decision:` or `Constraint:` so the next `cswap checkpoint write` keeps them.\n\n### Durable decisions\n- Prefer GitHub's native, free features; no API billing: Claude in CI runs on a subscription OAuth token.\n- A protocol change is made in OpenSpineConsortium/sdlc-protocol and reaches every consumer by its sync once the owner approves its release: the release job waits in the environment release and tags nothing without that approval, and the sync then vendors the approved tag, never main's head (ecosystem.json, release.yml, sync.yml).\n- coordbench measures what CooperBench measures, as CooperBench 63b9d44 does, differing only with a stated reason (PARITY.md); model-judged measures run only on purpose, never with ANTHROPIC_API_KEY.\n- Crowdsourcing: each contributor works under their own Claude account and the project never holds anyone's credential; crowd mode (scripts/crowd.py, CLAUDE_CROWD) enforces it.\n- Field metrics: no hosted server; on by default, disclosed, one switch off; aggregates only, a repository only as a salted hash; the failure-mode judge runs client side.\n- Open core (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"publicize it to make the installation process minimal, maintaining updates trivial\"): the runtime, the protocol and the plugin are public, so setup is simple and copies never fall behind; the hosted parts, the control panel, the account pool and its switching, the QA reports and the analytics, are the paid SymphonyBoard App; the template's private status is no longer counted on, though until the runtime carries its files the template stays internal and is adopted through the public symphony-start, which holds no file of it; the protocol and the plugin stay MIT, the runtime may take a source-available licence (ELv2 or BSL) once a lawyer has read it, what was published under MIT stays MIT, and a paid product runs on each customer's own Claude subscription, never on pooled accounts.\n- The collector's salt is derived from the App's private key, with SDLC_METRICS_SALT as an optional override; rotating the key or changing the override gives every repository a new hash.\n- The metrics workflow runs at 03:43 UTC, before the collector's 04:17 read, and on a merge that changes the switch; it never replaces a branch metrics that holds other files and never runs in a fork.\n- Adoption's mode existing takes the protocol page from sdlc-protocol's spec/PROTOCOL.md at the vendored commit, and the App's tokens are minted account-first, so the public run log says nothing about a repository a stranger names.\n- A session merges the project's pull requests once their check is green, as the owner asked and the earlier sessions did; the rulesets require no review.\n- The files an adoption brings are offered under the MIT License, in sdlc-start's LICENSE; the adoption copies no LICENSE into anyone's repository.\n- An adoption's repository settings are applied by a second, setup-only App, Symphony Setup (Administration write, Metadata read, public, no webhook), which the adopter installs on the one repository for the one run and uninstalls afterwards; installing it is the consent; the main App holds Administration only for the control panel's Share tab, which invites a collaborator as the signed-in person (the owner's choice of 2026-10-18, the Apps' privacy statement version 17), and never uses it to change a setting.\n- The owner's own several Claude accounts may be coordinated from one orchestrator of theirs, workers as claude -p subprocesses each with one account's token; the owner decided this knowing the terms-of-service question, and it does not change the rule for contributors' credentials.\n- The Polyphony rename comes after the setup App: the App first, then the repositories, then the user-facing identifiers; the package sdlc_protocol and the SDLC_* variables may stay.\n- Installing the App is the request: the install's redirect (the App's Setup URL) lands on the form with the installation filled in and one press of Create is the request; the run picks the installation's newest repository with no adoption yet, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise, and the form assumes the person knows what they are doing; the sweep (sdlc-start's sweep.yml) is the fallback for an installation that never reaches the form; a webhook, if ever hosted, dispatches sweep.yml for one installation with a short grace and adds no logic.\n- A repository given by ids, from the sweep or the form, is never named on the public side (no log, run input, step output, job summary or issue): the run resolves and masks the name and answers \"your repository\"; a guessed installation number gets one answer whatever is wrong.\n- The product is Symphony; the rename is two tiers: tier one, merged, renames the product in prose and what a merge can carry (the form's title, the branch symphony/adopt, the switches .github/symphony-metrics and .github/symphony-adopt, the plugin symphony); tier two, six pull requests merged on 2026-10-11 once the owner had renamed the repositories and the Apps, flipping the repository names, the App slugs, the marketplace's name and the sync branch symphony-protocol/sync; the package sdlc_protocol, the SDLC_* variables, the VENDORED record's first token, the plugin folder plugins/sdlc, the schema ids, the check run name sdlc/protocol and the readers of the old names stay.\n- Symphony for Chrome links the new names, since it ships after the rename; the site links the current names with a flip pull request, since it is live now.\n- The Apps are named SymphonyBoard and Symphony Setup, slugs symphonyboard and symphony-setup, with no OpenSpine in the name: GitHub refuses an App name that matches an existing account (Symphony, Symph, Symph0ny) or another App (Symphony Dev); the main App's homepage is https://openspineconsortium.com/symphony/.\n- One press adopts one repository: the repository the form names, else the newest the installation reaches with no adoption yet (scripts/resolve.py, LIMIT 1), the person pressing Create again for the next; on 2026-10-11 one press from an installation on all of an account's repositories, with \"Merge the pull request for me\" ticked, merged the adoption into eight before the run was cancelled.\n- The extension's Migrate button and the post-Create banner carry the repository through GitHub's own install link, /installations/new/permissions?target_id=<owner id>&suggested_target_id=<owner id>&repository_ids[]=<repository id>&state=OWNER/NAME; GitHub returns state on the Setup URL, and the form's Repository field has the id state so it arrives filled in; the extension's note symphony.migrate (30 minutes) chains the Setup App's install after SymphonyBoard's from the form page and, on an installation's settings page reached from Migrate, offers the Setup App's install.\n- Both Apps are required on the form's two paths: the named path refuses while Symphony Setup is not installed on the repository, with its preselected install link; the installation path adopts only a repository the Setup App reaches; the sweep adopts without it.\n- The two GitHub Apps have one privacy statement, PRIVACY.md in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-start, rendered by the site at /symphony/apps-privacy.html and linked from the Apps' descriptions; the extension keeps its own (version 2 with the migrate note).\n- The Chrome Web Store release kit is its own private repository, OpenSpineConsortium/chrome-store-release-kit, called as a reusable workflow at main by every extension repository of the owner's; symphony-extension releases through it on each push to main, and the store upload waits on the five CWS_ secrets and CWS_ON_PUSH.\n- The main App's logo is GitHub's mark in the icon's warm white with the orange baton, the Setup App's a gear, the ring and tile unchanged; the owner accepted the risk under GitHub's logo policy.\n- An adopted repository carries the GitHub topic symphony: the adoption run sets it through the Setup App and scripts/setup-repo.sh adds it from a clone, and the extension reads the repository page's About sidebar for it, with no API call, to show \"Ready for Symphony\" (a link to the board) in place of \"Migrate to Symphony\"; a repository adopted before the topic existed gets it by hand under About.\n- The installs are chained on GitHub's side: SymphonyBoard's Setup URL is Symphony Setup's install page and Symphony Setup's Setup URL is the form, both with Redirect on update, so an install from the README or the App's page reaches both Apps and the form with the Setup App's number; the extension (1.3.0) steps from Symphony Setup's account picker to the page preselected for the repository its note names; whether GitHub carries the state through both hops is tested by the owner once.\n- The form's Kind and Merge are required dropdowns with a default, Auto and \"Merge the pull request for me\" preselected (an optional dropdown opens on \"None\", and GitHub's form schema gives a checkbox no default), the sharing opt-out the one box left; the kinds say what they are: the whole template makes the repository a Claude project, the protocol only adds the coordination protocol for the teammates' pull requests to existing code.\n- The extension opens the popup's links through the browser's own new-tab call, since Chrome opens a popup's plain link in the last ordinary window and a private window lost it; and its step to Symphony Setup's preselected page covers the permissions page GitHub lands on directly for an account with no organization.\n- The site's root is the lab page (what the lab is, the two features of one code base at the same time, the three aims, how the lab works, the products), with two figures drawn by tools/home_figures.py in a wide and a phone layout, and Beethoven is its own tab at /beethoven/ with its flow diagram and its children, between Symphony and Onboarding; below 900px the header opens its drawer and the figures take their phone layout.\n- From Claude Code, /symphony:new (symphony-marketplace 0.1.2) and docs/start/claude-code.md make a project: gh makes the repository, one preselected install link installs both Apps and lands on the form, the run merges, the Claude GitHub App goes on, and claude --cloud opens the session; a Project is made in claude.ai alone, belongs to one account and cannot be shared, so the repository is what is shared and another account joins with its own Project.\n- Beethoven 20.0.0 (2026-10-11, merged and live on 2026-10-12): an approved account works on every installation of the student's own; the access service keeps the ten most recently seen per account and sends the maintainer a notice with one Withdraw link and no Move link; one installation still serves one account; agreement 13, privacy statement 14.\n- A claude.ai Project holds one thing, the blank Symphony repository; after its first push two or more people and any number of sessions develop it through the same upstream, each on their own account and Project, as the template was designed to support.\n- No settings-as-code app (issue #18, closed 2026-10-11): settings come from Symphony Setup for one run, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables; docs/project.md records it under Out of scope.\n- A session merges any pull request once its checks are green, in Symphony's repositories, the site and the Beethoven repositories, on the owner's standing permission in perpetuity (2026-10-13: \"You have my permission in perpetuity to merge pr's\"), which replaces the word per pull request the site and Beethoven needed before and covers symphony-protocol's gate files; privacy pushes needed no ask already (\"all privacy pushes, im tired of approving them\"); while Symphony is in its development phase, with no users yet (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"you can also push releases without my permission and spine program\"), it covers spine-program's pull requests too, in place of its rule that only the owner merges there; approving a release stays the owner's, since no session can (Constraints).\n- The field metrics break failures out by work setting (symphony-template#49): each pull request that ended in the 30 days counts once, in one of eight settings (solo, one_session, one_person_sessions, several_people, several_people_and_sessions, undetermined, workflow_agents, automation), with its exposure (work_seconds, partners, landings_under, landings_before_opening, file_overlap) beside every failure count, and pairs at work at the same time counted by relation; a person is the pull request author's login and a session a whole line (Claude-Session, the claude.ai link, the footer, or Symphony-Session: <token> that a local or claude -p session writes); identities stay in the run's memory; the breakdown reads only after every existing key is measured and is left out when its reads would reach the board's reserve or GitHub fails one.\n- The Apps' privacy statement (symphony-start PRIVACY.md: version 3 of 2026-10-12 added what the work cost, version 4 of 2026-10-13 the Actions setting, version 5 of 2026-10-13 what the run reads to refuse a repository adopted already, its site page live since site#14) is the exhaustive list of what the collector keeps: a new shared key is listed there, and its site page is live, before the collector keeps it, and the template's change may follow in any order.\n- One way of working (symphony-protocol 0.4.0, symphony-template#56, 2026-10-12): whatever claims an issue is a worker labelled worker:<name>, a person's sessions by GitHub login or SYMPHONY_WORKER, a routine or a worker in GitHub Actions by a file under .github/symphony/workers/ added through the \"Add a worker\" form or by hand; account:, agent: and \"- Account:\" are still read; team mode and CLAUDE_TEAM, CLAUDE_WORKER and CLAUDE_MAX_ROUNDS are gone.\n- The coordinator lives in the repository: the board's own run releases idle claims (the labels off, then a comment with why, whose, what the branch holds and the plan, the labels put back when the comment fails, so a failed run is retried; symphony-protocol 0.4.1) and tells overlapping work once, on GITHUB_TOKEN; Claude in coordinator.yml plans epics into tasks for registered workers, settles blocked tasks, integrates finished epics and runs the coordinator prompt weekly; a coordinator routine is only for a repository with no Claude token.\n- What the work costs is recorded beside the work, with no routine, API key or server: a Symphony-Usage trailer on each session's commits and a symphony-usage-* artifact per Claude run in Actions; metrics.yml reads them (actions: read, at most 200 new a run, carried in the Actions cache) and writes economics and coordination, whose seven ratios are over 1 where parallel work did better (symphony-template#57; the collector keeps them, symphony-app#11).\n- One public runtime (the owner, 2026-10-13: \"i love this idea, especially if it would simplify the setup process\"): symphony-protocol, to be renamed symphony, holds all of Symphony's logic as a dispatcher workflow and a composite action that every adopted repository calls by a version through a few thin caller files (a whole-template copy from 145 files to about 38, a protocol-only one from 28 to 8); the background jobs follow v1 while the required check, the hooks and the Claude jobs run a reviewed pin; a release goes out only through an approved environment after a canary; it replaces the parked update route for copies, and claude-code-action's workflow validation and nested permissions are measured before it is built.\n- The update route for an existing copy is parked; the old test repositories are deleted and made again from scratch, to test the path a future user takes (the owner, 2026-10-13).\n- Symphony Setup and scripts/setup-repo.sh tick \"Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests\" in a whole-template adoption (symphony-start#36, symphony-template#59).\n- A protocol-only adoption brings the workers and the coordinator too, and registers a default worker unless a box opts out; the control panel is a site page with a small stateless sign-in service acting through the App's user token (the owner, 2026-10-13; not built yet).\n- Every pull request an autonomous worker makes passes a required QA gate with a report a person can read: two required checks pinned to GitHub Actions, check and qa, where qa runs from the default branch, reads the pull request only through the API and never runs its code, and folds in GitHub's own CodeQL, dependency review and secret scanning where the plan has them; beethoven-qa stays the Python design-by-contract lane (not built yet).\n- As few moving parts as possible: no server of ours; the Apps mint tokens with their webhooks off, and the webhook server, which neither manifest turns on, is to be deleted, since CI's protocol check covers it; every schedule is a fallback, never the only trigger.\n- The owner's accounts form one pool, one organization secret per account and a list of their names, picked by pure code in the library with records in the Actions cache, and then CooperBench runs on Symphony in coordbench's Actions, in an environment named bench (not built yet).\n- The protocol is checked by a validation harness like the jinja pilot, every interleaving of a few workers against an oracle written from the spec's text, with mutants proving the checks bite and a concolic lane run by hand (symphony-protocol validation/DESIGN.md); what it finds is fixed (the owner: \"if you find anything using those tests please fix them also\"): F1, F3, F5, F6, F10 and F11 in symphony-protocol 0.4.1, released and synced on 2026-10-13; for the rest the owner decided on 2026-10-13 the library fix on every finding, correctness over cost, no deadlock, no missed work, no orphaned worker, one pull request each in the order F7, F12, F4, F14, F9, F13, F2, F8, then the stuck claim, the last raising the version to 0.5.0 for the owner's approval; F7 (symphony-protocol#20) and F12 (#22) are on main and unreleased on 2026-10-14.\n- A copy runs the template's tests: tests/test_filled_copy.py fills copies, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, and runs their checks, so a test that holds only in the template fails in the template first (symphony-template#60, #61).\n- The plugin's push guard runs behind scripts/guard.sh, which refuses a command that may push when Python cannot run, since Claude Code reads a hook's failure other than exit 2 as no objection (symphony-marketplace#10, plugin 0.1.7, from spine-program's handoff).\n- Only people with write access queue work for a worker: the coordinator's tasks from an epic the owner marked keep todo, its tidy's proposals wait for the owner, and a todo anyone else adds starts no worker (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- GitHub stops a worker changing what steers workers (its own file, the prompts, .claude/, tools/, scripts/, .github/): the qa check runs from the default branch's copy, and code-owner review covers those paths (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- The Claude token in an organization secret is best one of an account nobody uses by hand, since the workers share its five-hour window; that is advice and never enforced, since some people have only one Claude account, whose workers then share its window with its owner (the owner, 2026-10-13).\n- At most 10 Claude runs a day per repository, the coordinator's included, set by a repository variable, and none while the default branch is red or after three failures in a row (2026-10-13, as recommended; Phase 3).\n- Each release of symphony-protocol, and later of the runtime, waits for the owner's OK: its release job waits in the environment release, whose required reviewer is the owner, and the sync carries only a published release, from the commit that raised main to its version (2026-10-13, from 0.4.1, whose release the owner approved that day, the first). For the development phase the owner allowed releases without that OK (2026-10-13); since no session can approve or publish one, each still waits for the owner's click until the owner says, in the owner's own words, to switch the approval off in release.yml, a change that merges only while no release waits, since GitHub refuses GITHUB_TOKEN a tag at a commit whose workflow files differ from main's.\n- The owner's handoff of 2026-10-14 (docs/checkpoints/2026-10-14/HANDOFF.md) sets how the sessions work: one thread per item, at most three at once, the owner told as each lands; one pull request per change from the thread's own branch, bash scripts/check.sh first where the repository has it, an adversarial review before each substantive pull request, merge once green on the standing permission, never approve, publish or change a symphony-protocol release or its approval gate without the owner's own words, never push to or force-push a default branch, no API key or Claude credential anywhere, say exactly what is missing and stop, ask before anything marked the owner's, every manual step with its exact link and what to click.\n### Constraints\n- The cloud environment blocks cooperbench.com, arxiv.org, cursor.com and docs.github.com; CooperBench's paper and data come from the GitHub repository cooperbench/website.\n- The cloud environment withholds CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN; coordbench reads COORDBENCH_CLAUDE_TOKEN, each account its own.\n- sdlc-template is internal to its enterprise; outsiders need a public copy or sdlc-start.\n- sdlc-start and its Actions logs are public: nothing of the template may be committed or printed there.\n- The cloud session's proxy refuses the Actions variables and secrets API paths, and the auto-mode permission classifier can refuse an outward write (a label through gh api, a repository attached mid-session); the GitHub tools' pull request and merge calls were allowed.\n- A cloud session's tools reach only its own account's sessions, environments and routines; there is no cross-account messaging, and Remote Control drives a session on the owner's own computer.\n- A cloud session's container has 4 CPUs, 15 GB of memory and about 30 GB of free disk; Docker's daemon is installed and starts on demand; a background command stops after two hours.\n- A GitHub App's installation on an organization has one repository list: the install link opened with \"Only select repositories\" replaces it; a repository of the organization's is added under Configure, and the main App stays on \"All repositories\".\n- GitHub hosts no always-on compute a webhook can reach: Actions runs on repository events, a schedule or an API call with a token, and a webhook delivery carries none; a newly added schedule may wait long for its first run; the proxy refuses deleting a branch and the organization's installations endpoints.\n- The sweep never touches the organization's own repositories, since the main App is on \"All repositories\" there; a heredoc on a command also fed by a pipe takes the pipe's place as stdin, so a workflow writes data to a file and reads it by path.\n- The proxy refuses renaming a repository and creating one in the organization, and the GitHub tools cannot create one either (403); both are the owner's; openspineconsortium.com is blocked too, so the site is checked by its own build and a local render.\n- A restart of the session's worker keeps /home/user, the scratchpad and the finished agents' outputs but kills running agents and workflows.\n- Parallel Bash calls in a cloud session share one working directory, so a cd in one call can land another call's commands in the wrong repository; calls that depend on the working directory run one at a time, or use git -C and absolute paths.\n- github.com draws the repository header with Primer React: the action list is ul[data-testid=\"repo-header-actions\"], the Star button a[data-testid=\"star-button\"], the owner's id and login are octolytics meta tags, and #repository-container-header and ul.pagehead-actions are gone; github.com/new needs a login to read.\n- docs.github.com and dagshub.com are unreachable from the cloud session (DNS and the proxy); GitHub's documentation is read from the github/docs repository's content files on raw.githubusercontent.com, and github.com's HTML pages answer 403 to curl.\n- GitHub's install link takes target_id (what GitHub's own buttons send), suggested_target_id and repository_ids[] (the OAuth-to-App migration guide; up to 100; omitted means all repositories) and state, which GitHub returns on the Setup URL after an install; an issue form fills the field whose id matches a query parameter.\n- The GitHub tools take the session's scoped repository names, the old ones (sdlc-*), and gh api on a renamed repository redirects to a repositories/{id} path the proxy refuses; git pushes to the old names redirect fine; a reusable workflow in a private repository needs Actions access \"Accessible from repositories in the organization\", and a run that failed at workflow resolution cannot be re-run (403), only pushed again.\n- A cd in one of several parallel Bash calls resets the shell's working directory for the others (\"Shell cwd was reset\"): every parallel call uses absolute paths or git -C.\n- A Project on claude.ai/code belongs to one user and cannot be shared, is on Pro and Max only, runs its threads in parallel (200 new threads a day), and no API or CLI makes one; the only HTTP way to start a cloud session is a routine's fire endpoint; claude --cloud starts a session from a clone's remote branch and bundles the clone when the Claude GitHub App is not on the repository.\n- In a cloud session a cd inside a Bash call can be lost to the shell's working-directory reset, so a commit meant for one checkout lands in another: every git command takes -C with the absolute path; and the proxy refuses git push --delete, so a stray branch (claude/new-skill on symphony-template, 2026-10-11) is the owner's to delete.\n- The site's build reads the sibling Beethoven checkouts as checked out: build master against the extension's main through a worktree named by BEETHOVEN_REPO while a branch is checked out, or the branch's texts reach master.\n- The auto-mode permission classifier counts a merge that a repository's rules leave to the maintainer as an unrequested commit unless the owner's words ask for that merge, and it refused a read right after such merges.\n- A cloud session's GITHUB_TOKEN runs tools/metrics.py collect on a repository the proxy reaches under its current name (OpenSpineConsortium/sdlc-template-test: 33 calls on 2026-10-12).\n- beethoven-qa and beethoven-agents carry Claude Code hooks under .claude/; a session doing other work reads them as files and registers no repository root for them.\n- The PR subscription tool can warn that the Claude GitHub App is not installed on a repository and still deliver that pull request's events.\n- Python's urllib sends a redirected request every header of the first, Authorization included, to whatever host the redirect names, so a download from GitHub (an artifact's zip) is asked without following the redirect and the storage address is fetched with no token.\n- In a single-branch (shallow) clone a pushed branch has no remote-tracking ref, so a stop hook calls it unpushed: add the branch to remote.origin.fetch, fetch, and set its upstream.\n- The site's build needs beethoven-extension, beethoven-vscode and symphony-extension checked out beside it (attached read-only, no repository root registered), and the site has no CI; a build moves every page's stamp.\n- \"Allow GitHub Actions to create and approve pull requests\" cascades from the enterprise to the organization to the repository, and a 409 on the repository's setting means a policy above it; a cloud session cannot read or set the repository's workflow permissions.\n- /install-github-app needs gh signed in as the account that may install on the repository.\n- The owner installed the Claude GitHub App on all of the organization's repositories on 2026-10-13; the subscription tool may still say it is missing under a repository's old name and deliver its events anyway, so a session also reads a pull request's checks itself before acting on silence.\n- /usage's dollars are a list-price estimate, not a bill.\n- The front door's adopt-run job runs template code beside both Apps' keys; the runtime's design splits the staging into a job that names no secret.\n- Python 3.11 and later refuse int() of a string of more than 4,300 digits with ValueError, so every reader of digits someone else writes (a branch name, a marker, a trailer) bounds them first, in ASCII digits (claims.number_in, usage.count_in).\n- On the owner's Windows machine gh and git fetch stall about 11 s per GitHub call on DNS, so they run through WSL there, and python3 may be the Windows Store's stub, which exits 9009 for every script.\n- A cloud session can neither approve a deployment nor publish a release: GitHub's \"Review pending deployments\" needs deployments: write, which the session's token lacks (403, 2026-10-13), and the session's proxy refuses creating, editing or deleting a release (\"not permitted for this session type\"); a release is made by release.yml's GITHUB_TOKEN once the owner approves its run, or by the owner by hand.\n- After a session's approval and release calls were refused, the auto-mode check refused related reads too (a docs fetch, a fetch of the consumers, a search of the session's own notes) as a bypass of the release gate; a change to the gate needs the owner's explicit words first.\n- git does not pass GIT_CONFIG_COUNT to the receive-pack and upload-pack it starts for a local remote, and git 2.55's automatic maintenance runs detached and races a test's cleanup, so a test that makes repositories sets gc.auto=0, gc.autoDetach=false, maintenance.auto=false, maintenance.autoDetach=false and receive.autogc=false both in the environment and in each scratch repository's config (symphony-template#63, symphony-marketplace#12).\n- The code scanning alerts API answers a session 403, so a CodeQL finding is read by running the CodeQL bundle locally: codeql database create --language=actions, then database analyze with codeql/actions-queries:codeql-suites/actions-code-scanning.qls.\n- gh api reaches a renamed repository under its new name once add_repo has attached it with push access (symphony-protocol, 2026-10-13); the others stay reachable through the GitHub tools under their old names.\n- The GitHub tools' merge took an expectedHeadSha that was not the pull request's head and merged anyway (symphony-template#63, 2026-10-13): a sha is copied from git, never retyped, and main's tree is checked after a merge.\n- gh api through the proxy refuses a repository's collaborators, invitations and Actions workflow-permission paths and the organization's secrets, but the GitHub tools' list_repository_collaborators answers; branch deletion is refused by both the delete-ref call and git push --delete (403, 2026-10-14), so a session deletes branches only through the owner's own machine (Remote Control).\n- GitHub marks a draft pull request merged, not closed, when another pull request merges the draft's branch into the default branch (symphony-template#71 and #72 through #73, 2026-10-14), so such a draft needs no closing.\n- GitHub runs no pull_request workflow on a pull request that has a merge conflict, so a conflicted draft's head shows no required check, only what other events start (symphony-protocol#21, 2026-10-14); the conflict is resolved first.\n- git 2.55.0 on GitHub's runners prints a UTC date in %cI as ...Z where git 2.43 prints ...+00:00, so a test compares the parsed instant and not the string (symphony-protocol#24's canary test, 2026-10-14).\n- A cloud session cannot read a GitHub App's settings pages (an App's permissions and description, the organization's installations, the Actions box, the secrets pages), so each click there is given to the owner with its exact link (the handoff of 2026-10-14).\n- The grid reaches GitHub from a compute node (a Beethoven session cloned public repositories over HTTPS on 2026-10-14), and a private repository such as spine-program needs the student's own token, which Claude never handles.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/docs/project.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# {{PROJECT_NAME}}: goal and settings\n\nThis file is the project's goal and the settings every account's thin Project shares. It is the one place where the goal is written: CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session, and the claude.ai Projects carry no copy of it. It changes by pull request like everything else.\n\n## Goal\n\nNot yet written. One paragraph: what this project makes, for whom, and what done looks like. The owner writes it, or asks a session to, when ticking the \"Set up this project\" issue's box for this file, and merges it by pull request.\n\n## Scope\n\nIn scope: the areas this project owns, each in one line; docs/agents/OWNERSHIP.md maps them to workers.\n\nOut of scope, by decision: what this project does not do, each with the reason, so nobody proposes it again.\n\n- A settings-as-code app (github/safe-settings, or the Repository Settings App reading .github/settings.yml), decided no on 2026-10-11 (issue #18): it is a dependency with admin rights on every repository it covers, and a server of our own in one case, for settings that Symphony Setup applies for one run from the front door, scripts/setup-repo.sh from a clone, and organization rulesets, secrets and variables for what an organization makes.\n\n## Repositories\n\n- {{REPO}}, this one: the shared plane for every account and every session; the default branch is {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}.\n- Other repositories this project reads or uses, four, each owned by the OpenSpineConsortium organization, whose owners merge to its default branch:\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, public: the claim protocol as a Python library with no dependencies (the board, the protocol check, team mode's clerk, the push guard's rules and the merge gate). It is vendored here under tools/sdlc_protocol, with tools/VENDORED recording the version, the commit and the sha256 of every copied file, and scripts/check.sh fails when the copy differs from that record, or, on CI, which clones the library, from the library's files at that commit. A change to the rules is made there, never here: once the owner has approved and published a release there that carries it, its sync workflow opens the pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template with that release's copy, and the template's check decides. A repository made from the template is not on the sync's list; its own .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each new release in instead, weekly, as a pull request from a branch of the same name, or as an issue linking that pull request where GitHub Actions may not open one. By hand it is `python3 <symphony-protocol>/scripts/vendor.py <this repository> --consumer OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template` and a pull request. Its ecosystem.json is the one list of which repository takes which modules, and its ECOSYSTEM.md, generated from that list, maps all five repositories.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/coordbench, public: the playground and benchmark that compares ways of coordinating Claude Code agents on CooperBench's tasks, this protocol among them; its sdlc arm runs on the library's merge gate, vendored under coordbench/vendor. It began as experiment/ on OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template's branch claude/10.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-marketplace, public: the Claude Code plugin marketplace with one plugin, symphony, which brings the skills, /board and the hooks to a repository not made from this template. .claude/settings.json names the marketplace and enables nothing, since this repository wires the same hooks itself.\n  - OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-app, private: the GitHub App SymphonyBoard (slug symphonyboard). Its installation token lets template-init's first commit start the Checks and the Board, which a push with GITHUB_TOKEN cannot, and the symphony-protocol sync opens its pull requests in every consumer with it; its webhook server, where it is run, posts the protocol check as a check run of its own, sdlc/protocol.\n\n## Model and effort\n\nEvery account's Project uses the same model and the same effort, so the work reads the same whoever did it. Record the choice here and set it in each Project's settings when you make the Project (docs/setup/claude-project.md).\n\n- Model: not yet chosen.\n- Effort: not yet chosen.\n\n## Crowd mode\n\n- Crowd mode: off. To open the project to volunteers who each work on their own Claude account, the owner runs `python3 scripts/crowd.py setup` (docs/setup/crowdsourcing.md), whose pull request turns this line on with the decisions that come with it; from then on the required check holds the repository to them.\n\n## Owners\n\n- Repository owner: {{OWNER}}, who merges to {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}; .github/CODEOWNERS names {{CODE_OWNER}}, the owner's own login, or in an organization's copy the person who made the copy, because GitHub asks a user or a team for review and never an organization (docs/setup/github.md, section 7).\n- Workers: each person's sessions, under the person's GitHub login, and the routines and workers in GitHub Actions the registry .github/symphony/workers/ names (docs/agents/WORKERS.md); none registered yet. Each worker's label is worker:<name>, made when it first needs it.\n- The Claude token: the secret CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, from one account's `claude setup-token`, on whose plan @claude, the workers in Actions and the coordinator workflow run (docs/setup/claude-action.md); not set until the setup issue's second box is ticked.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on {{REPO}}. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by .github/workflows/board.yml, which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is; the session hook prints it at the start of every session, and docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or a worker in GitHub Actions, which .github/workflows/workers.yml runs on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike. docs/agents/WORKERS.md says how they share the repository.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session's environment sets `SYMPHONY_WORKER` to another; a person needs no file to work here. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. .github/symphony/workers/README.md lists the settings, and `bash scripts/check.sh` fails on any file that does not hold together (`python3 tools/workers.py check`). A worker is added with the \"Add a worker\" issue form, whose workflow .github/workflows/add-worker.yml opens the pull request that adds its file, or by adding the file by hand in a pull request of its own. A person may register too, with `\"runs\": \"session\"`, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take as Pick says.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done (Blockers).\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. Where the labels of several workers name one issue, it is theirs alone in the same way: given to each of them, and held by the first of them to claim it, whose Announce takes the others' labels off. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below). The board's Queue leaves an epic out, since nobody takes one whole.\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\ntemplate-init makes every label above but worker:<name>, six in all, when the repository is made, and scripts/setup-repo.sh makes them again from a clone. A worker's label is made when the worker first needs it: the claim skill makes it before putting it on an issue, and the coordinator when it gives a worker a task.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first. Else take a blocker, with or without todo, that the Blockers section lists, that nobody has claimed and that no worker's label gives to another, since a blocker comes before the Queue. Else take an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of. Of the blockers and the Queue, take only one in your area when your file under .github/symphony/workers/ gives you one; an issue that lists no files fits every area (Planning). Wherever an issue is listed, never take an epic, which the coordinator plans (Planning), nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link, on the template in docs/agents/claims/README.md. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue. The board reads two parts of the claim file: the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix (a line with nothing after it but markup or a note in parentheses names nobody: the board shows such a claim as unnamed, since the file is there to be corrected, while the clerk and a release, which must name a worker, take the one the issue's label names, as they do once the pull request has deleted the file), and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes its `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off every other worker's label, one an earlier claim left there or one that gave the issue to several workers, so that from the claim on the labels name the holder alone, as the claim file does; and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. GitHub opens one pull request at a time between the same two branches, so where one that someone who may not write opened from the branch stands in the way, which is no claim's (Rules that hold everywhere), close it, as anyone who may write can, and open your own. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, which it does in whichever run finds the branch behind or conflicting, once per commit of the default branch, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. The board reads a branch as behind where GitHub calls its pull request behind, as it does under a rule that requires an up-to-date branch, or, where GitHub's word is blocked or draft, which says only that something else stops the merge, a rule still holding the pull request or the draft itself, where the comparison of the branch with the default branch says so. A pull request GitHub calls clean, unstable or has_hooks, a draft among them, since GitHub gives a draft the word its branch has, is not held for being behind under the repository's rules, so it hears nothing however far the default branch has moved; one GitHub calls dirty, in conflict with the default branch, hears it. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch. Since a worker that has fetched holds a remote-tracking ref that matches origin, git itself would take its lease onto any claim, so before a lease onto `claude/N` the push guard reads the claim file `docs/agents/claims/N.md` on the branch the lease holds against (the commit the lease names, else origin's `claude/N` as the fetch left it) and on the branch pushed, each at its tip or, once the branch has deleted the file, where its history last added it, and refuses a lease that would take another worker's claim: one whose two files name different workers, or whose branch pushed carries none; a claim file git cannot show leaves the lease to GitHub's rules, as the guard leaves everything it cannot read (the template carries it since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy takes it once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, whose Given to workers heading 0.5.0 changes, since the copy's protocol.yml brings only tools/, and with the template's tools/guard_push.py where its own predates symphony-template#69, since an older one hands the guard no claim files).\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes `bash scripts/check.sh` and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `\"lands\": \"auto-merge\"`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. A worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, such as a cloud session whose `gh pr` calls are refused, keeps the claim file instead, pushes the finished work with `bash scripts/check.sh` passing, comments \"finished; ready to mark\" on its draft and stops, leaving the steps above to a person who can take them; until then its claim is not released (Release). Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself (docs/setup/github.md). An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: .github/workflows/tidy.yml deletes `claude/<issue>` once no open pull request uses it, and with it any `released/<issue>-<sha>` a released claim left (Release).\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. The board lists every open issue labelled blocker under Blockers, claimed or not, and a worker takes one there before the Queue, as Pick says. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it held waits for it, helps on the one pull request by review, or takes other work it does not stand in the way of (Pick).\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready says it is done (Finish). When a branch's last commit came, here and below, is when GitHub received the push that set its head, as the repository's activity records that push, so no worker's clock decides it; an entry that set the branch to another head, or deleted it, dates it too when it came after the run began, a push after the run read the branch, while an older one is stale and lists no push that set the head; the head's committer date stands in where the activity, read once more after a pause when the first read lists no push that set the head or GitHub gives it no answer that holds, still lists none, or where GitHub refuses to read it, since the push a run was started by is the one the activity is likeliest not to list yet, but never earlier than the newest entry either read listed for the branch, which the push that set the head came no sooner than. In the run that a push to the branch started, which the workflow's event names, a head no read dates is dated at the run's start instead, and so is one a read dates by an entry that set it more than a few minutes before the run began, an older push of the same head that the activity lists while it does not list that push yet, or that push itself where the run, queued behind others, began that much after it, for that run alone, since that push came just before the run, whatever the two reads list, so no claim is released in the run its own push started; the next run reads the activity again. The coordinator here is a step of the board's own run, in .github/workflows/board.yml, on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine (docs/agents/WORKERS.md). Before it releases, it frees each reopened issue whose labels still speak of a claim that ended with its close (Finish), so the in-review that claim left finishes no claim, and a claim branch still standing for the issue is released in that same run once it has gone a day without a commit. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off, and puts the label todo on it when it carries none of todo, epic and blocker, so the Queue lists it again even when a worker's label alone gave it to the worker, while a blocker is listed under Blockers without it; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails at the labels, taking one off or putting todo on, puts back the labels it took off, even one whose taking off GitHub answered with an error, unless GitHub refused it with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of taking it off, since GitHub may have taken it off and a label put back that is still on changes nothing, and takes off the todo it put on, even one whose putting on GitHub answered with an error, since GitHub may have written it, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. A run whose comment fails reads the issue's comments, and the comment there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, makes the release whole. Where they list none and GitHub refused the comment, with an answer from 400 to 499 other than a rate limit's, on a try that followed none GitHub refused for a rate limit, which GitHub gives in place of writing anything, the run puts back the labels it took off and takes off the todo it put on, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over. Where they list none after any other answer to the comment, a rate limit's refusal, a 429 or a 403 that names a rate limit or a wait in retry-after, on the last try or on one the run made the comment again after, which GitHub's documentation never says it gives in place of acting on the call, a server error, a redirect, an answer the run could not read or no answer at all, none of which says GitHub wrote nothing, and GitHub may list a comment it wrote only later, or where the run cannot read them, whatever GitHub answered the comment with, the run cannot tell whether a release comment stands, its own, an earlier run's or one someone else wrote: it leaves the labels as the release left them and counts no release of its own, so its page lists the claim as held and the branch keeps its name, since labels put back beside a comment that stands would hold the claim for good, the comment telling every later coordinator the claim is released and the label claimed telling the board it is held, and the next run, reading the comments, finds the release whole where a release comment that counts stands, or releases the claim again where none does, unless its holder pushed since, which keeps the claim, its labels as the release left them, naming no holder, until it finishes, a person puts them back or it goes a day without a commit and is released again, or a person put the label claimed back beside a comment that stands, which undoes that release (below), though a branch at a head a release already kept is released in that same run by its latest release comment, where one that counts stands, which counts for such a branch whenever it came, with no word on the issue of this run's release where GitHub wrote none. Where GitHub refuses the putting back too the labels are left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; so, running on every board run, it releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, todo put on as the coordinator puts it, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest. A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue as it lists any unclaimed one, in the Queue or, for a blocker, under Blockers, though until the same run has renamed the branch Pick reads the branch as another worker's and the clerk deals the issue to nobody (Planning), and .github/workflows/board.yml renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file (.claude/skills/claim says how). Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept, one a branch `released/<issue>-<short sha>` stands at, brings nothing new: its latest release comment counts for it whenever it came, before that push or after, so the board deletes it instead of renaming it, since its work is already kept, and the coordinator releases it only where the board counts no release of it, no release comment counting or the label claimed put back, once it has gone a day without a commit. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules, and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target (docs/setup/github.md).\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest, the work that began first, when GitHub says the push that made its branch came, or, for a pull request from a fork or from a branch it says nothing of, when the pull request was opened; work whose beginning nothing says, which can only be a claim with no pull request yet, counts as the newest, since a claim opens its draft at once (Work in the open), and the number breaks a tie. In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it; a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. The marker names each piece by its identity: a claim and its own pull requests are one piece, the issue's number, told on that issue, so opening a pull request tells nothing again, and a marker on one of the claim's own open pull requests counts as one on the issue; a pull request that is no claim's is a piece of its own, its number, told on itself. An issue's number names each claim on it in turn, so a marker tells a claim's work, or tells of it, only when it came no earlier than the push that made that claim's branch, as GitHub dates that push for the order above, and every marker counts for a claim whose push GitHub does not date. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude in .github/workflows/coordinator.yml on the repository's Claude token (docs/agents/WORKERS.md), plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it todo and `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue, labelled todo alone. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: tools/team.py. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run of .github/workflows/workers.yml do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes its own claim that needs work, one whose claim file names it, whatever the issue's labels say, or, where no claim file names a worker, one whose issue's label names it, a worker:<name> label before the older spellings and the first by name among labels of one spelling, as a release names whose the claim was, but never a claim that counts as released, which no worker carries and none takes until the board has renamed its branch (Pick, Release), nor a claim labelled blocked, whose holder waits on a peer or the coordinator (Labels) and so has nothing else to do; else the task it was given, one given to it alone first, and a task the labels of several workers give going to the first of them, in the order the registry lists the workers in Actions, with nothing else to do; else the next unclaimed blocker or queued issue, blockers first, whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area). The order is the team's: every worker the deal is for, in .github/workflows/workers.yml every registered worker in Actions, whether or not the run starts it, so a worker outside the run keeps its share, and two runs that look at one state deal no issue to two workers. The workers of one run are dealt from one look at the repository, taken once for the run and handed to each worker (`python3 tools/team.py deal`), since two workers looking for themselves at different moments, one while a peer's ready pull request is still clean and one after the default branch has moved past it, could be dealt one issue; a worker that asks alone (`python3 tools/team.py next`) is dealt by the same rule from its own look.\n\n## Locks\n\nThis section is off unless the switch says otherwise, and nothing above depends on it: the environment's `SYMPHONY_LOCKS`, else the file `.github/symphony-locks`, says off, advise or enforce, and anything else is off (sdlc_protocol.locks.mode_of).\n\nA claim locks an issue; a lock is how a claim says what it works on, so that two claims never edit one function, one section or one resource with no merge at once, the rule a coordinator otherwise applies by hand by naming one owner. A key names what is locked: `path` the whole file; `path#name` one symbol of it, a Python function or class or `Class.method`, read with the standard library's ast from its first decorator to its last line, or a Markdown section by its headings from the top, as `Title > Section`; `path#<top>` everything in the file outside its named symbols; and `scheme:id`, such as `doc:a1cb9900`, a resource outside git, always whole. Any other file is locked whole. Two keys meet when they name one resource and either is the whole of it, or they name one symbol, or one symbol holds the other. A lock is a write or a read: two entries conflict when their keys meet, at least one is a write, and they belong to different claims. A claim's write on a Python symbol comes with reads on what that symbol's code names directly, the file's other symbols, its class's methods through self and names taken from the repository's own modules by a module-level `from ... import` (sdlc_protocol.locks.plan), so a change to what another claim builds on waits; what a reference does not show, a dynamic call or a dependency through data, is the required check's to catch on the merged result.\n\nThe locks are entries in one table, LOCKS.json on the branch `locks`, which moves only by fast-forward pushes and is never forced or deleted: a worker reads the table, decides on the tip it read, and pushes one commit on that tip, which git refuses once the tip has moved, so the push is a compare-and-swap, and a refused worker reads again and decides again (sdlc_protocol.adapters.git_locks.change). The compare-and-swap is the clients' alone until a ruleset on `locks` blocks forced pushes and deletions, which the switch needs before enforce: the push guard refuses a forced push anywhere but does not yet refuse deleting `locks`, which would drop every lock. Locks taken at the claim are pushed with the claim branch in one atomic push, `git push --atomic origin locks claude/<n>`, the claim branch under a lease that expects no branch at all, so a worker gets the issue and every lock it asked for, or nothing, and never adds commits to a claim already there; a claim branch the remote already has means the issue is held, and any other refusal that the table moved. A claim takes locks later only once its branch is on origin, since until then its locks would count as ended. A worker never waits while it holds locks: refused at the claim, it picks other work; refused when it asks for more mid-work, it finishes what it holds and files the rest as an issue of its own that names the holder's. Nobody waiting while holding means no deadlock.\n\nA lock lives while its claim does: the branch `claude/<n>` is on origin and its head is not yet in the default branch, read by the branch and not by a commit, since a worker may rebase its own claim. The board's run also ends the locks of a claim whose issue is closed or whose release it counts, which git alone cannot see. A lock that has ended is dropped, by the board's run or by any worker in the commit that takes its key, and never comes back: a claim whose release is undone takes again what it held, and may be refused. A new claim of the same issue inherits what is left under its number.\n\nGit cannot keep an edit inside its grant, so a check holds each change to the locks (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks verify`): the change from the merge base, hunk by hunk on the base version, gives the keys it touches, an insertion at a symbol's edge touching the symbol and `<top>` both, since a three-way merge conflicts on edits that only abut. A touched key that another live claim holds is a problem for any pull request; for a claim's, so is a touched key it holds no write on, except in a file the change adds. At advise the check prints its problems, at enforce it fails on them. The table is trusted to everyone who may write the repository, as the claim branches and the board are.\n\nThe same reading tells a change whether it must bring the default branch in (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks overlap --base origin/main`), with no lock table and for any pull request, a claim's or not: what the default branch changed since the change's fork from it, with the reads its edited Python symbols take, is set beside what the change edits, with its own reads, and the change brings the default branch in only where the two meet with a write on either side, the default branch having edited what the change edits or builds on, or built on what the change edits. A Python file the change writes whole, or outside its symbols, reads what the whole file depends on, since such an edit builds on whatever the file does. Where they do not meet, the change lands as it is, and what a reference does not show is caught by the default branch's own check after the merge, as the required check on a pull request runs on a test merge made when it last ran and never at the merge itself; a repository that runs it leaves off GitHub's rule that a branch be up to date before it merges, which would ask every change to bring the default branch in whatever it changed. Judged at GitHub's test merge, the checkout a pull_request event gives, it reads the branch's own side, and in a shallow clone it deepens until it finds the fork. It obeys the same switch: at advise it prints the meetings, at enforce it fails on them.\n\nHere the switch is .github/symphony-locks, at advise since 2026-10-20 (the owner's word that the rule requiring an up-to-date branch be off, docs/setup/github.md), and each `python3 -m sdlc_protocol locks` above is `python3 tools/locks.py`, the shim over the library vendored under tools/: .github/workflows/ci.yml runs `python3 tools/locks.py overlap` against the branch the pull request is onto in every pull request's own run, as the job overlap, and .github/workflows/overlap.yml asks every open pull request again whenever {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} moves, writing the answer on its head as the check run overlap (tools/overlap.py), so that a ruleset requiring that check, once the switch says enforce, holds only the pull requests that must bring {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}} in. The claim skill takes no locks at the claim yet, and the ruleset on the branch locks that enforce needs is not applied yet.\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard, tools/guard_push.py, wired as a hook in .claude/settings.json, refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it (docs/setup/github.md).\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from (.claude/rules/memory.md).\n- `bash scripts/check.sh` passes before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (tools/check_protocol.py, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in this repository, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it.\n- A claim and its pull request live in this repository, so a pull request from a fork is no claim's, whatever its branch is called: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename, which closes the claim's own pull request, leaves it open.\n- A claim's pull request is opened by the repository's owner, a member of its organization, a collaborator or anyone else who may write to the repository, or by a GitHub App, which opens one only where it is installed, as the workers in Actions open theirs; a pull request anyone else opens from a claim's branch, as GitHub's documentation says anyone who may read the repository can, is no claim's either: the board lists it as a pull request of its own and tells it what it tells any other, it finishes no claim, marked ready or said finished, and a release's rename closes it with the claim's own, since GitHub closes each open pull request whose branch it renames. Where GitHub will not answer whether someone may write, a pull request they opened stays the claim's, and a release comment or an overlap marker they wrote counts for nothing, so a lost answer never frees a finished claim and never keeps work from being told.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/docs/memory/MEMORY.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Memory index\n\nOne line per fact under docs/memory/: a dash, the fact's title as a link to its file, a colon and one line saying what it is. CLAUDE.md imports this file into every session; a fact's own file is read when its line says it matters. Add or correct a fact with the remember skill (.claude/skills/remember): one file, one line here, in the same pull request as the change it came from. This index stays under 200 lines and 25 KB, a limit of this project's own: an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing here or a line names a file that is not there.\n\n- [A copy runs the template's tests](a-copy-runs-the-templates-tests.md): every copy's required check runs the template's tests over the filled files, sharing perhaps off; tests/test_filled_copy.py runs a filled copy's check, sharing on and off and in crowd mode, so a test that holds only in the template fails there first.\n- [A new repository starts with the owner's commit](a-new-repository-starts-with-the-owners-commit.md): the push guard refuses a push to main in any repository a session's git touches, so the owner creates a new repository with \"Add a README\" ticked, or adds one from its empty page, and sessions bring everything else by pull request.\n- [A Project with several repositories runs no hooks](a-project-with-several-repositories-runs-no-hooks.md): GitHub's ruleset, the claim lock, the required check, /board and the claim skill still hold; the push guard, the board at session start and the Symphony-Usage trailer do not, so keep one repository per Project.\n- [A template copies no settings](a-template-copies-no-settings.md): the SymphonyBoard App applies a copy's GitHub side on its own (.github/workflows/settings.yml runs scripts/setup-repo.sh with the App's token on template-init's push, a ruleset change and by hand), the owner's rule of 2026-10-20; Symphony Setup and setup-repo.sh by hand are only fallbacks, scripts/crowd.py setup for a crowd repository.\n- [Agents](agents.md): agents are Claude Code subagent files under .claude/agents/ with .github/symphony/agents.json, imported by pull request (tools/agents.py, agents.yml), run in sessions, Actions and as workers on the tokens accounts.json gives their names, and measured as they are by coordbench --agent-file and a flowbench stage's agent; docs/agents/AGENTS.md.\n- [Artifacts are kept as files](artifacts-are-kept-as-files.md): the artifacts in the claude.ai gallery of each of the owner's accounts live in artifacts/ of their repository, less what the export's screen holds back for the owner (Symphony and Beethoven here, SchwingNet and spine work in SchwingNet), a page as index.html with its files, a Docs document as index.md, one row each in artifacts/README.md; each new artifact and each other account's are added the same way, an account's all at once by /export-artifacts, and artifacts-grid.yml keeps both folders in ~/claude-artifacts on the grid for every account (the owner, 2026-10-22).\n- [beethoven-qa in a Symphony repository](beethoven-qa-in-a-symphony-repository.md): a repository made from the template carries beethoven-qa 6.0.0 or later, the newest 10.0.0 (the gate proves only what a change reaches), beside Symphony (the claim protocol on one side, the contracts gate and code standards on the other, a draft with a claim file satisfying both); the kit hook is beethoven-qa-session-start.sh since the old name collided with the board hook; check.sh runs the copy's vendor-check only under pixi; the steps are docs/setup/beethoven-qa.md.\n- [Beethoven's worker and Claude projects](beethoven-worker-and-claude-projects.md): a project reaches the worker only through its own Work locally, never through the worker's link, so Beethoven 21.0.0 (beethoven-extension#17) opens the project's page named in Settings; the research is docs/research/beethoven-worker-opening.md, with what is not yet measured (the successor's resume, --spawn worktree); a merge to beethoven-extension's main can be a Chrome Web Store release (CWS_ON_PUSH).\n- [CI runs Python 3.12](ci-python-is-3-12.md): the checks run on GitHub's 3.12, so tools and tests use no API newer than that; test with python3.12 where it exists.\n- [Claim branches push by name](claim-branches-push-by-name.md): a claim branch is made with --no-track, a clone made for one branch is told to fetch every branch, every push names its branch and the lease the tip pushed, since git's push.default, a single-branch clone and a lease after a fetch each sent a push somewhere the push guard cannot see.\n- [@claude runs on the main account's subscription](claude-mentions-run-on-the-main-accounts-subscription.md): mentions run .github/workflows/claude.yml on the token from `claude setup-token`, spending plan limits, never an API key; in crowd mode it never runs.\n- [Code owners are users or teams](code-owners-are-users-or-teams.md): GitHub never asks an organization for review, so template-init writes the owner, or in an organization's copy the person who made it, into .github/CODEOWNERS; check with the codeowners/errors API.\n- [Crowd mode](crowd-mode.md): scripts/crowd.py sets a repository up for volunteers on their own Claude accounts; the variable CLAUDE_CROWD, the required check's crowd guard and queue.yml then keep the Claude workflows off, Claude secrets out, checkpoints out of commits and the queue to maintainers.\n- [Field metrics](field-metrics.md): .github/workflows/metrics.yml writes aggregates only (no name, title or text) to the branch metrics with GITHUB_TOKEN; a maintainer's own claude -p adds App F counts; a breakdown by work setting (one person's several sessions apart from several people) and in pairs; what the work cost, from sessions' Symphony-Usage trailers and the Claude workflows' usage artifacts, and seven alone-versus-parallel ratios, over 1 where parallel did better; PROFILE.json beside it sums the Symphony-Profile lines and the runs' profiles the same ways, unread by the collector; the switch is .github/symphony-metrics or SDLC_METRICS=off; the release marker and the board's words it counts.\n- [Flat-rate subscriptions come first](flat-rate-subscription-first.md): every feature runs entirely on flat-rate subscription accounts, and an allowed Claude API key is used only when a setting puts it ahead of the subscription (the owner, 2026-10-25); no key is ever pasted: a credential a service makes at sign-in comes from a one-click sign-in the owner's own app completes, and only a value no sign-in can make is an encrypted GitHub secret, under secret scanning and push protection, carried to the grid by the key route.\n- [Handoff at the weekly limit](handoff-at-the-weekly-limit.md): at 90 percent of the weekly window the hooks of tools/handoff.py tell a session once to write the page the next account resumes from (the handoff skill); the status line and the grid's files are the only sensors, so a claude.ai cloud session hands off on the person's word; docs/research/automatic-handoff.md.\n- [How this repository works](how-this-repository-works.md): the repository is the shared plane for every Claude account and session; what lives where.\n- [Issue dependency graphs](issue-dependency-graphs.md): GitHub holds the epic hierarchy and the \"blocked by\" dependencies natively and draws no graph, Linear draws none at issue level and Jira only on Premium; the recommendation of 2026-10-17, awaiting the owner's choices, is native blocked_by written by the coordinator and a Mermaid section drawn by the board, with Linear as an optional one-way tier (docs/research/control-board-graph.md).\n- [Living docs](living-docs.md): the lessons and their Colab notebooks live in the public symphony-protocol (lessons/, notebooks/, scripts/lessons.py), chosen by the owner on 2026-10-20 over an export from here, and are changed there; here every module needs a docstring for the Reference page, and docs.yml, run only on the owner's call since 2026-10-25, publishes the site where Pages deploys from Actions and SYMPHONY_DOCS is pages, both set by scripts/setup-repo.sh (members only here, the owner's choice of 2026-10-20).\n- [Notebooks](notebooks.md): drive files held together as a notebook, a private repository pointing at them by id and never moving them, with research, drafts and references.json from a citation manager's BibTeX or RIS export, citations into Word as tracked Word or Zotero fields (tools/docx_cite.py); tools/notebook.py and the notebook skill; Elsevier text and token private, never public; docs/research/notebooks.md.\n- [One API key, for AI Scientist alone](one-api-key-for-ai-scientist.md): the owner allowed one Claude Console key on 2026-10-25, for AI Scientist alone, paid from the monthly Console credits with no cap, made by Claude Code's own Console sign-in and never pasted, and used only when a setting puts it ahead of the subscription; every other no-key rule stands, and SchwingNet's \"usage credits\" means a subscription's extra usage; the plan and the owner's decisions are docs/research/ai-scientist-pro-plan.md.\n- [The Outlook digest](outlook-digest.md): the digest runs on GitHub alone (digest.yml, tools/github_digest.py): a daily comment mentioning the recipient, which GitHub emails, answered by replying with a line such as \"12 Accept\"; the Outlook card with one-press buttons (tools/outlook_digest.py) waits on Wayne State's mail consent; docs/research/outlook-digest.md.\n- [Polished papers over a hidden raw layer](polished-papers-over-a-hidden-raw-layer.md): the owner's standing brief of 2026-10-25 for AI Scientist Pro and future sessions: readers get a polished, abridged, themed paper, dense with references, drawn in the field's figures and argued definitively from recorded runs, with no links out to raw artifacts; the textbook stays whole behind it, each result tied to its experiment's CLAIM-compliant ledger for one-click reproduction.\n- [Profiling sessions](profiling-sessions.md): tools/profile.py reads a session's transcript and gives what it spent as counts alone, context call by call, tool calls, reads and re-reads, time, and a session's commits carry them since its last as a Symphony-Profile line, which collect sums per pull request into PROFILE.json, MCP tools folded to one name, and tools/profile_report.py writes the programme's report.md from its committed records; the research programme under docs/research/beyond-cooperation/ measures a way of working with it before changing it, names failures as MAST does, and no run that spends tokens starts without the owner's word.\n- [Protocol changes come from symphony-protocol](protocol-changes-come-from-symphony-protocol.md): the claim protocol is changed in OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol, where the merge that raises the version is the release unless Developer mode is on; its sync pull request `symphony-protocol/sync` brings each published release to OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template, while a repository made from the template takes each release tag by its own workflow, protocol.yml, whose pull request waits for the owner where more checks are required; never edit tools/sdlc_protocol, which scripts/check.sh holds to the hashes in tools/VENDORED and CI to the library's own files; a sync below VENDORED's version is a downgrade to close.\n- [Stale-branch statistics](stale-branch-statistics.md): tools/staleness.py counts, from GitHub's history, each pull request's catch-ups of the default branch, its check runs redone on them and how long it sat green but stale; staleness.yml records every merge on the branch staleness with a snapshot of open pull requests and the up-to-date rule's state; docs/research/stale-branch-failures.md.\n- [Subagent token figures are context, not spend](subagent-token-figures-are-context-not-spend.md): the tokens the session's tools report for a subagent or a workflow are each agent's context at its last turn; what a step spent is summed per message id from its agents' transcripts, tokens written and read with the cached share, as tools/profile.py counts a session; docs/research/skeptic-reads-and-lens-reviews.md.\n- [SWE-bench runs from GitHub and PyPI](swe-bench-runs-from-github-and-pypi.md): the task repository SWE-bench/swe-bench-tasks, compiled by `swebench dataset build`, scores an instance where huggingface.co is unreachable; never --task-repo, which rebuilds the image; the images pull through mirror.gcr.io; OpenSpineConsortium/flowbench builds on it.\n- [Symphony versus competitors](symphony-versus-competitors.md): the comparison of 2026-10-16 is docs/research/symphony-versus-competitors.md (and a Claude Doc); its three claims are better by design and graded Inferred, no outcome is measured yet, and coordbench numbers stay out until the owner lifts the hold; the site's /symphony/compare.html sets the general powers beside familiar tools in seven kinds of work, and /symphony/use-cases.html gives the use cases (docs/research/symphony-compared-by-domain.md).\n- [The check runs in parts](the-check-runs-in-parts.md): ci.yml's job parts runs check.sh without its tests and the tests in four parts by tests/durations.json (tools/test_shard.py), side by side, and the required job check waits for them; re-measure with `python3 tools/test_shard.py measure` when a slow test lands.\n- [The control panel](the-control-panel.md): the site's /symphony/control/ drives a Symphony repository through GitHub's API as the signed-in person (the board, tasks, @claude, workers, pull requests, workflow runs) and /symphony/cost.html prices the work against the alternatives, unsorry first; Sign in with GitHub first (the App's user token through a stateless Worker), a pasted fine-grained token the fallback; it took no Claude token until the owner dropped that rule on 2026-10-18 for the Tokens page's Add a slot box, which saves a pasted token as an organization secret through the App; its assistant is an issue labelled assistant that claude.yml answers read-only in ten turns with .github/symphony/assistant.md appended, and the floating Claude button at the top right asks it (or opens Add Token where no Claude token is set) and offers the panel actions its answer names as buttons the person presses (site #28, 2026-10-18; docs/research/ask-claude-bar.md; the button since 2026-10-20, docs/research/claude-drives-the-panel.md, with the plan for Claude driving the panel by named actions and a command-line tool, parked as a future feature); the research note is docs/research/control-panel.md.\n- [The drive organizer](drive-organizer.md): a whole drive read into a catalog on the branch drive-catalog, Claude proposing projects as epics whose tasks carry proposed and no todo until a person runs accept, which adds the folders to the sync, and the sync's run starts the tasks once the files are in; private repositories only, nothing scheduled; tools/file_digest.py reads any file as text; a session does it through the Microsoft 365 connector too (tools/drive_connector.py, the drive-organize skill), moving files only on the owner's go and undoably; Wayne State approved the Folders app for native Graph access on 2026-10-19, confirmed live 2026-10-20, so the drive is read only through Graph under the owner's sign-in and the connector walk is retired (docs/research/onedrive-direct-graph.md); docs/research/drive-organizer.md.\n- [The front door is one press](the-front-door-is-one-press.md): install the App, press Create on the form its Setup URL opens, merge; the run picks the repository by id, reads the kind from the tree and shares aggregates unless a box says otherwise; the sweep is the fallback.\n- [The grid runs a job through Beethoven](the-grid-runs-a-job-through-beethoven.md): .github/workflows/grid.yml runs one command on a WSU compute node through the runner chain that hpcworker's tools/grid_runner.sh serves as Slurm jobs (a private repository, labels self-hosted and grid, the output back as the job's log and an artifact); grid-gate.yml starts, reads or stops the chain over the ssh gate, a key whose forced command takes only those three words, which hpcworker's `grid_runner.sh --gate-setup` sets up in one command in the grid's shell; fixed job words through grid-accounts.yml's action job (update, the spine-datasets promoter, SchwingNet's own grid/gate.sh words) so a session runs grid commands in the owner's place; no Claude token goes there; docs/setup/grid.md has the owner's steps, docs/research/wsu-grid-and-github-actions.md the research.\n- [The overlap check](the-overlap-check.md): the rule requiring an up-to-date branch is off everywhere (the owner, 2026-10-20); `python3 tools/locks.py overlap` asks a pull request to bring the default branch in only where what the default branch changed meets what it edits or builds on, run by ci.yml on each pull request and by overlap.yml on every open one when the default branch moves, as the check run overlap; the switch is .github/symphony-locks, at advise.\n- [The plan ladder](the-plan-ladder.md): which GitHub and Claude plans unlock what, verified 2026-10-13 against docs/setup/free-or-paid.md; public repo means free Actions, the enforced gate and free scanning, a private one needs Pro or Team for the gate and an add-on for scanning; no payment method means a hard cap.\n- [The review record](the-review-record.md): every pull request marked ready, a person's or a session's, carries a section \"## Adversarial review\" in its body, held by tools/review_record.py in the required job check (drafts, bots and pull requests opened before 2026-10-22 05:00 UTC excepted); CONTRIBUTING.md; docs/research/same-ci-for-every-contributor.md.\n- [The sync offers adopted repositories their files](the-sync-offers-adopted-repositories-their-files.md): sync.yml and tools/sync_adopted.py offer every adopted repository, on every installation, the library, the board's, tidy, metrics, Token and drive sync workflows with their modules as one pull request from symphony/sync, replacing only versions the template once held and adding only the Token and drive sync units.\n- [The token pool](the-token-pool.md): the branch pool holds each Claude slot's latest five-hour, seven-day and per-model (Fable) use from runs' rate_limit_event, pool.yml's probes and cswap via tokens.py usage, since a setup token cannot read the usage page; tools/pool.py pick is the auto mode over 32 slots, steered per slot (model, effort, fallback, resume, paused, id, owner) by SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_SETTINGS; issues a token worked carry token:<id> for the leaderboard; docs/research/claude-token-pool.md.\n- [The token wizard](the-token-wizard.md): python3 scripts/tokens.py stores each Claude account's subscription token as an Actions secret (CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN, the pool SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_1 to 8 with SYMPHONY_CLAUDE_ACCOUNTS) by running claude setup-token behind a pseudo-terminal that masks the token and piping it to gh, never shown or written, pasted only at a hidden prompt where no terminal can run claude; each slot's sign-in opens in a browser profile of its own through BROWSER, and cswap's account list names the slot, never its credentials; on Windows a copy in Credential Manager that show reads back, and share for a benchmark's repository; it also runs in a Codespace from the browser alone (scripts/codespace.sh); docs/setup/claude-tokens.md; the Tokens page's Add a Claude account mints on an Actions runner, the token leaving it only sealed (token.yml, tools/token_run.py).\n- [Word revisions](word-revisions.md): a folder of Word documents is revised as tasks; tools/docx_review.py writes a review sheet and one task per document, queues new comments (papers.yml) and applies a worker's revisions as tracked changes; a connected Google Drive or OneDrive syncs both ways through drive-sync.yml (docs/research/drive-sync.md); several reviewers revise one document as authors of their own (docx_review.py panel, the lens-review skill, docs/research/lens-review.md).\n- [Workers and the coordinator](workers.md): one way of working from one person alone to several people, routines and workers in GitHub Actions, each a worker under its label worker:<name>; routines and workers in Actions are files under .github/symphony/workers/, added by the \"Add a worker\" form, and workers.yml runs those in Actions, dealt the open work once a run from one look; the coordinator releases idle claims and tells overlaps in the board's run, and plans epics in coordinator.yml.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/memory.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Shared memory\n\nThe shared memory is docs/memory/, imported into every session through the line `@docs/memory/MEMORY.md` in CLAUDE.md. A claude.ai Project's own memory is private to the account that made it, so anything every account should know goes here instead.\n\n- One fact per file under docs/memory/, named in kebab-case (`how-this-repository-works.md`), with a one-line description as the file's first line.\n- One index line per fact in docs/memory/MEMORY.md, in the form `- [title](file.md): one line`. MEMORY.md stays under 200 lines and 25 KB; the limit is this project's own, because an imported file loads whole into every session's context, and Claude Code's guidance for an instruction file is under 200 lines. `scripts/check.sh` fails when a fact is missing from the index or the index names a file that is not there.\n- A thread that learns a fact, or finds one wrong, adds or corrects it in the same pull request as the change it learned from. Memory never travels alone and never waits for a later pull request.\n- Never a secret, never a token, never a cookie or a key. The checks look for the shapes of common secrets and refuse them.\n- What holds only for one piece of work (what was done today, what is left) is a checkpoint (.claude/skills/checkpoint), not a memory.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/writing.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Writing\n\n- Plain prose that says why, in comments, commit messages, pull requests and documents alike. What the code does is read from the code.\n- Short functions; names that read as English.\n- No emoji in code, output or documents. ASCII and LF in everything under .claude/, docs/, tools/ and .github/; prefer ASCII elsewhere.\n- No new dependencies: Python 3.12 or later with its standard library, plain bash, and GitHub's own actions and anthropics/claude-code-action, pinned to a major tag. A proposal to add one is an issue first.\n- Nothing refers to a file that does not exist.\n- In conversation, put a fact worth keeping on its own line tagged `Decision:` or `Constraint:`, so the checkpoint skill and `cswap checkpoint write` keep it word for word; the rest of a session is inferred and may be lost.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/research.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Research accumulates in docs/research/\n\nWhat a session finds out is kept in the repository, under docs/research/, where every account and every later session reads it: a reply, a status line, a Claude Doc or an artifact is read once, and a claude.ai Project's files belong to one account. A handoff under docs/checkpoints/ says where the research is and never carries it, so research never waits for a checkpoint and is never lost with one.\n\n- One file or one folder per topic, named in kebab-case. A programme, a question measured over time, is a folder with a README that says where it stands; a finished study, comparison, deep-research run or fact-check is one file. docs/research/README.md lists both, one line each, and the line comes in the same pull request as the file.\n- Every file opens by naming its source: the thread's title, the date, and the link of the Claude Doc, artifact or upload it came from where there is one. A doc or artifact a session cannot read from its container is listed in the README by link and never written down from memory.\n- Research lands in the pull request of the work it came from or, when the research is the work, in a pull request of its own, before the thread's checkpoint. A note stays here once its question is settled; a pull request that settles a finding for good may move it to docs/history/, the longer record, and a fact every session should know becomes a memory (.claude/rules/memory.md) that points here.\n- ASCII and LF, as the checks hold everything under docs/ to, and plain prose that says why (.claude/rules/writing.md); no secret, no token, no transcript and no tool output, as docs/history/ holds too, and the checks refuse the shapes of common secrets and any .events.json.\n\nContents of /home/user/symphony-template/.claude/rules/board.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# The board comes first\n\nBefore any change in this repository:\n\n1. Fetch: `git fetch origin`. The session-start hook does this once; do it again before you push.\n2. Read the board and docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (how work is claimed). The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rewritten by the board workflow; read it with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`, the form /board uses, since `git fetch origin board` updates no remote-tracking branch in a clone made for one branch, as a cloud session's is. The session-start hook prints it at the start of every session; docs/agents/STATE.md on the default branch only describes its layout.\n3. Check the claim on the issue you mean to take: a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, means another worker has it, and a `worker:<name>` label that is not yours, with none that is, means the issue is that worker's: pick another. An issue the labels give to several workers, you among them, is each of theirs, and the first to claim it holds it. An issue the board's Given to workers section gives you comes first, then an unclaimed blocker, then the Queue; never take an epic, which the coordinator plans, nor an issue labelled blocked, which waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nYou work here as a worker under a name of your own (docs/agents/WORKERS.md): in a person's session, the person's GitHub login unless the environment sets SYMPHONY_WORKER; in a routine or a worker in Actions, the name its prompt gives. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Claim with the claim skill (.claude/skills/claim) before the first change to any other file, and open the draft pull request at once.\n\nNever push to a branch another worker claimed. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim; after a rebase, your own claim branch takes `git push --force-with-lease=claude/<issue>:<the tip you last pushed> origin claude/<issue>` (the claim skill's step 7), the branch named so that the push forces nothing else and the tip so that it never forces over a push made since, and nothing else ever does. When the board comments \"main moved\" on your pull request, bring the default branch in: rebase on it and push again, or merge it in. An issue labelled blocker is claimed like any other, and nobody fixes it in parallel.\n\nA hook enforces the push rules before they can be broken: tools/guard_push.py, wired in .claude/settings.json, refuses a push to the default or board branch, a plain --force anywhere, a + before a refspec, which forces that ref as --force does, the refspec : and patterns with *, which move branches wholesale as --all, --branches and --mirror do, a push to a name that reads as a claim and claims nothing, such as claude/03 (a claim branch is claude/ and the issue's number in one to nine ASCII digits with no leading zero, docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md, Claim), --force-with-lease outside your own claude/ branch, and, where the copy carries the fence (the template since symphony-template#78 on 2026-10-14, ahead of symphony-protocol 0.5.0, the release that carries it; an older copy once that release is published, by hand as docs/start/protocol.md's step 7 says, with the template's tests/fixtures/board-STATE.md, and with its tools/guard_push.py where the copy's own predates symphony-template#69), --force-with-lease onto a claude/ branch whose claim file on origin names another worker, or with a branch that carries no claim file for the issue, since a lease after a fetch is the one push git itself would take onto another worker's claim. A refusal is the rule working, not an obstacle; the answer is a pull request, a rebase or another branch, never a way around the hook. Branch protection on GitHub (docs/setup/github.md) stands behind it.\n\nContents of /home/user/spine-program/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# spine-program: the root working context\n\nThis is one private repository that seeds a set of Claude Code projects, one per numbered folder; each folder is\nself-contained (its own `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `STATUS.md`, `TODO.md`, `MEMORY.md` + `memory/`, three manifests and the\nsalvaged material). Open the folder's `CLAUDE.md` first: the rules below bind everywhere, and the folder adds its own\ntraps. Nothing here is imaging data or a weight; the grid and the named repositories hold those. Since 2026-10-13 the salvaged record, each folder's `results/`, `figures/`, `manuscripts/` and `regulatory/`, lives on the grid too, under `/rs/rs_grp_oschome/shared/spine-program/archive/`, and the folders in the tree are symlinks to it: `ARCHIVE.md` says how a session reads a file from either place and how a task brings one back into its branch (issue #87).\n\n## Read first\n\n- `_plan/PLAN.md` sections 1-2 (the division and the builders' rules) and section 9 (the corrections of the final pass).\n- The folder's `CLAUDE.md`, `README.md`, `STATUS.md`, `TODO.md`.\n- `<folder>/docs/handoff/README.md` section 5 (the ten rules as the handoff wrote them) and\n  `<folder>/docs/handoff/RESULTS_CATALOG.md` \"How to read a row\" (every folder carries the student-readable handoff files).\n- `<folder>/DATA.md` (every grid path the folder uses, who may read it, a read-only check line); its access facts of\n  2026-10-11 supersede older statements in the `docs/handoff` copies.\n\n## The map\n\n```\n01_carm_level_detection      Greg-only   the C-arm path to a hospital test (SchwingNet as it stands -> service -> PREREG -> real fluoroscopy)\n02_transitional_naming_truth Greg-only   the transitional truth file, reader protocol, power table (paper 2); feeds 01's PREREG\n03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset   student     release v11, the dataset article, the NeurIPS camera-ready, Zenodo and HF\n04_openspinetoolkit          student     ostk's v11 defects and their one-hour tests; blocks 05\n05_ct_morphometry_parity     student     the spinopelvic estimator, the level atlas, 3-D to 2-D parity\n06_transitional_ct_census    student     the harvest over 21,622 CTs, the registry, ruleclass arms, the review tree\n07_plain_film_level_id       Greg-only   the radiograph corner detector, XRSpinoPelvic1K, the film-naming line\n08_render_devices            Greg-only   DRR banks, device cards, PolyPose, synthetic-to-real; serves 01 and 05\n09_wrong_level_clinical_qi   Greg-only   the iliac-crest paper, the scoping review, the QI protocol (01's per-case form)\n10_data_platform_grid        student     211 sources, the Ceph migration, identity and leakage, the registry, grid rules; the dependency of all\n11_students_grid_access      mixed       Beethoven, onboarding, the lab recipe, the med-ed record; waits on 10\n12_website_pacs_demo         student     the public site, the PACS demo, the level-service client; waits on 01 S2, 04, 05\n13_aidas_program             mixed       the AIDaS program map: workflow, IRB, data and literature mining, C-arm training, recruitment, the LLM; REFORMS\n99_archive                   Greg-only   superseded, refuted, dormant; never run, never quoted\n_plan/                                   the division, the disposition of every row, the exclusions, the inventories, the build record\n```\nEvery Write holder may read all 13 folders (Greg's answer of 2026-10-12 to `OPEN_QUESTIONS.md` A29, issue #68); the\nlabel governs claiming and hand-outs, not reading. `Greg-only` means Greg works the folder first, and a student takes an\nissue there only after Greg hands it out (his comment on the issue naming the student's login, or the label\n`greg-first` removed); `student` means a hand-out can be cut from the folder after the redactions it records. The\nrepository itself is private whatever the label.\n\n**How the folders relate.** 10 is the dependency of all (data, identity tables, the registry, the access gate). 04 blocks\n05. 02 feeds 01's PREREG and consumes 06. 08 serves 01 and 05. 09's per-case form is 01's S5 / S8 scaffold. 12 waits on 01\nS2, 04 and 05. 11 waits on 10. 99 is never run. The one circularity is stated on both sides: 01 depends on 12 for the\nservice client while 12 waits on 01 S2.\n\n## The standing rules (every folder's CLAUDE.md restates these in its own words)\n\n1. **Words and style.** Never the banned word, the French-derived word for where data came from: write origin, lineage,\n   source, where it came from. No fractional version numbers; fold a change into the next integer version. Figure captions\n   at most 60 words, the argument in the text. Plain sentences. Matrix tables in solid black with at most one outlined mark;\n   slides never use sideways bar charts. The affiliation is General Surgery (Wayne State University School of Medicine /\n   Detroit Medical Center), never Neurosurgery. Ashley Schehr is a person (senior scientist, co-PI, a published co-author),\n   never a product name; the product is Beethoven. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`, `memory/writing-preferences.md`,\n   `memory/user-affiliation-general-surgery.md`.)\n2. **Every number carries its file.** A count, date, path or claim names the file it comes from; what no file records is\n   written \"not recorded\"; say plainly what is not known rather than guess. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md` \"How to be useful here\".)\n3. **Check outside the computation.** The recurring failure in this programme is a number that is wrong but plausible:\n   three estimators produced finite, reasonable values from the wrong bone, the wrong surface or the wrong label map, and\n   internal consistency (PI = SS + PT) held throughout because both sides shared one bad landmark. Compare with published\n   values, an independent implementation, the spread of the distribution (not its median), a published prevalence; render\n   the mask; when a tidy explanation presents itself, test it before believing it. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`,\n   `ctsp:docs/SPINOPELVIC_ESTIMATOR.md`, `ctsp:docs/LEVEL_MORPHOMETRY.md`.)\n4. **Labels and orientation.** Two label schemes exist and mixing them does not raise: release v11 is VerSe-native\n   (L1 = 20 ... L5 = 24, sacrum 26, hips 30 / 31, femurs 32 / 33); the legacy map puts L1 at 1 and the femurs at 11 / 12,\n   and \"femur_left\" resolved against the wrong one comes back T4. Call `ostk.labels.labels_for(volume)`; never import a\n   fixed map. Identifier 29 (the carved S1) is retired in v11 and dissolved into 26; code that gates on 29 is retired with\n   it. Case ids are zero-padded (`zfill(4)`) in the release and were not in the old CSVs. Canonicalise to RAS to analyse;\n   never reorient when writing a label (`as_closest_canonical` + `nib.save` transposes a label away from its CT); CT volumes\n   are stored P, I, R. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`, `memory/label-scheme-v4-vs-ostk.md`, `memory/never-reorient-when-writing-labels.md`.)\n5. **Held out.** Held out means the person was never seen by the checkpoint or any ancestor; a render inherits its CT's\n   person; unknown is not clean. Every quoted score goes through `ssn:tools/eval_gate.py`. Never quote the leaked banks\n   (allspine v2, v6, v7, v8 for VerSe, `anchor_drr`, `vindrps`) or any number marked LEAKED, SUPERSEDED or RECORD ONLY in\n   `docs/handoff/RESULTS_CATALOG.md`. (`docs/handoff/LEAKAGE.md`.)\n6. **Pre-register, then score.** Write the rule and its success criterion before the run, hash it, stamp every output row\n   with the hash; a number produced before the freeze is calibration; comparators are pre-specified from the literature and\n   never tuned; manuscript numbers enter through a manifest key, never typed. (`docs/handoff/RECIPES.md`.)\n7. **The login node runs nothing.** Every computation, render, plot and download is an `sbatch` job; warrior kills python,\n   rsync, node, pip and conda by name; no container command on warrior and nothing of ours under its `/tmp`. `sbatch -D <dir>`\n   (a relative `--output` resolves against the submit directory); `afterany`, not `afterok`; IPC under `/dev/shm` (compute-node\n   `/tmp` is swept); large outputs on Ceph `/rs/rs_grp_oschome/go2432/`, not HOME; record a job before cancelling it; write grid\n   bash for bash 4.2; partition `reqp --qos=requeue` for CPU, `gmsap` / `gvohp` for GPU; Greg's python with nibabel and\n   scipy is `~/mambaforge/envs/spineps/bin/python`, in his HOME, which students cannot read; a student's environment is\n   `11_students_grid_access/STUDENT_ENV.md` (written by issue #66). Submit, estimate the wall time, write the job ids and\n   the ETA to the resume file, stop. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md` \"Grid\", `memory/no-jobs-on-login-node.md`,\n   `memory/submit-and-sleep-orchestration.md`.)\n8. **What stays private.** SchwingNet and all level-identification code, text, figures and weights; FluoroCap; the\n   `spinesurg-ct-nnunet` and `XRSpinoPelvic1K` repositories; this repository. Nothing about them goes public until Greg says\n   so; run `ssn:tools/disclosure_audit.py` before any talk, upload or submission; for the CNS talk use only\n   `CNS2026_Schwing_NotAllSpines_public_v12.pptx`. (`memory/fluorocap-repo-private.md`, `memory/schwingnet-disclosure-scrub.md`.)\n9. **Data directives.** Use all of a public dataset; a download budget is not a criterion; every truncation is fixed or\n   justified in writing before a rerun; a leaked initialisation is fixed by retraining, never disclosed as a caveat;\n   FluoroCap is out of training (a test set at most); identity comes from metadata first and the file name last; the IRB\n   umbrella (HPR 2026-269) covers only public, de-identified data downloadable without an agreement.\n   (`memory/no-unexplained-subsets-fix-not-disclose.md`, `memory/data-directives-2026-09-23.md`.)\n10. **Git, network, credentials.** In spine-program the worker doing the work, a person's sessions under the person's\n    GitHub login, commits and pushes only its own branch `claude/<issue>`, as `docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md` says; it never\n    commits to or pushes `main`; Greg merges. The claim push\n    is a plain `git push -u origin claude/<N>` in the Bash tool, never wrapped in `wsl`. In the code repositories\n    (`CTSpinoPelvic1K`, `OpenSpineToolkit`, `spinesurg-ct-nnunet`, `XRSpinoPelvic1K`, `openspineconsortium.github.io`,\n    `beethoven`) and on Greg's laptop: commit only when Greg asks; `CTSpinoPelvic1K` commits directly to master, no\n    feature branches, and pushes to `origin` and `osc`; `OpenSpineToolkit`: branch `main`, both remotes. The WSL network rule is Greg's Windows laptop's: there, network (git push, ssh, rsync, Hugging Face)\n    goes through `wsl -e bash -lc '...'`, and `ctsp:paper/mpda/make_submission.py` is the one exception and runs from\n    Windows. The git clauses of the folder `CLAUDE.md` files mean the repositories those folders name, never this one.\n    Tokens are read from files, never echoed, never on a command line or in\n    `sbatch --export`; nothing under the laptop vendor folder is read. Write scripts as files and run them by path (inline\n    heredocs collapse backslashes); never name a scratch script `numbers.py`, `io.py` or `pdb.py`.\n    (`memory/wsl-for-network.md`, `memory/bash-heredoc-backslashes.md`, `memory/scratchpad-shadows-stdlib.md`.)\n11. **The page limit** of the dataset article is 10 published pages and only `build.sh --reprint` measures it; read the\n    whole build output, because LaTeX emits a PDF and a page count on top of a broken table. (`ctsp:CLAUDE.md`.)\n12. **Personal data.** No student name or username, no researcher or personal e-mail address enters any file of this\n    repository; where a source carries one, the redacted copy is used and `MANIFEST_LOCAL.md` records the redaction by kind.\n    Three kinds of keep are recorded, not hidden: the author lists inside submitted or public manuscript sources (the record\n    of authorship; Greg's to confirm, `_plan/PLAN.md` section 5), Greg's own institutional corresponding-author address in\n    his manuscript sources and package metadata, and the maintainer addresses printed in third-party template files\n    (`neurips_2026.sty`, `medphy.bst`). Role mailboxes (`hpc@`, `IRBQuestions@`) and the ssh login `go2432@grid.wayne.edu` are\n    not personal data and stay. The worker name the claim protocol needs (the `worker:<login>` label, the claim file's\n    `- Worker:` line, the board) is working metadata like commit authorship, not the personal data this rule is about.\n\n## Prefixes and markers in the copied documents\n\n`ssn:` = spinesurg-ct-nnunet at 097933a; `ctsp:` = CTSpinoPelvic1K at 7ddfe38; `ostk:` = OpenSpineToolkit at cb43197;\n`xr:` = XRSpinoPelvic1K at 4f22935; `bth:` = OpenSpineConsortium/beethoven at 2f90319. Copied files spell where they came\nfrom with markers, never with a laptop path: `<laptop:Desktop>` (the Desktop root that held every checkout, so\n`<laptop:Desktop>/spinesurg-ct-nnunet` is `ssn:`), `<laptop:Temp scratchpad>` (a swept session folder; nothing there is a\ndependency), `<laptop memory folder>` (the live memory notes, copied redacted into each `memory/`), `<laptop vendor folder,\nnever read>`, `<laptop home>`. Only `MANIFEST_LOCAL.md` files and `_plan/` keep the laptop spellings, as the record of\nwhere each copy came from. A marker is history; the repository and commit are the dependency.\n\n## How to be useful here\n\nCheck against something outside the computation. When a tidy explanation presents itself, test it before believing it.\nSay plainly what is not known; `ctsp:docs/SPINOPELVIC_ESTIMATOR.md` records an unexplained failure in 128 records rather\nthan a guess at its cause, and that is the preferred form. Render the mask: twice in this programme one render settled\nwhat two or three rounds of reasoning from numbers got wrong.\n\n## What a cloud session reaches\n\nA claude.ai Project thread or a cloud session has this clone and GitHub, through its proxy, and nothing else: no grid,\nno ssh, no `sbatch`, no `wsl`, no access to Greg's HOME and no clone of the other repositories. The record folders the symlinks\npoint at are on the grid, so such a session reads one of their files from git history (`ARCHIVE.md`). Grid steps run from the\nBeethoven worker or by Greg, and their job ids and outputs come back to the claim branch. A change to another repository\nlands as `<folder>/patches/<N>-<slug>.patch` (`git format-patch` against the commit `MANIFEST_CODE.md` names) in the\nclaim's pull request, and Greg applies it.\n\n## How work is claimed\n\n@docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md\n\n@docs/memory/MEMORY.md\n\nBefore any work: fetch, read the board with `git fetch origin +refs/heads/board:refs/remotes/origin/board && git show origin/board:STATE.md` (or `/board`), and check the claim on the issue you mean to take. One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request. Never push to the default branch or to another worker's claim branch. The required check \"check\" (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`, `bash scripts/check.sh`) holds every pull request to the claim protocol and the review record (`CONTRIBUTING.md`); run it before a pull request. The push guard is the symphony plugin's and runs in local sessions only.\n\nThe protocol is the release of OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-protocol that `tools/VENDORED` records, vendored under `tools/sdlc_protocol`; `.github/workflows/protocol.yml` brings each later release in, weekly and by hand, as a pull request from the branch `symphony-protocol/sync` (or an issue linking one where GitHub Actions may not open a pull request), and nobody vendors by hand. Every worker here is a person's sessions, named by the GitHub login: the registry under `.github/symphony/workers/`, the workers' and the coordinator's workflows that PROTOCOL.md names are the template's and are not here; the board's own run releases idle claims and tells overlapping work.\n\nContents of /home/user/spine-program/docs/agents/PROTOCOL.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# How work is claimed\n\nThe queue is GitHub Issues on the repository. The board is the file STATE.md on the branch `board`, rendered from the issues, the branches, the claim files and the pull requests by the board workflow (`python3 -m sdlc_protocol board`, whose rules are sdlc_protocol.board), which rewrites that branch's one commit on every issue and pull request event, every branch made or deleted, every push to the default branch, and every six hours as a fallback; nobody edits it by hand. Read it with `git fetch origin board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`; a repository that follows this protocol also prints it from its session-start hook, so every session begins with it. A claim is the first push of a branch: GitHub refuses the second push of the same name, so two workers can never hold the same issue. Everything below is done by the worker doing the work, in the open, and the claim skill that ships with the template and with the symphony plugin is the same steps as commands.\n\n## Workers\n\nA worker is anything that claims issues: a person's sessions, local or in the cloud, with whatever helpers they start; a routine on someone's Claude account, which runs on its own; or runs in GitHub Actions on the repository's own Claude token. There is one way of working for all of them, whether one person works alone, one person runs several sessions and routines at once, or many people and their routines share the repository: each worker claims, works and finishes by the steps below, and the board and the coordinator treat them alike.\n\nEach worker has a name, unique in the repository: its label is `worker:<name>` and its claim files say `- Worker: <name>`, so two workers on one Claude account still tell their claims apart. A person's sessions take the person's GitHub login as their name, unless the session is given another. A routine or a worker in Actions is registered by a small file, `.github/symphony/workers/<name>.json`, which says where it runs (`actions`, `routine` or `session`), what it is for, the area of the repository it prefers (paths or globs), how its pull requests land (`owner`, the repository's owner merges, or `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green), and, for a worker in Actions, the model, the turns one run may take and how many runs it may take in a row; it never holds a credential. sdlc_protocol.workers reads and checks those files, and `python3 -m sdlc_protocol workers check` fails on any file that does not hold together. A person may register too, to give their sessions an area. Name a routine or a worker in Actions after its job, not after anyone's login, so it never shares a name with a person's sessions.\n\n## Labels\n\n- todo: the queue. An issue labelled todo and shown unclaimed on the board is free to take.\n- claimed: a worker has pushed its branch. The worker's own label goes on with it.\n- in-review: the pull request is ready; the owner merges it and the merge closes the issue.\n- blocker: the issue stops everyone until it is done.\n- worker:<name>: the worker that holds the issue, or that the coordinator gave it before any claim. An issue a worker's label names is that worker's alone: it is left out of the board's Queue and shown under Given to workers until the worker claims it. The older spellings account:<name> and agent:<name> are read as the same label.\n- epic: a request the coordinator plans into tasks (Planning, below).\n- blocked: a worker waits on a peer or the coordinator.\n\nsdlc_protocol.labels spells these names and the prefixes once, with the colour and description of every label a repository makes up front; a worker's label is made when the worker first needs it.\n\n## Pick\n\nFetch, then read the board (`git fetch origin board && git show origin/board:STATE.md`). Take an issue the Given to workers section gives you first; else an open issue labelled todo that the Queue section shows as unclaimed and that no open blocker stands in the way of, one in your area first when you have one. An issue with a branch `claude/<issue>` on origin, or a file `docs/agents/claims/<issue>.md` on the default branch, is another worker's, whatever its labels say. Before you start, read the board's Overlaps section: open work that changes the same files as the issue you take lands in the order it gives.\n\n## Claim\n\nPush a new branch `claude/<issue number>` from the default branch whose first commit adds `docs/agents/claims/<issue number>.md`: the worker, the plan, the files to touch, the issue's link. Nothing else is in that first commit. The claim branch of issue N is exactly `claude/N`, with N in one to nine ASCII digits and no leading zero, since GitHub refuses only a second push of the same name: `claude/03` is no claim on #3. The board shows such a branch as not a claim, and the push guard refuses a push to one and the protocol check fails a pull request from one, both naming the issue's own claim branch. If the push is refused because the branch exists, another worker has it: delete the local branch and pick another issue.\n\nThe claim file takes this shape, with N the issue's number and NAME the worker's name. The board reads two parts of it (sdlc_protocol.claims): the `- Worker:` line (or the older `- Account:`), whose name is the worker's label without the `worker:` prefix, and the list under `## Files to touch`, which fills the board's Files column and its Overlaps until the pull request has files of its own; a release quotes the `## Plan`. A claim file over 1 MB, more than GitHub's contents API gives the text of, is read for none of these: the board names the worker by the issue's worker label, or says the file was not read, and takes no files from it, and a release says where the file is in place of quoting its plan.\n\n```\n# Claim: #N\n\n- Issue: https://github.com/OWNER/NAME/issues/N\n- Worker: NAME\n- Branch: claude/N\n\n## Plan\n\nTwo to five sentences: what will change and why, in the order it will land.\n\n## Files to touch\n\n- the paths this work expects to change, one per line\n```\n\n## Announce\n\nAdd the label claimed and the worker's label to the issue, taking off any other worker's label an earlier claim left there, and post the plan as a comment, so the board and the people reading the issue see the same thing the branch says.\n\n## Work in the open\n\nOpen a draft pull request at once, from the branch to the default branch, with \"Closes #N\" in its body. Push often: a branch with no commit for 24 hours is released (below). When the board comments \"main moved\" on the pull request, the default branch has moved past the branch: bring it in, by rebasing on it and pushing again with `--force-with-lease`, or by merging it in, which needs no forced push and is what a worker in Actions does. That `--force-with-lease` is the one forced push the protocol allows, and only on your own claim branch.\n\n## Finish\n\nThe pull request deletes its claim file, says \"Closes #N\", passes the repository's checks and is marked ready; the issue gets the label in-review. The owner merges, or, for a worker whose file says `auto-merge`, GitHub merges once the required check is green; the merge closes the issue, and the next board no longer lists the claim. Delete the branch after the merge if the repository does not do it by itself. An issue closed without a merge loses its branch too: once no open pull request uses `claude/<issue>`, the repository's tidy workflow deletes it.\n\n## Blockers\n\nAn issue labelled blocker stops everyone: work it stands in the way of waits until it is done. It is claimed like any other issue, by one worker, and nobody else fixes it in parallel; a second worker that sees it waits, or helps on the one pull request by review.\n\n## Release\n\nA claim whose branch has had no commit for more than 24 hours, by the committer date of its head, is released by the coordinator, unless it is finished: an issue labelled in-review, a pull request marked ready, or a draft whose author's latest comment on it starts with \"finished; ready to mark\" waits for review however long it takes. That comment is how a worker that may not mark its own pull request ready, a cloud session whose gh pr calls are refused, says it is done, for a maintainer to mark. The coordinator is a step of the board's own run (sdlc_protocol.coordinator, through sdlc_protocol.adapters.github.coordinate), on the repository's own token, so releasing needs no Claude account and no routine. The coordinator first takes the label claimed and every worker's label off the issue, a label already gone counting as taken off; then it posts a comment on the issue whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: claim-released -->` and which says why and whose the claim was, what the branch holds beside the default branch and what the claim's plan was, so the next worker can carry the work on or start over. A release counts only once both are done (below). A run that fails between the two puts back the labels it took off, so the issue is as it was and the next run starts the release over, unless the comment is there after all, one GitHub wrote and lost its answer to, which makes the release whole; only when GitHub refuses the putting back too are the labels left off with no comment, until a run releases the claim or a person puts them back. It never pushes to the branch, deletes it or renames it. It takes its candidates from the open issues that have a branch `claude/<issue>`, whatever their labels, and skips a claim whose latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, which is a claim already released; sdlc_protocol.claims.release_due is that rule, so a coordinator that runs on every board run releases each claim once, and releases again one it released too soon or one claimed anew that went idle again. A person or a coordinator routine may release a claim too, in the other order: the comment first, then the labels, since taking a label off starts a board run, whose coordinator, finding no release comment yet, would release the claim with a comment of its own; or, for any claim but a draft its author said is finished, a person takes the labels off and leaves the comment to that run.\n\nThe board does the rest (sdlc_protocol.board.released_keys). A claim counts as released when its issue is open, labelled neither claimed nor in-review, its pull request, if any, is still a draft, and its latest release comment came more than 24 hours after the branch's last commit, with no commit since. That holds for a draft whose author said it is finished as for any other, though the coordinator never releases one: a person releases one by hand with the release comment first, then the labels, which frees it, the board reading its issue's comments once the label claimed is off. Taking its labels off alone leaves it held, with no labels, until a person posts the release comment or puts the labels back, since no coordinator's comment follows. A release comment counts when it is by the repository's owner, a member of its organization or a collaborator, by the repository's own workflows (the bot `github-actions[bot]`, which only a workflow someone who may write put there can write as), or by anyone else who may write to the repository. The board then leaves the claim and its pull request off the page and lists the issue in the Queue again, and renames the branch to `released/<issue>-<short sha of its head>`: the name `claude/<issue>` is free, the work stays under the new name, GitHub closes the branch's pull request with the rename, and the board says on the issue where the branch went. The next worker claims the issue by the steps above, a new branch `claude/<issue>` from the default branch, and may carry the released work on in it after the claim commit, keeping its own claim file. Since the coordinator releases in the board's own run, before the board is rendered, the same run renames the branch. A release comment made sooner, or by anyone else, frees nothing; a commit pushed to the branch before the rename, its holder coming back, keeps the claim; and putting the label claimed back undoes a release, before the rename or after it, when the branch is renamed back and its pull request reopened. A branch pushed again at the head a release already kept is deleted instead, since its work is already kept. Until the board has renamed the branch, a push of the name is still refused; the board renames it in the run that releases the claim, or, for a release made by hand, in the run that the label's removal starts. When GitHub refuses the rename for good, for a ruleset whose target takes in the claim branches or a token without the right, the board says so once on the issue, naming each ruleset that applies to the two names with its rules (sdlc_protocol.board.rename_refused_text), and every run tries the rename again; a ruleset meant for the default branch leaves `claude/**` and `released/**` out of its target.\n\n## Overlaps\n\nTwo pieces of open work that change the same file meet when the second lands. The board's Overlaps section lists each file that more than one piece of open work changes, an open pull request by its files or, while it changes nothing but its claim file, by the files its claim lists, and a claim with no pull request yet by the files its claim lists, with that work in the order it should land: a blocker first, since it stops everyone; then work marked ready, which waits only for its merge; then the oldest (sdlc_protocol.board.overlaps). In the same run the coordinator tells each piece of open work about the work it meets, once, in a comment whose first line is `<!-- sdlc-coordinator: overlap <numbers> -->` naming every piece it told of, with the shared files and the order, and tells it again only when work it has not been told of starts to meet it (sdlc_protocol.coordinator.overlap_comments_due); a marker counts only from an author whose release comment would count (Release), so no one else can keep work from being told. Each piece after the first brings the default branch in once the one before it has landed.\n\n## Planning\n\nAn issue labelled epic is a request for more than one piece of work. The coordinator, here Claude, in the repository's coordinator workflow or a coordinator routine, plans it into tasks, each with the files it owns and the interfaces it shares with the others, and gives each task to a registered worker whose area fits, labelling it `worker:<name>`; where no worker is registered the tasks go to the open queue. When a worker labels its task blocked, the coordinator settles what it needs; when the last task closes, the coordinator integrates: it checks the default branch as a whole and fixes what the pieces broke together, under this protocol. The clerical part, writing the plan into the epic, opening the tasks and telling each worker what to do next, is code, not Claude: sdlc_protocol.team, run as `python3 -m sdlc_protocol team`. Workers that ask at the same moment, as the workers in Actions of one run do, are dealt the open work so no two race for one claim: each takes the task it was given, else the next unclaimed issue whose listed files fit its area (the Task form's \"Files likely touched\", or a planned task's \"Files to touch\"; an issue that lists none fits every area).\n\n## Policy and worlds\n\nThe rules above are the same wherever they run. Two things change from one place to another, and sdlc_protocol.gate names them: a Policy, which says how the protocol behaves, and a World, which is where the branches live and which an adapter implements. The gate's decisions (claim, open a pull request, mark it ready, report its status, draw the board) are functions of the policy, the world and a queue of issues and pull requests, and they run no git and no HTTP themselves.\n\nThe policy's fields, with the values this document describes as the defaults:\n\n- claim_prefix, `claude/`: what a claim branch's name starts with, before the issue's number.\n- required_check, `check`: the check that must be green on the branch's head before the gate merges; empty where merging cleanly is the whole check.\n- bring_main_in, `rebase`: how a branch takes in a default branch that has moved. `rebase` is a session's way, above, with the one `--force-with-lease`; `merge` is a worker in Actions', merging the default branch in so nobody force-pushes. The \"main moved\" answer says the steps in the policy's own way (sdlc_protocol.gate.main_moved_instruction).\n- who_merges, `owner`: who moves the default branch. `owner` is the repository's owner by hand, as above; `gate` is the gate itself, when a pull request is marked ready; `auto-merge` is GitHub, once the required check is green, as a worker whose file says so lands.\n- idle_after, 24 hours: how long a claim may go without a commit before it is released.\n\nTwo presets name the two ways of landing above: ACCOUNT_POLICY (rebase, the owner merges) and TEAM_POLICY (merge, auto-merge). A policy also carries remote and default_branch, `origin` and `main` unless the place calls them otherwise, which are only the names its answers use, so an instruction reads `origin/main` or `team/main` as the place does.\n\nA world answers six questions: the default branch's commit (main_sha), a branch's commit (branch_sha), how a branch stands to the default branch (standing: clean, behind, conflicting or unknown), the state of the required check on its head (check: green, red, pending or none), the merge itself (merge, which returns the default branch's new commit or raises why not: MainMoved, the default branch left as it was; MergeRefused, for a reason the branch's author fixes; GateFailed, the world could not act), and the time (now). What each adapter supplies:\n\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.github, a repository on GitHub through the REST API, with a workflow's GITHUB_TOKEN or an App installation token: the standing from the pull request's mergeable_state, told apart further by mergeable and by how far the head is behind its base where GitHub says only that something blocks the merge, and unknown for a branch with no open pull request; the check from the check runs on the branch's head that carry the required check's name; the merge through GitHub's merge API, pinned to the head the gate looked at, and only when who_merges is `gate`, any other policy being refused with NotAllowed; the clock as given, or the wall clock.\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.bare_git, a bare repository that agents share, as coordbench's sdlc arm does: the standing from git itself, in a scratch clone, with the conflicting files named and two more words a git world can tell apart (no branch, nothing new); no checks, so the check is always none; the merge as `git merge --no-ff` under the gate's own committer identity, pushed without force, a conflict or a refused push being MainMoved; the wall clock.\n- sdlc_protocol.adapters.memory, a world held in memory for tests and simulations: standings and checks set by hand, a clock that moves only when told, so a simulation can hold a check pending for as long as it means CI to take, and a record of every merge.\n\nA refusal carries the code its command exits with, so a script can tell one kind from another: 1 refused (claim first, push, commit something), 2 someone else's (another holds the claim, the issue or the pull request), 3 main moved (bring the default branch in and try again), 4 gate failed (the world could not act).\n\n## Rules that hold everywhere\n\n- One issue, one branch `claude/<issue>`, one pull request.\n- Never push to another worker's branch. Never force-push the default branch or any branch that is not your own claim. The push guard (sdlc_protocol.guard, run as a Claude Code hook through `python3 -m sdlc_protocol guard`) refuses those pushes before they run, and branch protection on GitHub stands behind it.\n- Memory rides in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n- The repository's checks pass before a pull request is marked ready.\n- CI holds a `claude/<issue>` pull request to this protocol (sdlc_protocol.protocol_check, run as `python3 -m sdlc_protocol check`, part of the required check): the claim file is there while the pull request is a draft, gone once it is ready, the body closes its issue, and the branch lives in the repository itself, never in a fork; and a pull request from a branch that reads as a claim and claims nothing, as `claude/03`, fails it.\n\nContents of /home/user/spine-program/docs/memory/MEMORY.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Shared memory index\n\nEach project folder keeps its own MEMORY.md and its own memory/ (one fact per file); a fact about one project lives\nthere, not here. This file indexes only the facts that hold for the repository as a whole. The 13 project indexes:\n\n- [01_carm_level_detection](../../01_carm_level_detection/MEMORY.md)\n- [02_transitional_naming_truth](../../02_transitional_naming_truth/MEMORY.md)\n- [03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset](../../03_ctspinopelvic1k_dataset/MEMORY.md)\n- [04_openspinetoolkit](../../04_openspinetoolkit/MEMORY.md)\n- [05_ct_morphometry_parity](../../05_ct_morphometry_parity/MEMORY.md)\n- [06_transitional_ct_census](../../06_transitional_ct_census/MEMORY.md)\n- [07_plain_film_level_id](../../07_plain_film_level_id/MEMORY.md)\n- [08_render_devices](../../08_render_devices/MEMORY.md)\n- [09_wrong_level_clinical_qi](../../09_wrong_level_clinical_qi/MEMORY.md)\n- [10_data_platform_grid](../../10_data_platform_grid/MEMORY.md)\n- [11_students_grid_access](../../11_students_grid_access/MEMORY.md)\n- [12_website_pacs_demo](../../12_website_pacs_demo/MEMORY.md)\n- [99_archive](../../99_archive/MEMORY.md)\n\n## Cross-project facts\n\nOne line per fact, `- [title](file-name.md): one line`, in alphabetical order of file name, added with the remember\nskill (.claude/skills/remember/SKILL.md) in the same pull request as the change it came from.\n\n- [The claim protocol updates itself](the-claim-protocol-updates-itself.md): tools/sdlc_protocol is symphony-protocol at the release tools/VENDORED records, and .github/workflows/protocol.yml brings each later release in as a pull request from the branch symphony-protocol/sync; how to vendor by hand, and what of the template's worker machinery is not here.\n- [The record material is on the grid](the-record-material-is-on-the-grid.md): each folder's results/, figures/, manuscripts/ and regulatory/ are symlinks to /rs/rs_grp_oschome/shared/spine-program/archive/ since 2026-10-13, the files stay in git history at main 5597197, and ARCHIVE.md says how to read or bring one back.\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"user","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","sessionId":"cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"zany-kindling-liskov"}
